      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <p>HOME MADE SAUSAGES.</p>
        <p>MOLYNDUX'S HOME-MADE HIGH CLASS</p>
        <p>PORK SAUSAGES. Made from Pork Slaughtered on own Premises.</p>
        <p>ALSO MOLYNEUX'S BEEF &amp; PORK SAUSAGES.</p>
        <p>These will be found to agree with those that find all Pork too rich.</p>
        <p>-- Everyone should try these EXCELLENT SAUSAGES (both kinds), to ascertain which are most suitable for them.</p>
        <p>ALSO HOME-MADE PORK PIES AND BRAWN</p>
        <p>CHINA TEAS.</p>
        <p>Tea a la Sir Andrew Clarke. For years, whenever my Customers have asked me for a Mild Tea, I have advised the Pure. old Fashioned China which I ceep. and the advice has been appreciated. It is very satisfactory to them that such an eminent authority as Sir Andrew Clarke, the London Physician, corroborates</p>
        <p>opinion. 4 Here is an extract from an interesting lecture, delivered by Sir Andrew the other day, to the Students of the London Hospital: "Tea is a blessed beverage. Ido not know what I should do without it. But there is tea and tea, an one of the teas I have in my mind is the representation of all that is physiologically wicked. Igo about town a good deal holding consulta- tions here and there, and about five o'clock, when 1 get into a place, the lady of the hous6 will say to me : 'Sir Andrew, you look tired, do let me give you a cup ot tea.' I say 'thank you very much. But the tea has stood for half-an-hour, and she remarks, 'I know you do you not like it strong, Sir Andrew,' and she them pours about a tablespoonful of the tea into a cup and fills thecuP up with water. Now I call it positiveeruelty to give tea like that to anybody and I hope you, gentlemen, will always set your face against it. Tea, to be useful, should be first of all Black China Tea ; the Indian Tea which is being cultivated has become so powertul in its effects upon the nery sus system that a cup of it taken early in the morning, as many do, so disorders the nervous system that those who take it actually get into a state of tea intoxication, and it produces a form of nervous dis;,urbance, which is most painful to witness. If you want to have, either for yourselves or for your patients, tea which will not injure and which will refresh, get iilack China Tea, putting in the right measure—the old fashioned teaspoonful for each person and one for the blessed pot. Then pour on briskly boiling water, and within five minutes you must pour it off again. or it will become wicked instead of good." Such is Sir Andrew Clarke's opinion and advise. A few words of warning, however, are necessary to those who have for- gotten what this old fashioned tea is like. The colour of the tea after it is brewed will be found tobe much paler than that now in use, and the taste may not be acceptable at first owing to its apparent insipidness ; but if its use is persisted in, it will very soon be found to be much more suitable for the system, anel if, in a week to two after its use, a trial is again made of the kind now in ordinary use, you will wonder however you could have been using it,and you will endorse Sir Andrew's statement. This old fashioned China Tea, recommended by Sir Andrew Clarke, can be obtained now, as always or the last 35 years, at</p>
        <p>0111 MAS rul A.IOI,ICNEUX HOUSEHOLD STORES, ' 37, 39, &amp; 41, MARKET-STREET, EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>e4l!</p>
      </div>
