      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>" CAPITAL " CHAT.</head>
        <p>" CAPITAL " CHAT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Arthur Severn has just finished an anecdotal and biographical work on Mr. Ruskin, which promises to be of some interest. Mr. Severn is the son of Keats's Severn, and has been acquainted with Mr. Ruskin since a boy. He has from time to time been out with him on long driving tours, and one of the features of the book will be illustrations from sketches made by the author during these outings. The work will be published by Mr. Ruskin' s own publisher.</p>
        <p>The Queen during her sojourn in Italy will prob- ably witness a private performance of Verdi's new opera" Falstaff.' A Milanese musical paper says the manager of the Scala Opera House, haying heard of the recent operatic performances at Wind- sor and Balmoral, intends to approach her Majesty in the subject shortly after her arrival, and if he obtains the royal command the work will be given in the local theatre with the same artists who "created "the parts,</p>
        <p>Colonel Hutton,the new Queen's aide-de-camp,owes Ins reputation almost entirely to Africa. He "made" ene of the first mounted infantry corps that ever existed,and first brought the heliograph into practi- cal use at the relief of Ekowe. These feats brought him into notice, and he managed to monopolise a good deal of attention during his subsequent ser- vice under Sir Archibald Alison iu Egypt. He has been doing less brilliant, but quite as useful, work at Aldershot since then. He is only forty-four, and is reputed likely to "go far."</p>
        <p>Mr. Newnes and his coadjutors have now decided upon a name for the new evening Liberal organ, which is to appear here, as it is hoped, simultane- ously with the opening of Parliament at the end of this month. A visit to Stationers' Hall shows that three titles have been registered, one being for the evening paper and the others for the weekly organ. The name registered for tlse former is "The West- minster Gazette." The alternate titles for the weekly illustrated edition are "The Westminster Budget" and "The Westminster Weekly."</p>
        <p>The Rev. R. Montagu Butler, who has just pub- lished a pamphlet entitled " Rome's Tribute to Anglican Orders," a book calculated to give satis- faction to pious Anglicans, Wit 3 some years ago the popular curate at All Saints', Gordon-sq., London. Subsequently he went as a missionary to Calcutta, where he made a popular chaplain to the E.1.R.V.R., and was assistant editor to an Indian Church paper. Mr. Butler is at present on leave at Bristol. lie has written several works of a similar kind, and is making a mark among High Church controvsrsial- ists. He is still well under forty.</p>
        <p>The coming banquet of the Birmingham Press Club will not, be the first occasion to tind Mr. Jos. , Chamberlain speaking exclusively to an audience of newspaper men. Five years ago, when Mr. Chamberlain was making his tour among the High- land Crofters, he entertained " the reporters " one evening to a little banquet at Stornoway. lie stipulated beforehand that there should be nis re- port of the event, and under cover of this delivered a most interesting speech on journalism and public life. Perhaps that is the only speech ot Mr. Chain- berlaiii's since he be,ame, a big man of which there iv positively no record.</p>
        <p>--- The Duke and Duchess of Teck, with Princess May. a few days since visited St. Paul's Cathedral, and afterwards were entertained to tea by Canon Scott-Holland, at 1 Amen-corner, Paternoster-row. The royal party attended the afternoon service, and strolling through the cathedral, were unnoticed by ordinary folk. It is not generally known that Canon Scatt-Holland's now fain Nus labour sermon was prepared at Wimbledon residence of the Hon. Mrs Saitt-llolland, the mother of the Canon. Tne Canon with his sister, alter spending the Christmas holidays with their mother, returned to Amen-cor- ner en Saturday night.</p>
        <p>Mr. Frank White, the American newspaper man, who bad the newspaper luck to be on board the Umbria, was going home after a period of nearly : four years' service as London correspondent of the "New York Sun." Be made many friends in Lon- don, and shortly before his departure was enter- tained to a handsome banquet. This is not by any means the first time Mr. V; hite has provided "good copy '' for Charles A. Dana's journal. Hs once wrote an article which fairly set New York talking on ow I Spent Five Hundred Dollars in a Single Evening." Modesty is not always the charscteris- tic of your American journalist, but it is a very marked item in Mr. VI bite's engaging personality. Mr. White is tallish, fair, and always uncommonly dressed.</p>
        <p>Hugbenden Manor, into possession of which Mr. Coningsby Disraeli, M.P., is about to enter under the provision of his uncle's will early next year on attaining his twenty-sixth year, is frequently sup- posed to have been the residence of Edmund Burke at Bewtonstield. The estate purchased by Burke, which was named "The Gregorna," or " Butler's Court," is now in the possession of Sir Edward Law- son, the proprietor of the " Daily Telegraph." The house, has however, been almost entirely rebuilt, and only a few outoffices remain of the structure in Burke's time. Sir Edward Lawson, who purchased the property about twenty years ago from a family named Du Pre, has been indefatigable in collecting relics of Edmund Burke, and numbers amongst his curios the poniard which Burke threw on the floor of the Rouse of Commons in the famous dagger</p>
        <p>--- Mr. Lane, Q.C., who has been chosen to succeed the late Mr. Montagu Williams on the metropolitan bench of magistrates, is an Irishman and the eldest mi of a former rector of Killashee, in co. Kildare. At Trinity College, Dublin, he held a distinguished position among his contemporaries. He was a first prizeman and a medallist. and was orator or presi- dent of the Historical Society—a body similar to the " union "at Oxford. He was Itho a famous athlete. On being called at the Inner Temple in 1870 he joined the South Eastern Circuit, and pre- , sently acquired considerable reputation in the con- : duct of commercial cases. Ile had also an extensive genisaal practice, and it will be remembered that he was counsel for Mrs. Cathcart betore the Court of Appeal. Mr. Lane " took silk "in 1890, at the same time as the present Home Secretary. He has shown himself a sound lawyer, though possessed of no great oratorical gift, and is fifty years of age.</p>
        <p>--- There are two men in the House of Commons older than Mr. Gladstone—the Right Hon. Charles Pelham Villiers, who entered on Tuesday on his , ninety-second year, having been born on 2nd. of January, 1802, and .Nlr. Isaac Holden, the Radical member for the Keighley division of the West Rid- ing of Yorkshire, who was eighty-five his last birthday Mr. Villisrs is unquestionably the Father Of the House of Commons, both in point of years and in continuity of service. Mr. Gladstona, who is his junior by seven years, was a member of Par- liament in 1832, three years before Mr. Villiers entered the House of Com rnons„ but Mr. Villiers has at continuously in Parliament and for the aame constituency —NN olv e,rh a mpton—from the year 1835 till the present time, whereas Mr. Gladstone has been for an interval of two years, from 1845 till 1847. al' hough a Cabinet Minister, without a seat Ciladstone, moreover, suffered three defeats in Parliamentary conteete—at Newark in 1845, at Oxfotlt University in 1865, and in South Lancashire in 1868.</p>
        <p>Miss Cornelia Bradle - .'.lartin, the fiancée of the Earl of Craven, is no quite eighteen. The Earl met her at the Bradley-Martin shooting box in Scotland two years ago. Baltnacaan, with its fine preserves and splendid domestic appointments. has been a favourite rendezvous for titled Londoners. The father a the prospective Countess of Craven, was, in his early days, a struggling lawyer at Albany, New York state. Her mother was a Miss Sherman, the only daughter of an enormously wealthy but eccen- tric farmer, Isaac H. Sherman. The hyphen in the family name came after the American newspapers had boomed in high falutin terms the extravagent banquets which Mr,,3. Martin gave at her house at No. 20, West 20th Street, New York City. The Bradiey-Nitirtins now bif,ct the strictest European ideas of life, and i has long been an open secret among their American friends that Mrs. Bradley- .Martn would not be satisfied with a son-in-law below the rank of a peer. The eldest son of the family married a London music-hall artiste.</p>
        <p>West Southwark is going to have an interesting and curious Tory can idate. Ills name is Mr. Fred Horner, and he is better known on the theatrical than on the political stage. Ile has sent a Christ- Ina . card to the electors in the division intimating his inttntion to come out in opposition to Mr. It K. Causton. Be gives as some of his qualifications that he has written plays called "The Late Lamented," "The Bungalow," and so on. His pro-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <p>gram, so far as it goes, is a mixture of trimming and Ignorance. I.le announces that he "will visit every nook and corner of the constituency," with " the valuable assistance of Mrs. Horner." That's a vigor- ous candidate for you. But Mr. Horner should take warning by the fate of an abler and more popular theatrical man who stood in a neighbour- ing constituency at the Caunv Council election. West z•outhwark is an advanced constituency, but not in the same sense as is a fin de siècle play writer and newspaper proprietor. Mr. Horner may amuse himself by going into "every nook and corner" of the constituency "with the valuable assistance of Mn. Horner" if he likes, but he will find himself cornered when the election comes round.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>QII</head>
        <p>QII</p>
        <p>EE</p>
        <p>Egg examiners earn a living by frequenting the markets to pronounce .an opinion, based, of course, upon outward inspection only, whether the eggs offered for sale are fresh or not. The calling is sufficiently important to require its own trade union ; and the egg examintr rarely makes a mis- take. One may often see in French wine shops, more particularly at Eastertide, numbers ot eggs stained red. About a hundred egg dyers are em- ployed in this industry, and they deal with the eggs, two or three hundred in a batch, by placing them in hot red dye contained in coppels. The pro- duction of ants' eggs for pheasantries, aquariums, and large bird shops is carried on on such a scale that one " farmer " has received a dozen sacks of ants a day, imported from Germany and other countries. She—for the farmer is a lady—wears leatler gauntlets and pantaloons, but she is hide- ously disfigured by the bites of her stock-in-trade, which she feeds well, and keeps warm, encouraging them to lay eggs in huge quantities. A somewhat similar industry, maintaining, by the way, its special trade union, is that of the maggot breeders —a rather exclusive class—who undertake to supply the French anglers, fishing in the Seine, with live bait. They also rear worms, of which many mil- lions are sold yearly, at a franc to two francs per pound. The same men breed meal worms for birds, and sell them to the shops at 40 centimes per hun- dred. Another guild is formed of the toad mer- chants, who dispose of their stack, which they catch and breed from, to gardeners and florists, for toads are useful in keeping down slugs and other pests. Ratcatchers ply their trade, not in the sewers, but by haunting the streets at bight. Dogs drive the vermin into traps. The skins are used for gloves, and one may not be sure, in a thrifty and not over-squeamish city, that the flesh is thrown away. An allied calling to that of the ratcatcher is the cat killer, who chases a stray puss with ter- riers, and finds a market for its fur coat at the fur- riers', and for its flesh at some of the minor restau- rants', who dish it up as "rabbit" with a real bunny's head. Food of a kind which the siege of Paris reduced to a necessary article of diet mar comprise not only cats, rats, and horseflesh—for which a distinct demand exists—but also snails. More snails are eaten in Paris than frogs—the latter being collected along the banks of the Seine and sold, with the head and front legs cut off, on skewers. The snails come from Bordeaux. or the market gardens around the capital. You can buy them raw in a wine shop at 20 centimes to a franc per dozen. They are usually eaten baked, with parsley and bread crumbs, and are nicer than the English whelk.—From "Cassell's Saturday Journal.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>CELLARS OF THE BANK OF ENGLAND.</head>
        <p>THE CELLARS OF THE BANK OF ENGLAND.</p>
        <p>A writer, in describing a visit paid to the cellars of the Bank of England, Threadneedle-street, Lon- don, says:—" In the printing department sheets of postal orders are being worked off like so many coloured posters. This is a new department in the Bank. The old-fashioned money orders are printed elsewhere, along with the various kinds of station- ery used by the Post-office. Hard by there is a larger printing room, where six or eight smallish presses, such as might be found in an ordinary jobbing office, are at wcrk. I am invited to ex- amine the work which is being turned out. These are bank notes which are being thrown out by the thousand. In this dirty printing room, reeking of oil and ink, they turn out per day 50,000 bank notes of various denominations, and this output goes on continually. As the notes are paid into ,he bank from the outside they are seized and put away for destruction, and nes ones put in their place, the turnover being about a million pounds daily. One of the sights to which the visitor is conducted is the stock of paid notes, which are kept for five years before being destroyed. At the present moment this old stock numbers nearly 78,000,000 notes, which had a value of nearly two billions. They are packed in rough wooden boxes, and weigh over 90 tons. They are now merely so much waste paper, though it would be risky to sell them as such. There is a regular holocaust made of them once a fortnight, when they are burnt by the million in a furnace specially constructed for the purpose. For convenience of handling the gold it is cast into blocks of 281 b. each worth 1,600 sover- eigns. A block is handed you to feel by aniofficial, who advises you to take it in both hands on account of its weight. It is piled on trucks, each of which carries a load of tens of thousands of pounds. Other trvcks are leaded with little bags of sovereigns, representing in the aggregate— millions. The bullion vaults have nothing of the jeweller's shop about them. I do not recollect whether they are whitewashed, but they give one the impression of being so. Perhaps the most in- teresting department of all is that where bank notes are deposited are exhibited which have been paid after being for menths at the bottom of the sea or grilled to a cinder in a conflagration. There are notes here recovered from the wreck of the Eury- dice, which went clown off the Isle of Wight, and from that other wreck known as the Thirsk railway disaster. The grilled notes mostly have been got out of safes in burnt-down establishments. One or two of them are perfect in shape, although com- pletely carbonised, and on their blackened surface may still be read their number and denomination. Scraps of notes, too, are on view, all of which have been paid into the bank in the way of business. It is quite enough for practical purposes that the number of a note should be legible, and, indeed, in some cases very little more than this magical figure has beeu preserved. All the notes are pro- duced by surface printing from the electrotype. The great difficulty of the forger appears to be the imitation of the paper, and its watermark. Some of the forgeries are astonishingly clumsy. On one of .tlie sham notes there is a promise to pay fifty pence instead of fifty pounds, the forger hoping pro- bably that this device, while likely enough to de- ceive the eye would not. bring him within the reach of the law. Very few forgeries escape de- tection by the cashiers at the paying-in counter. Not long ago there was an() note stolen of which payment was stopped. In order to baffle the lynx- ey./1 officials the thief cut out a figure in the num- her and substituted for it another cut from a £5 note, the patching being successfully accom- plished by the application of hydraulic pressure. The bank records, extending over 200 years embrace many interesting autographs. Not all the eminent Englishmen of the past two years have held stock, I need not say, but a good many have done bO. Here one reads the names of Nelson and Welling- ton. Among the signatures in the Golden Book is that of Fauntleroy, the famous forger, who cheated the bank out of some £360,000.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>TOWN COUNCILS AND "FAIR WAGES."</head>
        <p>TOWN COUNCILS AND "FAIR WAGES."</p>
        <p>The "Lobbyist" of the "Financial Reformer "gives the following as a fairly complete and never vet pub- lished list of the Town Councils which have adopted a "Fair Wages" resolution:— • Accrington Liverpool Ashton-under-Lyne Leicester Barrow London Blackpool Manchester Bolton Northampton Brighton Norwich Bury Nottingham Birmingham Oldham Bristol Plymouth Cardiff Rotherham Derby Dublin shefeeld Scarborough Edinburgh Stockton Gloucester Sligo Grimsby Swansea Halifax Stafford Hull Sunderland Hartlepool Southport Ipswich West Ham - -- •</p>
        <p>This still leaves over a hundred Town Councils where the example has not been followed. No County Council (other than that of a county borough) has yet copied London. On the other hand, various School Boards, such as those of Plymouth, Scarborough, Jarrow, and Rotherham, have adopted the clause.</p>
        <p>CADBURY'S COCO/ is absolutetv pure.—The Public Ana- lysts have submitted CADBURY'S COCOA to cdose tests, and the result has always proved its great value as a sustain- ing and nourishing beverage.--Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>it CALLINGS IN PARIS.</head>
        <p>it CALLINGS IN PARIS.</p>
        <p>tr\ -.417\1\44 THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 7, 1893</p>
        <p>\A, Nkriffer;F---r-`1..."N WI ; .</p>
        <p>%</p>
        <p>nore a ligkrted lamp, and was on her way to answer the summons. The squire waved her back, but she did not obey. The aspect of his face, his wild excitement, his blazing eyes, held her spell-bound. Again that knocking sounded loud, irregular, spasmodic, as though the hand that produced it was frenzied. • - • The squire tore aside the bolts and pulled open tho door. A great gust of wind came in, .bringing with it rain and sleet, and in the midst of •it as if she had been the spirit of the storm, the wanderer whom he had seen tottered in, all wet and drenched, her clothes dripping, her arms upraised in suppli- cation. Her face was hidden by her long hair, which streamed about her like a wet veil. fier heal was bowed. Her form was clad in a draggled silk gown and a shawl that, despite rough usages, was still of great value. Her bonnet was gone, and an old veil alone shielded her from the storm. "Help, help,' she said piteously, in a voice that pierced Mrs. Quillet's soul. • But the old squire's face flamed with anger and sudden hatred. The door had slammed shut. • He opened it wide and pointed out in the storm. "There is no help for you here," he said, harshly. Go ! Go into the storm—and die I" The woman moaned. The housekeeper, thrilling with indignation, drew nearer, crying out— "Oh,, master, you never sent anyone away before like this! The storm is fearful. And she is a woman, and ill--" • "Hush !" interrupted Squire Markham, grimly. "There is no room for her here. Are you going, woman ?" He moved towards the wanderer menacingly. The stranger retreated with a cry of terror, and flung back her veil of dank, dark hair, and dis- closed her face. It was a young face—a girl's face—white and woful, with all the youth and light and tenderness stricken out of it. The lips were pale and quiver- ing; the cheeks thin and hollow; the eyes wild and lustrous, with the strange, quick glow of 'madness in them. It was evident at a glance that she was insane. Perhaps the storm and exposure of this very night had wrecked her reason. Perhaps she was in the early delirium of fever, Whatever the cause, and however long her malady had endured, she was certainly mad now. The squire recoiled from her in utter horror, livid to the lips. The housekeeper stood aghast. She was incredu- lous, frightened, stupefied. • It was not because she was insane, and that they feared her, that the squire and his old servant thus shrunk from her. it was because they recog- nised her. The housekeeper struggled with her great agitation' and cried out shrilly— "lt is Miss Clare ! It is our own Miss Clare 1". The squire uttered no denial, and said no word of remonstrance against the strange supposition. " Oh, master," cried Mrs. Quillet, hysterically, "you told me she was dead." She is dead," said the squire, in a strange, hard voice. "My daughter is dead." He continued to point to the door, but now his forefinger trembled with palsy. • "She is not dead," declared the bewildered old housekeeper; "not dead! Here she stands, master, alive—our own Miss Clare, You mean to send her out again in the storm—our own daughter, sir—our oen sweet young lady! What does it all mean? This is no dream. It is our dear Miss Clare. She stopped, as the young stranger looked frors her to the squire, with curious, vacant eyes, which knew not either of them. "Help !" said the poor young creature, wearily, her voice quivering, her sad eyes full of pleading. "I am going home! I want to go home! Help 1" The sqeire turned his face from her. The wanderer hesitated, her wan features quivered, and she made a quick spring forward is the direction of the haughty old squire, and sank helpless and unconscious at his feet..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>WESLEYAN LOCAL PREACtIERS'</head>
        <p>WESLEYAN LOCAL PREACtIERS'</p>
        <p>MONTHLY MEETING.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of this association, held on Tues- day evening, in the schoolroom of Bold-street Chapel, Mr. H. Butler gave an address on" Budd- hism and Christianity, some points of contrast." The speaker dealt in a very interesting manner with Buddha and his teachings, and showed that his gospel was, so to speak, one of sweet despair ; but that, on the other hand, Jesus Christ and Christianity was the life and hope and immortality of the world, and the remedy for the woes and wants of mankind. Messrs. Collier, George, Ford, W. Ashton, and the Chairman took part in the dis- cussion, and on the motion of Mr. Storey, the speaker was thanked for his instructive address. This being Mr. Butler's last public appoarance as a J preacher among his brethren previous to his de- parture for America, opportunity was taken to present him with the following resolution :—" The local preachers of the Warrington Wesleyan Circuit desire to convey to Mr. W. H. Butler their warmest wishes for his future prosperity. They deeply appreciate his ability and valuable services as a preacher of the gospel, as a tem- perance advocate, and as a Christian controversialist, and they have been interested in his efforts to upholi the doctrines of the Bible against the arrogant scepti- cism of the day. They recognise his intellectual capacity, and his willingness exer to advance the cause of truth and righteousness, and while re- gretting his removal from Warrington, trust that he may continue 'to walk by the same rule and mind the same things,' and that both he and his wife may find under the direction of Providence a sphere oh even more congenial and more extended usefulness, &amp;c."—Mr. E. Ford, in proposing the resolution, spoke in eulogistic terms of Mr. Butler as a man of superior mental and moral qualities, which he had devoted in various ways for the benefit of the Warrington circuit and the town at large. He had gained a position as a debater, and could hold his ground with opponents logically and with ability. As a preacher he did not run so much in the beaten track, but sought to present truth from its newest stand-points, and though at first he was not easily understood, yet as time wore on they begAn to find out his worth and appreciate him, and were all regret- ting his leaving them.—Mr. W. Collier seconded the pro- position, and felt that in losing Mr. Butler the local preachers were losing an able man.—Mr. Roscoller said that Mr. Butler always made people think, and such preachers were not as common as they might be.—Mr. Walter Ashton and Mr. W. E. Ashton said they, were indebted to Mr. Butler individually, and they owed him personally more than any other man.— Mr. Clement Geerge shared in the great loss that would be felt by Mr. Butler's departure, for he had received much benefit from his instructive teach- ing.—Messrs. Story, Sandford, and Isaac Johnson added confirmatory testimony.—The Chairman (Mr. Moss) said he always held their friend in the highest esteem, and had taken a friendly interest in him from the begin- ning. Be was a man of high moral tone, and possessed gifts of rare value, and he wished him well. Their own church would be the poorer, but the kingdom of Christ would be the richer. The resolution was heartily passed. —Mr. Butler, in reply, treated his brethren to a bit of his autobiographical history, and showed how he had been in the shadows of doubt, but now had emerged in- to the full and clear light of the gospel of Christ. He had been misunderstood he knew ; indeed, some people did not understand him yet, and he lid not care much for a man who could be seen through at a glance. He was very much obliged to his brethren, and he hoped they would hear of him again, for he was going to Boston solely with the object of entering the Methodist Episcopal Church, and with a greater and clearer faith in God and in Jesus Christ than ever he had before. He hoped to become a minister of that Church. and be the means according to his power of doing good in the world.</p>
        <p>locai</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>Y.M.C.A. AND MR. HOItDEN.</head>
        <p>THE Y.M.C.A. AND MR. HOItDEN.</p>
        <p>INTERESTING PRESENTATIONS. On Saturday the committee of the Warrington Y.M.C.A. invited Mr. Horden and his son (Mr. Altert E. Horden), who are leaving town for Brighton, to take tea with them at Friars'-green House. At six o'clock, the time appointed, the committee-room was found to have been converted into what was designated as" quite a banquet. hall," the room and tables having been decorated with considerable taste by Mesdames Wyllie, Stringer, Yeadon, and Miss S. Stringer. Ample justice having been done to the good things provided, Mr. WYLLII, chairman of the committee, called upon the Secretary (Mr. W. H. Bayley) to read the following letter :—" Grappenhall Heyes, Warrington, December 29, 1892. Dear Sir,—Your letter has duly reached me to-day. I ought to have told you that I had returned home. I heartily concur in the decision of your commit- tee that the presentation to our good friend, Mr. Borden, should take the form of an album, and I feel much pleasure and satisfaction in sending you a photo that may recall me in days to come to the memory of One whom we shall always regard as the best and most valued friend of our young association. I sin also much pleased that the efficient services of our financial secre- tary, Mr. A. E. Horden, are to be recognized at the same tiens. I cannot close without a deep regret that we are about to lose such valuable services and such good friends. Wishing them every happiness in their new sphere, I remain, yours faithfully, J. CHARLTON PARR. W. El. Bayley, Esq." The CHAIRMAN said they had assembled that evening with mingled feelings—feelings Of regret and pleasure— deep regret that they were about to lose One who might truly be said to be one of the fathers of the association they had the honour to represent on that Occasion, and pleasure, remembering that their dear friend was being removed to such a salubrious locality. Alter dwelling upon the many good qualities of Mr. Harden, and the invaluable services rendered to the Y.M.C.A. by him, he said it gave him jay, and he felt it to be an honour to be allowed, on behalf of the committee, to hand to him a small memento of their e4eem and goodwill. He then presented Mr. Harden with a beautiful " As you like it " tinted album, bound in Russian leather, and containing cabinet photographs of the president, trustees, and members of the committee, and an illumi- nated address, chastely executed by Mr. 3rooko, as follows Presented to Henry Harden, Esq., on hit removal to Brighton, by the committee of the Warring- ton Y.M.C.A., as a token of esteem and goodwill for the valuable services rendered to this association from its Inception as trustee and member of the commiitee.— Signed, on behalf of commttee, J. Charlton Parr, presi- deut ; W. Robert Wyllie, chairman ; W. H. Bayley, secre- tary ; S. B. Stringer, treasurer." Mr. Jona FAIRCLOUGH said he could not allow the occasion to pass without a word. First he was requested to deliver a message from Miss Dutton expressing her regret at not being able to be present,. but her hearty accord with the presentation to and good wishes for Mr. I:harden and Mr. A. E. Horden. For himself he heartily endorsed all that had been said re- specting their friend ; in fact the half had not been told. He deeply felt the loss the association would sustain in the removal of one who had endeared himself to everyone with whom he had come into o intact. Dr. T. STARER!' SMITH said he was there to pay a tribute of respect to one for whom his esteem and ad- miration had grown from their first acquaintance, While they regretted the removal of their friend, they could not but rejoice with him that he was about to exchange the murky atmosphere of Warrington for the beautiful and life-giving air of Brighton. (Applause.) Messrs. GARLAND, Kerry, S. B. Stringer, W. 11. Bay- ley, Horton, and others, bore their testimony to Mr.Horden's good qualities,and expressed admiration and lave for their friend and colleigue, after which Mr. Horden thanked them for their beautiful present, and said they could not possibly have been more fortunate in their choice of the presentation. It was one above all others he would have chosen had he selected it for himself. Until that moment he had not the slightest idea that he was coming for anything except a friendly cup of tea. He could not express the feelings of his - heart. He was sure they had been extravagent in their kind words to which they had given utterance. He had only been one ;he had done his best, but there were others. There was their excellent friend the president. In Mr. J. Pairclough they had a tower of strength ;in the secretary they had a valuable friend, and he assurel them it was not alight matter to leave so manyto whom he had a growing attachment. The presentation was one from which he should derive pleasure and joy as from time to time he glanced through at the faces re- presented. Mr. W. H. BATLEY was then celled upon to present Mr. Albert E. Horden, who has been an ideal financial ' secretary, with a beautiful copy of Tennysm's Poems, bound in Russian leather, containing an illuminated address executed by Mr. D. S. Stone with good taste— "Presented to Mr. Albert E. Harden, on his removal to Brighton,by the committee of the Warrington Y.M.C.A., as a token of esteem for the valuable services rendered to this association as financial secretary. Signed on be- half of the committee," as in the former case. BATLEY. BUTLYIE, H. DUTTON, CROWDER, J. T. PAT-rigor', and CRITCHLRY testified to the many good qualities of the secretary, and to the manner in which he had endeared himself to everyone with whom he had come into contact by his courteous and genial deport- ment. Mr. A. E. Flonnsx, who was evidently overcome by expressed of h s d sedt h e pleasureduties. H His services ehadri caels- wthaeyTfenlyt kind ntdhescdoisdsch,a had been a labour of love. A vote of thanks to the Chairman and to the ladies for their services brought a very pleasant ceremony to a close.</p>
        <p>LINEN LASTS LONGER, Radiant in its whiteness! Delightful in its aw,etness I And will keep much longer clean when soaked and washed in foaming lather ot HUDSON'S DRY SOAP. Hudson's preserves the most delicate lawns. laces. muslin, and fine underwear I Om injury, and it will not fray cuffs or jag collars. acke • you'll never lack it." Advt.</p>
