      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>KILLED BY A SNOWBALL.</head>
        <p>KILLED BY A SNOWBALL.</p>
        <p>On Thursday morning at ten o'clock a young woman, aged 29, named Jane Aspinall, of Abbey-st., Leigh, died under extraordinary circumstances. Four weeks last Sunday night she was near the Leigh Parish Church when a boy threw a snowball containing, it is believed, a stone, and hit her full in the eye. The eye has ever since been very bad, and she never fully rallied, and died as stated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>GENERAL BOOTH AND THE" DARKEST ENGLAND" FUND. •</head>
        <p>GENERAL BOOTH AND THE" DARKEST ENGLAND" FUND. •</p>
        <p>HE DOES NOT INTEND TO HAVE TRUSTEES:</p>
        <p>Speaking at the Northwich Salvation Army Citadel on the recent report of the committee of investigation, General Booth said that while his presence there was not intimately connected with the social scheme, yet his silence on that subject might be misconstrued, although he had come to the conclusion that whether he opened his mouth or kept it closed, there were some people ever ready to misunderstand him. The committee of inves- tigation had reported in favour of the Salvation Army in a most positive way ; but one of the suggestions con- tained in the report he would not be able to adopt, as he considered himself quite equal to spending all the money the country liked to trust him with. It was not his in- tention to have trustees. He was not going to have any masters outside the Salvation Army. The committee had reported that he done exactly as he promised, but added that the establishment of the colony over the sea should be proceeded with. If with that suggestion they bad sent a cheque, it would have cleared the way to some extent. He was ready to start that por- tion of the scheme as soon as the money was forth- coming.</p>
        <p>THE NEW WATCH FACTORY AT PRESCOT. A PROMISING INDUSTRY. Attention is directed to an important departure in the watch-making indusdry which is about to be inaugu- rated at Prescot, for many years one of the principal centres of the watch movement manufacture. It will be remembered that some time ago the business had fallen so much into decay that fears were entertained of its disappearance from the town altogether, and to pre- vent such a calamity it was resolved to promote a company, arid to erect a factory having all the most recent appliances for turning out complete watches of , the highest class of workmanship. Accordingly the 1 Lancashire Watch Company, Limited, was formed, and the building is now completed and fitted, and in full working order. The formal inaugurating of the business takes place on Wednesday, Thursday, and Friday next week. On the first day the Earl of Derby, who will preside, will be presented with the :first watch made in the factory, and will deliver an address. The Rev. H. vicar of Prescot, one of the prime movers of the scheme, will receive an address, and Mrs. Mitchell will be presented with a watch. In the evening the directors will hold an "At home." On Thursday week, the second day, Lord Arthur Cecil will preside at a dinner to the employes, and amongst those taking part in the proceedings to follow will be Lord Kelvin, the Earl of Camperdown, the Right Hon. A. B. Forwood, M.P., and Mr. T. Snape, M.P. On Friday, the third day, Lady Margaret Cecil, will preside at a tea party of the women employees and wives of the mien, and addresses will be given by the Countess of Latham and the Hon. Victoria Grosvenor. The three evenings will be closed with musical entertainments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>KITCHEN BOILER EXPLOSION AT ROCK</head>
        <p>KITCHEN BOILER EXPLOSION AT ROCK</p>
        <p>FERRY. WOMAN KILLED. WRECK OF THE PREMISES.</p>
        <p>On Thursday afternoon the kitchen of the house.s2, Mersey-road, Bock Ferry, was completely wrecked by the explosion of a small high pressure boilerfixed behind the fire. Tho house is in the occupation of Mr. J. Bishop, head schoolmaster on the training ship, Althar, and about half-past three, he being absent, Mrs. Bishop was in the kitchen banging clothes on a small line stretched across the mantel-piece. While she was doing so, as she afterwards stated, the whole front of the fireplace was dashed out, knocking her down and rendering her un- conscious. A little lad named Poole, who was Mr. Bis- hop's attendant on the ship happened to be in the house at the tine, and with the greatest pluck he ran into the kitchen, and undeterred by the clouds of steam and soot, which made it impossible for him to see anything, groped his way about the kitchen, to discover if any person had been injured. At his first attempt he did not find Mrs. Bishop, but after going into the lobby to get a little air, he made a second search. He heard Mrs. Bishop give a slight moan, and finding her beried in the bricks, plaster, and other debris, he managed to extri- cate her. By dint of great exertions, the plucky little fellow dragged M. Bishop into the lobby, and then, having done his own part, be ran out to seek what help he might find. An inspection of the house showed that the back of the fireplace had been blown out. knocking a rough doorway into the adjacent passage. The inside of the apartment was a total 'wreck. The boiler itself was found in a cupboard leading out of the farthest corner from the fireplace; the back door was smashed to pieces, and a hundredweight or so of bricks from the fireplace were found lying in the yard. Two wall cupboards were smashed, and the window was . blown out. All the furniture, crockery ware, and orna- inents were simply shattered, vrhile fragments of the mantlepiece, the front of the boiler, and the pipes were scattered all over the floor. It was also found that the cisterns upstairs were frozen, there being almost six inches of ice on them. Mrs. Bishop, who from the first was in a very precarious state, succumbed to her injuries at half-past nine on Thursday evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>WARRINGTON CORPORATION BEFORE THE NEWTON BENOIT.</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON CORPORATION BEFORE THE NEWTON BENOIT.</p>
        <p>ACQUITTED ON A WEAK °HAUGH. Under the Weights and Measures Agts,the Corpor- ation of Warrington were summoned on Saturday. before the Newton bench of magistrates, for being in possession of a weighing machine of unjust balance. Inspector Spencer prosecuted and Mr. J. L. Whittle, Town Clerk of Warrington, appeared on behalf of the Corporation of Warrington. Inspector Spencer said that on the 28th November he tested the weighing machine in possession or the Warrington Corporation, at the Water Works, VS'inwick, and he found it to be considerably against the purchaser. He drew the attention of the manager to this, and he pro- mised to draw the attention of the Corporation officials to it. He believed that the machine was used by farm- ers and others in the neighbourhood.—ln answer to Mr. Whittle witness said he was acquainted with most of the regulations under the Weights and Measures Acts. He was not aware that he ought to have asked the County Council for instructions before he proceeded with the case; he did not know that it was particularly necessary to serve a special notice on the defaulters, before he could summon them.—Mr. Whittle ; But it is ridiculous pretending to summon anyone without doing so. The Act distinctly says that in the case of any machine not being stamped after the 3rd of January, you must give the owner thereof a proper notice. Did you serve this natice upon us ?—Yes. Mr. Whittle : Did you give notice to the Corporation I'- 1 gave them a notice when I took them out to other people.—Mr. Whittle: You don't understand. You are compelled to serve a special notice upon any defaulter three months before you can take proceedings for an unstampecl machine only.—Well, I presumed that the Corporation of Warrington would know all about it. (Laughter.)—Mr. Whittle : You have no right to pre- sume any such thing. You have taken out the summons without the sanction of the local authorities ; you have failed to give the proper three months' notice, and now I must draw the attention of the Bench to the fact that apart from those two discrepancies in your prosecution, you cannot prosecute the Corporation for that machine because they do not Use it for trading pur- poses, as the Acts require.—They do use it. Farmers weigh their measure on it and pay.—Mr. Whittle : That was so two years ago, but it is used for no other purpose by the Corporation than checking loads of coal which they purchase. It is merely on a footing with any private individual keeping in his house a pair of scales to check his groceries with—Mr. Ross, the manager of the water works, was called upon to corroborate this. He did so. Inspector Spencer (hotly) : May I call a witness to prove serving the noticea.—The Chairman : Yes.—The witness was then called, being Inspector Spencer's assistant. He said he served the notice on the Corpor- ation when he served notices on other people. He gave it to Mr. Place. (Loud laughter.) Mr. Whittle : You gave it to Mr. Place ! Why, you might as well have served the notice on a scavenger in the street. Mr. Place is the market inspector, and wouldn't know anything about it, (Renewed laughter.) And that was the only notice you gave before taking proceedings, was it ?—Well, yes—it—yes, I think it</p>
        <p>was. Mr. Whittle then drew attention to the facts that as the machine was a private one no proceedings could be instituted ; secondly as three months' notice had not been given, the case could not be proceeded with ; and thirdly, a fact which he should have to report, the Inspector had not gained the sanction of the Local Government Board before he took out the summons. And again the only notice that it was attempted to serve upon them was given 0 the Market Inspector. (Laugh- ter.)—The Bench dismissed the' case.</p>
      </div>
