      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>DARKWOOD.</head>
        <p>DARKWOOD.</p>
        <p>A TALE OF LONEMOOR.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER I. A STRANGE RETURN.. It was a wild night upon the Yorkshire Moors. The wind was blowing fiercely, the rain was driven in sharp and fitful showers, intermixed with sleet; and darkness, like a dense mourning pall, overspread the wide desolation. In the midst of the wide moor, from the uneurtained window of a solitary house, a light streamed out into the black sberm. This light came from a sitting-room, in which an old man sat alone before a glowing fire. Ho was a gaunt, hard-featured man, stern of visage, grim and cold, with hair white as snow—a haughty proud old man, with an expression as of defiance to the whole human race. He was Squire Markham, the last of his race, a I widower, who lived the life of a hermit, shunning and listing the world. The time had been, and very recently, when the whole house of Lonemoor had been thronged with guests, members of excellent country families, to whom Mr. Markham had been known as a genial, hospitable host, a courteous kindly gentleman ; but for a whole year guests had been refused entrance at the portals, the grass had grown unchecked upon the avenues and in the neglected flower gardens, time windows of the mansion had been closed to the sunlight, the rooms had been wrapped in a dead silence, and Squire Markham had shut himself up seein; no one except his servants, and leading a morose and isolated existence. What had wrought t•mis change? What had transforme,d the genial. hospitable gentleman into the moody, suspicious recluse whom we hex() presented to the reader? What had turned his hair during that twelvemonths? The reason popularly and pityingly assigned for the change in him was the death of his only child, a girl of seventeen, which event had been declared jest one year before the opening date of our story. his daughter had been his idol. She had been beautiful, spirited, and affectionate, and he had lavished upon her such love and tenderness as fathers seldom feel. Her childhood had been one of exceptional hriehtnese ; every luxury that money could buy had beea bestowed upon her; teachers and masters in every accomplishment had been pro- vi.led for her ; an indulgent governess had watched over her ; and Aliss Clara had grown up a wilful, yet loving, a spoiled, headstrong, wayward girl, f till of caprice, pat noble, and loved by all who knew her. , People had predicted that Miss Markham, with her beauty and prospective wealth, woull make a ; grand marriage. Her father had dreamed that his darling would win a coronet. But dreams and ; predictions had been alike in vain - the statement of her early death had put an end to both. ' But how had she died? She had gone abroad with friends to spend some I men, ha in travel. Those months had nearly passed, and her father was anticipating her return, when a to:egram earl come to him commending his imme- . date presence at Berlin, at which place his - daughter aud her friends were at that time stop-</p>
        <p>ping. quire Markham had set out for the Continent iy the first train, proceeding o Berlin in all haste. When he returned to Louemoor a month later he was clad in mourning garb ; his hair Netts streaked with white; his visage had grown stern and grim; his manner cold and suspicious, and his entire op- rearance was that of a man whose light of life had been stricken o.tt, and wh se existence had become suddenly one of hopeless bitterness. Ho had upon the very day of his return announced his daughter's death, had dismissed the larger num- ber of his servants, denied himself to visitors, and settled into the barren life he had since Md. There were many who wondered that the unfortu- nate young heiress had not been brought home to be buried beside her mo her, but it was believed ' that Miss Markham had eied of an infectious disease, and that her father had not been allowed to transport her remains to England. The truth of this report could not be oonfirmed by questioning Spire Markham, for after announcing his daughter's death he never again mentioned her name, or suffered another to mention it in his presence. The old hotmekeeper who had loved her young mistress with a mother's love, closed the handsome suite of rams Miss Mark:tat!: had occupied, but visited it daily in secret to air the dainty garments it still contained, and to dust the furniture as if she expected her young lady's return, and intended to be prepared for it. A dreary gloom hung over the mansion of Lone. moor during those long and dreary months, a gloom that to-night pressed wt.h awful weight upon Squire Markham's heart as he sat alone by his fire- side. The room was long and low, with dusky shadows in the far corner. The walls were wainscoted vritti black oak, and thkkly hung with gilt-framed pictures. A moderator lamp, with books, engra- vings, and newspapers, stood upon a large centre table. Low, carved bookcases, well filled with books, stood upon either side of the wide chimney. The alcove that held Clare's piano had been re. moved to the attic, with Miss Markham's favourite 1 drawing-room chair and writing table. The room was luxurious, but very gloomy. Eves • in contrast to the storm outside, with its lamplight t and firelight, it did not seem homelike. A sound—a step upon the hall stair—startled Squire Markham, and he raised his head and • looked eagerly, in a quick forgetfulness, tovrardi I the door. ! The step passed on. It was only Mrs. Quillet, the housekeeper, who was moving about intent upon her duties. Squire Markham sighed heavily and bent forward and stirred up the fire, adding a</p>
        <p>few mole. What e. night it is!" he muttered, as a fresh gust of wind and rain beat against the window panes and tore round the house like a pack of wolves. "An awful night for the homeless. Strange howl think of her to-night? It is just a year to-day since I received that telegram from Berlin—just one year !" His face was hardened at the reoollection, and grew grimmer and sterner. His thin lips set them- selves in a hard, bloodless line. The door opened, and the housekeeper, a motherly Yorkshire woman, with a broad and kindly face, which for the last year had been always shadowed, stopped into the room, hearing a tea-tray. Squire Markham, comprehending her errand, snored his hand in a gesture commanding her departure, although he did not turn his head. The housekeeper withdrew her burden as silently as possible, and again the old squire was alone. 'A year tonight," he muttered. "How the thought of her haunts me. And what an awful night." .door slammed somewhere in the house. The Vain dashed spitefully upon the window-pane. And again, like a pack of hungry wolv, s in pursuit of prey, the wind tore round the old mansion and swept onward over the moor. An awful night, truly. Heaven pity any home- less wanderer that night upon the desolate York- shire wastes ! Squire Markham listened to the storm with ears soddenly quickened to its undertones. Ah ! what was that? A voice—a human voice! Some one out upon the moor crying for help. He sprang up, his face whitening, his breathing suspended for o mmient. . . . Yes, it was a voice—a woman s voice —in piteous appeal for help. Squire Markham rushed to the window, but the rain was falling in a solid sheet at that instant, and he could see nothing But as the fitful shower lessened he saw the broad stream ot light from his own window falling upon the sodden moor, and in the pale and sickly glare a woman's figure was tottering towards the house. No beggar had been turned unfed and unhelped. from Lonemow. The purse of the old squire had ever been open to the needy. The sight of that wanderer in the storm might be supposed to touch his heart. He made a movement from the window, as if to call his housekeeper, and then he halted as if suddenly tunaod to stone, and glared from the window as if the woman were some frightful Gor- gon. His breath came quick and hard new. His eyes Lwl in them a wild, incredulous tnare. The woman staggered across the si ream of light, still crying pitifully, her arms uptossed, her face In shadow. She was making for the front door; and still the old squire stood motionless, seeming frozen with a great horror. fie could not watch her slow progress to the door. He was still standing in that trance-like stillness when a sound vibrated through the great house— the sound of the brass knocker upon the Goldin* used front door. As if the sound had broken his trance, he bounded forward, his face white and furious, his eyes blazing. He entered the hall at the same zomentwith Mrs Quillet.who. pale and frishtened.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>CIIAPTIE IL</head>
        <p>CIIAPTIE IL</p>
        <p>BETWkIIIN LIEN AND DRAWL The old squire's face did not soften as Ms stem eyes slowly reverte4 to the prostrate figure of the poor young wanderer at his feet. Terrible se Nemesis, grim and pitiless as an avenging fate, he stood like a statue, without mercy or relenting it</p>
        <p>his heart. And yet that girl was indeed his own daughter and only child—that Clare Markham who had once been the light of his house and heart, the belie of the country side—that daughter whose death he had proclaimed to the world, and which 'was supposed to have wrecked his life. The fierce wind seized the door he still held open, and whirled it shut again. The girl lay on the floor, her long black hair streaming over her face, which partially showed , through it white as markle, her arms thrown above her head, her figure inanimate. The house- keeper, with a look of i leading and reproach at I the stony-visaged father, flew to the girl and knelt beside her, gatherin,, her up into her arms. "You will not Reial her away upon a night like tkis, sir ?" she cried. "She must not go. She shall not go. For the love of heaven, master, for the sake of my young lady's mother, have pity on your child !" But the squire heard the appeal unmoved. "She cannot go forth like this," exclaimed Mrs. Quillet, in passionate pleading. "See, she is help- less, unconscious. To put her out now will be to commit a deliberate murder. She must stay till daybreak—till she can walk. If you put her out to-night, master, she will die, and the oountry will ring with the scandal of it." The stony feature of the old squire quivered now. He was proud of his old family name. .No breath of soandtl had ever tarnished ita purity. He could have borne to see his daughter dead, on the moor, but he could not have borne to have her story the subject of gossip in every alehouse and cottage in the county. He said, harshly— ' " See that she is gone before daybreak. I will not see her again." He secured the fastenings of the door, and went into the sitting-room again. He had scarcely disappoared when the butler, Quillet, the hus-and of the old housekeeper, ad- ' vanced from the further corner of the hall where he had stood for some minutes, paralysed with error and amazement. _</p>
        <p>The light fell on the girl's face. He gazed at the girl's wan features, half hidden by the dark hair, and whispered, shrilly— "ls it our Miss Clare? What is this mystery! The master said she was dead. Why does he treat her thus ?" The housekeeper's face was strangely pale and drawn. A look of trouble had settled in her eyes. She answered, sharpy— ''Don't be asking foolish questions I How should I know? I believe her dead. I can scarcely yet believe that she is alive. Take her up and carry her to my room. Pour girl ! Poor lamb I" The butler obeyed, picking up the young wan- derer and conveying her down the hall into a pas- sage beyond, and to the housekeeper's own room, Mrs. Quillet following with the lamp. The housekeeper's room: was well lighted and well warmed. The butler laid the girl upon a low, broad lounge, and wheeled the latter near the fire. "Now go and make a fire in Miss Clare's own room," said the housekeeper. "She must be got to bed directly. Thank heaven, the rooms are well aired. I'll make the bed myself. And, John, say nothing to the housemaid. Let no one know that Miss Glare lives, and is come home. The master weuld kill us if we let the truth come to light." The butler hurried away on his errand. Then the old housekeeper, pitiful and tender to the nursling who had, laid on her bosom long ago, drew away the long streaming hair from the girl's face, and gazed on those wan features with strum Intentness. Then with tears she kissed her face, iier lips, her hands. "Poor girl !" she murmured, removing the clinging shawl and the small sodden boots, and 2hafing the little cold hands. "Heaven have pity on us all I Better had she died than to live for such a fa e as this I" She took up the slender fingers in her own stumpy hands. The girl's fingers were bare. No wedding ring gleamed upon 'them. The house- keeper sighed heavily, and muttered to herself word of rief and anguish. And yet she did not relax in her efforts to restore warmth and life to that chilled and wasted frame. :4he poured brandy between the pale lips, and worked over the girl with a zeal and anxiety that were inexpressibly pinful and bitter. The heavy eyelids lifted at last, but the dark eves were vacant and wandering in their glances. The girl only moaned sorrowfully, and dropped her • eyelids again, too weak and weary even to speak. "M is Clare," said the old woman softly ; " Miss Glare, is it really you? How you have changed, , my darling. But whatever has happened, rye a heart for you still, Miss Clare. ;;L1L.44.1. :a A alibde 9 422PCX-WW4Z,..I' 4-44</p>
        <p>timed over her features, Out se MAI not, speux. The butler now reappeared, to say that a fire had boon made in the young lady's chamber. "Take her up then," said the housekeeper, "and carry her upst,irs." -Qtiillet obeyed, and the little procession marched into the great hall again, and up the Wide stair, case.. • The oh; squire must have heard the trampling of feet as they passed his door, but he made no</p>
        <p>. . iss Markham's bedchamber wa ling and large, with lofty 'frescoed ceiling, and with walls hung with fluted crimson silk. The carpet was of crimson velvet.' The windows were hung with crimson 'silk curtitins. It looked a very bower of warmth and splendour new, with a glowing fire in the wide grate, and with a dozen wax candles lighted in their silver sconces on the low marble man tel. piece. The butler hal drawn a sofa before the hearth. He laid the girl upon it and departed in quest of freshly-aired bed linen in the housekeeper's room below. He presently returned, and Mrs. Quillet hastened tb make the bed, her .husband assisting her. The old butler's eyes continually reverted to the girl on the sofa, while his firigers were busy with the fleecy blankets and great pillows. Presently he said, in a hushed whisper— "l can't make it out, Maria. The master said that our yonng lady was dead. Miss Clare was different from this. Isn't there some mistake 1" - "What mistake should there be? Don't I know the girl I nursed from infancy to womanhood"? There's a mystery, John. Heaven alone knows what it all means; but this I do know—that is our young lady yonder, alive and in the flesh, and while I live I will stand by her." Neither attempted to solve the mystery further. They had believed Miss Clare was dead,- they ascepted her return to life as something beyond explanation. • Go now," said the housekeeper at length, "yon must mount the fastest horse in the stable and ride to Penistone. Bring a doctor at once—not the old family doctor, but a stranger. And mind, John, not one word of Miss Markham. You want his services for a poor young wandering woman who came to our door in a storm. Hark to that storm 1 It will be a rough ride and a long one; but hasten, John, for the love of mercy. She has sore need of help." The butler departed. 'Then the motherly old housekeeper undressed her charge, robed her in a eight-dress taken from a drawer near at hand, and placed her in the wide bed. She combed and orushed the tangled hair, and bathed the palid face, Intl all the while the girl watched her with vacant :iyes, speaking no word, but moaning now and then- as in extreme pain. Mrs. Qui Ilet having cared for her charge, gathered dr) her discarded clothing. The gown was of silk, but it was faded, draggled, and torn. Ruffles of point lace were on the neck • and sleeves. The 3 hawl was an Indian cashmere of peculiar vattern, great value. he Thousekeeper consigned the articles to a wardrobe, after an unavailingisearch for some eine' I to the mystery of the past year of her young lady's</p>
        <p>life. The pocket is empty," she said to herself. "She had, no money ; she wears no jewels ; she has I,ot ?vett a wedding ring. Where has she been during the past year? How has she offended her fathet I' beyond all forgiveness ?" The storm raged on, beating against the windows, Ind sighin4 and whirling, and moaning among the trees. the old squire paced the floor, a prey O maddening thoughts, scourged by the furies of recolleCtion, embittered to deepest hatred of the t;irl upsta rs, and invoking curses upon her with every stride and breath. And in that upper room ,life and death fought fiercely for that object of hi, hatred. Mrs. Quillet feared that every moment might be the last for her charge. But when, three hours later, after a hard, cold ride, the butler returned, bringing with him a doctor, Clare Markham lay on her pillows, white and spent, like one dead, save that her breast rose faintly with her slow, almost inaudible, , breathing, and beside her lay a puny infant, a little wailing creature, whose hold on life seemed too weak to endure for an hour. " The child will probably die," said the doctor, ' as he turned to depart, after giving instructions te Mrs. Quillet to prepare certain strengthening pb• tions for the patient, "and the no her al-o. I caa do nothing further. If you should need me you cam send for me again."</p>
        <p>( To he continued ).</p>
