      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>RAILROAD ON ICE.</head>
        <p>A RAILROAD ON ICE.</p>
        <p>--- The communications between the two shores of the St. Lawrence river at Montreal are made, as is known, by means of the Victoria tubular bridge constructed some 35 years ago, which is the longest of its kind in the world, the metallic span being 6,500 ft. long. But from this point to the Atlantic, for a distance of 1,000 miles, there is no other bridge, and all the railroads established on both sides of the St. Lawrence have necessarily to cross it. The company of the Grand Trunk Railroad. which built it, levies a right of way toll of 10 dole. per car, and Sc. per passenger. To avoid payment of these moneys the South-Eastern Railway Com- pany had the idea some 10 years ago, of construct- ing, in winter, a communication between the tete shores by means of a railroad established on the ice. Every winter the work is done over again, and it amply repays for the outlay. The length a this ice road is about two miles, between Hochelage and Longuell. The readway is easily built. The track leaves the main track parallel to the shore, then curves gradually in such a manner as to be perpen- dicular to it, and then, again, before it strikes the other shores, it curves anew so as to become nearlY parallel to the opposite side, and then it is con' ! nected with the main track on this shore. Mr. Senical the engineer of the line, constructed it a&amp; as follows ,—Pine timbers about 10in. by I2in., and from 16ft. to 25ft. long, are placed like ties on the rough surface of the ice, being blocked so as to ha ' horizontal in a direction perpendicular t! ; the roadway by means of blocks of ice, an' according to whatever grade may be adopted in the longitudinal props. These long cross ties arrl placed at about 71t. 3in. from centre to centre an° they receive two parallel lines of longitudinal tim- bers of the same dimensions, 10in. by 12in., distant also from each other in the length of the track of 71t. 3in. Over these longitudinal ties, or stringers* and perpendicular to them, are placed. in the usual manner, and at the ordinary distance from each other, the small cross ties used in railroad construe' tion, to which are spiked the rails as is customary. In this manner the rails are laid on a sort of crib, about 301 n, high above the level of the ice. lge spokes, no joints of any kind, are used to fasten timbers or ties together, every piece of timber being • merely laid down ard blocked to its proper position or level with ice blocks. The whole crib is then. filled with broken pieces of ice up to the level 01 the bottom of the rail, and this kind of ballasting is even made to project beyond the ends of the first timbers laid at bottom. Holes having been dug through the ice surface, the water ot the river i&amp; pumped over the whole, and in 24 hours, or there- .4! about a perfectly solid and compact track is ob- tained, over which trains can run. The thickness of the ice of the river in these latitudes is never less than 16in., and this does not include the ire ballasting of the tracks. It is much more, if We , refer ourselves to the preceding experiments, than is required to support the charge which can be placed upon it, especially if we consider that, fret this mode of construction, each lineal foot of tritc! correspords to at least 25 square feet of ice to he s/. the load it may have to support. The same timber' can be used, of course, the following year.</p>
