        <p>THE PLEASURE FAIR CONDEMNED. The MAYOR moved the adoption of the minutes of this committee the principle one of which a recommendation published in the" Examiner" last week, that the Secre- tary of State be applied to for an order abolishing the pleasure or folly fairs. Alderman BLACZIEURST seconded. Councillor MILTON asked whether the ratepayers of Warrington had wished the Corporation to d ispense with the pleasure fair. (Hear, hear.) He did not think it was the Council's business except as a requisition from the ratepayers, to do away with the pleasure fair. He thought the idea was more a fad than any real wish to do void to the town. He did not see why poor people should be deprived of their pleasure at the fair. Many gentlemen who objected to these fairs could go to Man- chester or Liverpool to enjoy themselves,and poor people had not the money to do this, and he thqught the Cor- poration might allow them at least twice a year to have their fair. He moved an amendment that the minutes be passed with the exception of that relating to the</p>
        <p>A SERIOCS ALLEGATION. COUIRtiIIOT FLEMING desired to say that he believed a quantity of bad meat was being sold in town. It was a Inatter of the deepest importance, and required the trn- le2ediate attention of the Council. Ths persons selling Meat were afraid of being detected by having their p'eat delivered in Warrington and so had it sent on to el4kLetb. (Sensation.) nue Mayon : Is your information reliable? e;012 ncillor FLEMING : Perfectly. It ia from a very , Pectable ratepayer of the town who is in a position "e know, Tha MAYOR: We shall all thank you if you will im- van all information to the Chairman of the ,Committee, Ind necessary steps will be taken at once. SMETIIIIRST drew the attention of the Conn- WI to the fact that now the epidemic was abating the t*Pers were not supplied with reports of its progress. Councillor SUTTON said this was done for a proper ebject,and publicity would be given to the reports when necessary. The minutes were then adopted. THE REVELATION NOT FORTHCOMING. The minutes of the Sanitary Committee were sub- Initted for adoption by Councillor F. W. Monks, and se- Ided by Councillor Beaman. There was nothing of note 111 those minutes,and as they were about to be adopted, Councillor Straw rose and said : I am rather sur- rised to see those minutes being passed so quickly. If ~Ina not mistaken the Chairman told us at last meeting ;.nat he intended to make a startling revelation. Where le it ? Councillor F. W. MONIES : It will be made in good mine.</p>
        <p>fair. Councillor DixoN said the matter had been before the Council seven or eight times, and expressed the hope that the fair would be done away with as Church-et.</p>
        <p>fair had been. Councillor Dr. CANNELL seconded the amendment. Alderman SUTTON pointed out that the Committee had several times declared in favour of the fair being done away with in the interests of the public and those being near it. (Cries of "No.") He said yes. (Councillor FLOOD : No, no.) They must all have been asleep, or did not know what was taking place at the time. Council- lor Flood could say what he liked afterwards. Would anybody in the Council like to live in the neighbourhood of—(A Voice: The organ)—where this fair was held. The noise was disgraceful and the conduct and langu- age and everything else there was more than anybody could bear. On the occasion of the last fair, he was told, there was a child lying dying near it and the noise could not be stopped. The fair was a disgrace to the town, and ought to have been put a stop to long ago. Councillor SHAW GREEN said he should be extremely sorry to interfere with the pleasures of anyone in the borough, but he lived near the fair, and the noise was deafening and horrifying. During the last fair there was an engine screeching from about 6 in the evening till midnight, and peace or comfort was impossible. Objections had been raised by the inhabitants of the neighbourhood, and therefore on that ground and on general grounds he supported the resolution. Councillor HEATON said he lived as close as any Coun- cillor to the fair. Councillor SHAW GREEN said Councillor Heaton lived further off than he did. Councillor noon said the Council had power under their bye-laws to restrict the nuisance of the engines, and other things, but thought fairs were introduced for the amusement of the people, and the movement for their abolition was started by religious faddists and was an interference with the liberties of the citizen. The MAYOR: I don't think so. Councillor FLood maintained that when the 'general public demanded the abolition of the fair and net till then would be the time to advocate it. Councillor R. T. Farectouem thought it was not the fair but the noise that was objected to. The noise created by the whistles was most objectionable as he had good reason to know when certain fair people were allowed to go down to Howley with their stands. Councillor ROBERTS congratulated the Markets Com- mittee on the position which they had taken up in re- gard to this question. No matter how fossiised, anti- ' quated,and musty an institution had become,there were always some people ready to rise and defend it. (Laugh. ter) Often fools stepped in where angels feared to tread. (Much laughter.) Be was not going to suggest to which class the mover and seconder of the ai...endment be- ' longed. (Renewed laughter.) He would leave that to the Council to decide for themselves. (Laughter.) Be- Jere the Council proceeded to throw cut the resolution which had been carefully considered by the Markets Committee, some reason might be given for doing so. They had had no reasomgiven by either the proposer or secender. The latter merely plumped himself down after stating that he seconded the amendment. (Laugh- ter.) He admitted that there was a time when the old- fashioned village fair was a delightful institution. They had splendid exhibitions of fat women—(laughter)— •mermaids—(laughter)—very great swindles in their way. (Laughter, and "Councillor Roberts was younger then.") Younger,and a great deal more foolish. (Laugh- ter.) The only thing he regretted was that some men, as they got older, did not get wiser. Be repeated that the old-fashioned fair had ceased to exist. What had they now? They had a fair in which abounded scream- ing, horrid machinery that was a source of anguish to the people who lived within a radius of a mile from the centre of the fair twice a year in order to gratify the taste of a certain number of people in Warrington, and also to please a few travelling showmen, who were not the best of people ; they were people who were calcula- ted to menace the health of the town. Then again, they only came to take the hard-earned wages of the labour- ing classes, that might be spent in a more profitable manner. Then the Ceuncil would allow the continuance 1 of the fair, forsooth, to give these itinerants the oppor- tunity of swindling the public I He was delighted that the resolution had been passed by the Markets Commit- tee. If it was contended that the Council had no right to interfere with the sources of pleasure to the people, he would urge that the source of pleasure in this ease was very limited. They ought not to pander to tke lowesttastes of the people, but to cultivate the higher tastes. Councillor Smarm:rare eaid he had not received a single complaint from the Town Hall Ward as to the fair exaept as to the whistling. He thought this was all that should be done away with. Councillor alma supported the Committee's recom-1 mendation. Councillor Dr. CANNELL said he had had the pleasure of shooting down the galleries at last fair, and should shoot there again. (Laughter.) H, had seen Alderman Sutton shooting there when a boy, and did not think the Ccuncil had a right to take this old privilege of the fair from the poor. Councillor rzutrierox expressed his surprise that any respectable member of the Council should be in favour of the continuance of the fair, which was considered a nuisance, by people living in the vicinity. (Cries of" Name.") The people at the wheelwrights shop close to complained. On the occasion of a certain football match, the Secretary of the Warring- ton Football Club was informed by the Medical Officer is reply to his query, that it would not be safe to .hold a match unless the players were vaccinated. (Laughter.) He was surprised that the Authorities had not insisted on the travelling showmen being vaccinated. (Laughter.) A division was then taken. Four voted for the amend- ment, and 19 for the motion, which was carried. The minutes were thus adopted. GAS COMMITTEE.—THE BAD GAS AGAIN. At a meeting of the Gas Committee, the Borough Treasurer submitted a statement of the amount required for the payment of interest and annuities, &amp;c., due on the 31st December. The following resolutions were I passed That 30 lamps be erected in the district of the Runcorn Rural Sanitary Authority, at 40g. per lamp per annum, lighted until twelve midnight from the 14th I August to the 14th May ;" " that the tender of Messrs. Ashmore, Benson, and Pearce to supply a scrubber at £596 be accepted ;" "that a 3-h.p. Crossley's gas ' engine be purchased for use in the fitting shop at Long- ford ;" and" that four lamps be erected on Wilderspool Causeway, and that the Rural Sanitary Authority be I charged 545. per lamp per annum." Councillor R. T. FAI EICLOUGH moved the adoption. Councillor DEPHERD seconded. Councillor FLEMING said he had had very great com- plaints about the quality of the gas. It was stated in the minutes that the average illuminating power was equal to 18.79 candles. He doubted that very much,and it Might be so at a certain period but it had been far below it at other periods. Councillor Fleming also re- ferred to the heaviness of his gas bill, and that of an ex- councillor who had complained to him. Councillor R. T. FAIRCLOCIGII : Last year they com- plained that the gas did not give sufficient light. We then caused the engineer to put more pressure on, and</p>
