        <p>as the result the gas bills are larger. (Hear, hear, and laughter.l Councillor Smarnunsr : That's a nice way of getting out of it. (Laughter.) The report was adopted. FIRE ENGINE COIDLITTRIC. Councillor HUTCHINSON moved the adoption of this Committee's minutes, and referred to the following minute which had been pas.ed since the printing of the general Council minutes :—" That a sirip of land on the south-east corner of the yard at the Fire Engine Station be let to Mr. Hugh Greenough, for the purpose of improving the entrance from Heath Side to his premises, at the annual rent of Ss., and subject to six months' notice Mr. Greenough to take down and re- build the wall on the line agreed to, and to rebuild the wall in its present position when required by notice to do so." He explained that the arrangement would not interfere with the convenience of the committee, but it would certainly be a convenience to a tradesman, and probably prevent many an accident. It was only reasonable that the proposal should be sanctioned by the Council. Councillor Dr. CANWILL seconded, and the minutes were adopted.</p>
        <p>WATER COMMITTEE. Thib Committee recommended that the Appleton water be supplied to Messrs. Armitage and Rigby. The DEPUTY Maroa moved the confirmation of their minutes. The committee, he said, were anxious that other manufacturers should apply for similar water, so that it could be made use of to a greater extent than it was now. Councillor Hamann seconded, and the minutes were adopted.</p>
        <p>THE DISAPPEARA.NCE OF A. MANCHESTER GIRL. SENSATIONAL SEQUEL. The body of the girl found under the ice on the canal at Blackburn, on New Year's Day, has been identified as that of Catherine Louisa Cahill, aged eighteen, belonging to a family residing at Levenshulme, Manchester. Her father is a pensioner in Woolwich Hospital. She was employed as a waitress at a temperance hotel, and on Sunday, the 18th ult.. she disappeared, after having vainly tried to persuade a fellow-servant to commit suicide with her by tying their legs together and jump- ing into the canal.</p>
        <p>THE STRIKE OF COLLTERS IN GERMANY.</p>
        <p>IJeT</p>
        <p>- SAARBRUC/C, MONDAY.—Largely attended meetings of miners on strike were held yesterday at Louisenthal, Bildstock, Guichenbach, and Holz, at which it was de- cided to hold out. To-day the strike in this district is almost general. BERLIbT, MONDAY NlGHT.—Telegrams from Saarbrueck state that the strike of miners is now general through- out the Saar district, and that only 4,546 men remain at work out of nearly 30,000. Disturbances of a more or less serious character have occurred at Ensdorf and Elversberg and at the Vonderheyd Pit. A strung force of gendarmerie is being concentrated at Treves, the nearest large town to the disturbed district, and the 69th and 29th battalions of infantry are being held in readiness to proceed to any point where it may be deemed advisable to summon military assistance.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 7, 1898. ANNOYING PUBLIC MEN. EXCITING INCIDENTS OF LIFE IN THE PROTEST FROM MR. W. S. CAINE. UNITED STATES. Mr. W.S.Caine,M.B.,writes to the" Standard " : With Naar YORK, TUBSDAY.—A mob of some three hundred several other members of Parliament resident in London persons assembled to-day outside the gaol in the village I have been annoyed by some rascal sending forged of Bakersville, North Carolina, and attempted to pene- orders for goods to a large number of tradesmen all over trate into the building for the purpose of lynching a Landon, to their great inconvenience and loss. This man who was confined there on a charge of murder. ha i been repeated frequently during the last six or Their attempts to force an entrance were, however, seven years, and the letters ordering the goods are pro- stubbornly resisted by the Sheriff and his 50 deputies, nounced by an expert to be in the same handwriting, and during the fight one of the mob was killed and Bev- badly disguised,: in which letters of a most offensive eral others were injured. The mob threatens further character have been written to the friends of at least violence. three or four public men to my knowledge, myself WEDNESDAT.—Reports have been received here from included. Perhaps you will permit me through your Coalcreek, Tennessee, stating that great agitation pre- columns to warn London tradesmen against executing vaße amang the miners there. The State troops were orders purporting to have come from public men, who [lily fired upon by miners, and the authorities are are not their regular customers. I would gladly pay taming precautions in view of possible troubles. fifty pounds reward to anyone furnishing evidence on The World's" correspondent at Farmersville Lout- which this troublesome person could be brought to jut- Mann, telegraphs that the hearing at the magistrates' tine. court at Marion of a lawsuit in which two prominent farmers were concerned on one side and three on the other, the litigants expressed themselves dissatisfied with the magistrates' decision, and a pitched battle with pistols ensued in court. About thirty shots were fired, three persons being killed and two injured. Adolfo Villareal, a wealthy ranchman at Santa Rosa, Mexico, who was recently convicted of hiring a per- son to assassinate an Englishman named M‘Kellar,living near him, has been condemned to ten years' imprison- ment, with confiscation of his estate. A telegram from Indianapolis published here states that eight persons, supposed to be Missouri cowboys, entered a saloon in the hamlet of Cory, Indiana, where a similar number of residents of the place were drinking. Owing to the cowboys refusing to pay for what they , had, a quarrel arose, which culminated in fighting, in which revolvers, glasses, and all kinds of missiles were freely used, with the result that six persons were more or less seriously injured. PITTSBURG, WEBB/MAL—Twelve MOTO informations have been made against men on strike at Messrs. Car- neige's works at Homestead, Pennsylvania, for com- plicity in the alleged poisoning of non-unionist work- men. LonisviLzn, TUBSDAY.—A fight, arising out of some trivial matter, occurred to-day between two parties of citizens at Sayersville, Magoffin county,and has resulted in the death of several of the combatants. I man man named John Davis was shot dead. W. Descins, constable was fatally wounded, and Montville Descins, justice of the peace, is supposed to have been mortally wounded. Shepherd Cole, coroner, was fearfully in- jured with a club, and he will probably die from the effects of his hurts. Brice Patrick was shot in the bowels and is reported to be dying ; and a rifle bullet struck W. Patrick in the head. The latter was at first supposed to be dead, but he subsequently rallied, and recovered. No arrests have yet been made, and another fight is feared.</p>
      </div>
