      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>SAFETY OF THE UMBRIA.</head>
        <p>SAFETY OF THE UMBRIA.</p>
        <p>All anxiety regarding the Cunard steamer Umbria and her crew and passengers was dispelled on Saturday by the arrival of the steamer at New York. It appears that on the 23rd ult., when the Umbria was south of the Banks of Newfoundland. a fracture was discovered in the shaft, and the machinery was stopped at half-past five in the afternoon. After drifting for twelve hours in the storm she was assisted by the Hamburg liner Bohemia, which towed her For seventy-five miles, but at nidnight on Saturday, the 24th, the cable broke. There was then a heavy gale blowing, and the vessels lost sight of each other. After drifting three days to the south-east the Umbria started again with her own machinery, which had been repaired with great difficulty by the chief engineer, Mr. Tomlinson. The weather by that time had become fine, and the vessel proceeded to her destination at half speed. The Umbria entered dock at noon on Saturday. An immense crowd was assembled on the pier, and loudly cheered the passengers as the vessel passed in, those on board heartily returning the greeting and giving cheers for the captain and officers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>_-*---- SKATING.</head>
        <p>_-*---- SKATING.</p>
        <p>For the first time in the history of speed skating in the London district, a straight mile race among ama- teurs was decided at the Welsh Harp lake at Hendon on Wednesday under brilliantly fine conditions and on a track of sharp ice. Twenty-three competitors tried under the time test for a series of valuable prizes, and the result was most successful, for not only did several ' of the competitors cover the distance in good time, but the amateur champion of London, Mr. B. B. Tarring, accomplished the journey in the fast time of 3min. 142-5 sec., the best on record in England. Owing to the approach of darkness the competition was decided on the first round, the following being the fastest times: —B. B. Tarring, Crouch End, 3min. 14 2-ssec. (£5 ss. price), 1;C. E. Dell, Willesden, 3min. 30sec. (£3 3s. prize), 2; R. C. Aveling, Stratford, 3inin 32sec. (silver medal), 3; R. W. Holmes, Tottenham, 3min. 33sec.„ 4; Hiam, Stratford, 3min. 374.5 sec., 5 ;J. G. Magmusson, Westminster, 3min. 40 3-53e0., 6 ; B. C. Cane, Balliam, 3mln. 43sec., ; E. Osmond, Norwood, 3min. 44 •1-ssec., 6. At 'Littleport Skating Club meeting on Wednesday the first place in a race for £32 was taken by :lames Smart.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>LIBERATOR BUILDING SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>THE LIBERATOR BUILDING SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Efforts are being made to realise the assets of the Liberator Building Society. It is feared that the share- holders will lose their capital, and that the depositors will not receive more than three or four shillings in the pound, Mr. Arnold Morley, M.P., has joined the Liberator Relief Fund Committee. Some of the more pressing cases of distress caused by the failure of the Society have been relieved. The fund raised, however, is at present comparatively small, amounting only to gB5O. At Knighton, in Radnorshire, many persons have been rendered penniless by the collapse ot the Soeiety and the House and Land Investment Trust.</p>
        <p>The Press Association has good reason to believe that Mr. J. Spencer Balfour, at present member for Burnley, has left the country, and that he is unlikely to return for some time. Before his departure he executed a power et attorney in order that his business affairs may be managed during his absence. The fact that Mr. Balfour is no longer in England will probably be known in the course of a few hours to the officials con- nected with the liquidation ef the many companies forming the Balfour group. It is understood that his application for the stewardship of the Chiltern Hundred will be granted by the Chancellor of the Xxchequer.</p>
        <p>A RIIIT BLISSITO TO MOTHERS.—Mothers who value the health and comfort of their infasts, and the value of etaiet aights, should always have a. bottle of Mrs. Johnson's Ameri- can Soothing Syrup at hand during the teething period. The dangers and difficulties of teething are reduced to a minimum by using this elegant embrocation, which contains nothing that can injure the most delicate infant. Sold by all chemists at is. lid. per battle; or post free for 15 stamps from the sole proprietors, BARCLAY AIM SONS. Limited, 95, Farringdou-et London.--stdvt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting was held at the Town Hall on Tuesday night. Mr. J. Pritchard presided and there were present the Rev. Canon Maitland Wood, Dr. MacDougall, Messrs. A. Salkeld, Thomas Handley, G. Moss (clerk.) PRITATE SCHOOLS. On the minutes of the last meeting being read, Dr. McDougall referring to the resolution with regard to private schools, said the Board had no intention of in- terfering with such institutions that were well con- ducted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>STATISTICS.</head>
        <p>STATISTICS.</p>
        <p>... The report of the School Attendance Officer showed the certified Government accommodation in the public elementary schools was 3,349 ; total number on rolls, 3,737; same period last year, 3,619 ; increas*, 118 ; total average attendance, 2,948 ; last year, 2,685 ; in- crease 26.3 ; percentage of attendances 788 ; last year, 74 ;increase 48.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>PAUPERISING EDUCATION.</head>
        <p>PAUPERISING EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>Attendance Officer Bowyer reported that ths Com- mittee of the Board of Guardians had met during the the month, and had remitted the fees of 55 children attending the public elementary schools for quarter end- ing March 31st next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>THRHE TZARS' WORK.</head>
        <p>THRHE TZARS' WORK.</p>
        <p>Dr. McDougall proposed a cordial vote of thanks to the chairman and remarked that the Board had done meritorious work during its three years' existence, and he thought the recent action of the ratepayers as represented by a large body of leading members of the community, were satisfied with the work the Board had done. He spoke of the exemplary manner in which the chairman had conducted the business of the Board.— Canon Wood seconded the motion which was supported by Mr. Salkeld and carried unnnimou.ly.—The Chairman in reply sail it would be well to recount a little of the history of the School Board during the past three years which had not been unfruitful. The accommodation at the elementary schools had been increase d by 120 new places, the average attendance had increased by about 264, and the percentage of attendance was 81.9 more than one per cent. highei than three years ago, and the highest yet recorded. The first winter after their elec- tion the Board established a continuation evening school which had been carried on successfully by the headmas- ter and his assistants. There had been an average attendance of about 60 pupils who had received a superior education to what could be obtained in the elementary schools. Since their appointment the Assisted Educa- tion Act had come into force and the Board had inter- preted that Act in its widest sense, and gave free education in the Board's schools, and the children of the ratepayers had had the full benefit of that Act from the first day possible, namely September 1891. During the last few months they had also establisned savings Banks in connection with their schools, and they had had a very beneficial effect upon the children in teaching them provident habits, and their accumulated petice now amounted to £6O. They hail an efficient teaching staff, and the members might congratulate themselves that their schools were Pow in a very flourishing and efficient state. In addition, they had voluntarily taken upon themselves other duties— the responsibility of promoting advanced education by establishing science and art classes, and this work had been a success. The students took deep interest in their subjects, and manifested a desire to qualify themselves to occupy higher positions in society. He thought the work of the Board during the past throe years aught be considered eminently satisfactory,and that the members had done their duty to the ratepayers as regarded pri- mary education, and more than their duty in endea- vouring to promote and carry on higher education in some of its branches. In conclusion, he proposed a vote of thanks to the vice-chairman.—Mr. Handley seconded. —Carried.—Canon Wood replied, and mentioned that he had only had to occupy the chair on one occasion In reply to Dr. McDougall, the Chairman said the cost of free education to the ratepayers did not exceed £4O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>0•/•••••••110. •••••.•••</head>
        <p>0•/•••••••110. •••••.•••</p>
        <p>Speaking at Newcastle on Monday night, Mr. T. M. Healy, M.P., said he hoped he should soon be able to respond to the toast of " The Irish Parliament." His only fear with regard to the Horne Rule Bill was that Mr. Gladstone, with hi a great parliamentary experience, would pin them rather too tightly in ths financial clauses. He pointed out how heroically the Liberal party had stuck to Ireland, and said that such devotion deserved the fidelity of the Irish party. I KAY'S Tto PILLS, specific in Neuralgia. Face-ache, 9+cl. and 130. ; postage Id. Of all Chemists.—AdeS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>RE-UNION PILGRIMAGE TO JERUSALEM.</head>
        <p>A RE-UNION PILGRIMAGE TO JERUSALEM.</p>
        <p>It was somewhat humorously suggested during the Grindelwald Conference, that Dr. Lunn might appro- priately organise • re-union conference, to be held in Jerusalem ,and that the birthplace of Christianity would be a natural centre for such a gathering. Whilst not attempting so serious a task as this, Dr. Lunn and Mr. Woolrych Perowne, the son of the Bishop of Worcester, have organised,according to the forthcoming number of the" Review of the Churches," a re-union pilgrimage to Jerusalem, which promises to be of very considerable interest. Archdeacon Farrar, who made a special study of the Holy Land preparatory to writing his great work, " The Life of Christ," will deliver lectures to the pil- grims at Jerusalem and other centres. The pilgrims will visit Rome on their outward journey, and will return by Athens, paying a visit to Egypt and the Pyramids in the I course of the pilgrimage. They will thus cover all the most important centres of Scripture history. This pro- gramme will enable them to see something of the develop- ment of Christianity under varied conditions. They will in tura visit places where the dominant influence is exerted by the Roman, the Coptic, and the Greek churches, and it will be difficult to return from such a tour without having gained an increased spirit of Catho- licity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>SCOTCH LABOUR rARTY.</head>
        <p>THE SCOTCH LABOUR rARTY.</p>
        <p>The annual Conference of the Scottish Labour party was held on Tuesday in Glasgow, and was attended by about 160 delegates, ineluding several ladies. The Rev. Alexander Webster (Kilmarnock) presided.—The secre- tary, Mr. Keir Hardie, m.r., read the report for the past year. It stated that 1892 had been a notable year in many respects. The labour movement had made grati- fying progress. After referring to the labour candidates in various parts of Scotland, the report stated that a serious difficulty for a time was the finer', ial one, but, thanks to the untiring efforts of the members and the aid of sympathisers,that was overcome, and for the first time they met with a balance on the right side of the ledger. (Applause.) Constituencies, Mr. Hardie said, where labour candidates were to be run at the next election should begin to prepare for the contest which might come soon, though his impression was that the Government would last two sessions at least. Still it was best to be in readiness. The report was unanimously adopted. A resolution was adopted as follows That the Scottish Labour party be called the Independ- ent Labour party (Scottish section), federating—while preserving the full control over the internal arrangements of our own organisation—on an equal representative footing with the English Labour party. Other resolutions carried were— (l) That the future working of the organisation be by National Council ; (2) that it is necessary that there should be an increase in the number of factory inspec- tors, and that competent working women should be appointed ; and (3) that the party should approve of the resolution adopted by the Trades' Congress in favour of the nationalisation of minerals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>ABDUCTION OF ENGLISH GIRLS TO THE CONTINENT.</head>
        <p>ABDUCTION OF ENGLISH GIRLS TO THE CONTINENT.</p>
        <p>EXTRAORDINARY CASE AT HULL. Some facts of an extraordinary character connected with the illegal abduction of two young women from Hull to Hamburg are,says a Hull correspondent,occupy- big the attention of the German authorities at Hamburg as well as the Home Office authorities in England. It appears that about a fortnight ago a young woman from Leith, who was on a visit to her sister at Hull, was, in company with that sister, walking along one of the dock quays at Hull near the spot where a Hamburg steamer trading weekly to Hull was lying, when they were ac- costed by two men, one of whom, it afterwards trans- pired, occupied a subordinate position on board the ' versel in question, and the other a ship's mate who was going to Germany in the same steamer by the next tide. The young women, in reply to a question, said they would like to see the interior of the vessel, and went on board with the men. Having been shown round they were invited to have something to drink. They con- sented, and it is alleged that the drink they accepted must have been drugged,for they at once lost conscious- ness, and when they recovered they found they were at sea. Terribly alarmed at the position in which they found themselves, they appealed to the captain, who, however, did not then put back and the involuntary passengers were informed that their passage had been paid. There was nothing for it but going to Hamburg, whither the vessel was bound,but on arriving there they asked for the address of someone who would give them shelter, and they were directed by the man who had first spoken to them to a house in Erika Strasse, a street which has the reputation of being ane of the lowest in the city. The women were taken to the house in ques- tion, when, to their further horror, they found it was one of those infamous dens which are peculiar to great continental towns. An old woman was in charge, and to her they appealed to be allowed to see the British Consul, and no doubt apprehensive of the consequences should the women be compelled to remain, their wishes were complied with. The British Consul was accordingly placed in possession of the facts of the case, and the officials saw that the English worsen were put beyond the reach of further danger, while the German police were sent on the track of the persons who had been guilty of the abduction. The latter were arrested, and are now awaiting trial at Hamburg for violating the German law by importing two English girls into that city. The old woman in charge of the house, on being asked by the German officials if she knew the man be- longing the steamer who had sent the young women, replied that she did, and that he had " brought her plenty of pretty English girls." The young women had been sent back home to Hull by the British Consul on board the Wilson liner Panther. Particulars of the case have been forwarded to the Home Office, and it is under- ' stood that an endeavour will be made by the English authorities to make an example of those who have been guilty of so atrocious a crime.</p>
        <p>It is reported that the success in breeding lions which has long been a marked feature of the Dublin Zoological Gardens, is still maintained. The lioness " Queen," 1344ught in 1865, has had, siace then, nine litters, num- bering twenty-eight cubs, of which only lne died. Twenty-five were sold for an alt £l,OOO. and one male " Romeo," and one female are still in the gardens. COAOULINE.—Cement for Broken Akrtfelee, E.t. and le. ; postage, 2d. Sold everywhere, home and abroad.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>MINING ACCIDENT 1N RUSSIA.</head>
        <p>MINING ACCIDENT 1N RUSSIA.</p>
        <p>EIGHT MEN IMPRISONED FOR TEN DAYS. ST. PIITURSBMIG, WEDNESDAT.—At Jurjivka, on the Donets Railway, a mine recently became flooded by a sudden inrush of water, and of eleven men who were in the workings at the time only three succeeded in escap- ing before all communication with the surface was cut off. It took ten days to pump the water out, and all hope of finding the remaining eight men alive had been abandoned. When the rescue party entered the mine on the tenth day, however, they found all the men still living, though terribly exhausted, as they had had nothing to eat during the whole of their imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>HOW JAPS PULL TEETH.</head>
        <p>HOW JAPS PULL TEETH.</p>
        <p>Japanese dentistry is a thing to marvel at. A gentle- man, who was on a visit to Mr. Hubbard, the American Minister to Japan, sends the following interesting narra- tive :—" One day I was troubled with the toothache, and Mr. Hubbard took me to a dentist, and explained to the saddle-coloured operator that I wanted the grinder extracted. I was placed in a bamboo chair, and tilted slightly back. The dentist examined my teeth, talking meanwhile to Uncle Sam's representative. Suddenly his thumb and forefinger closed on the troublesome tooth, and before I had the faintest idea of what was going to happen he lifted it eut and held it up before me, smiling at the same time that vacant smile peculiar to the children of the Orient. "You were waiting for the forceps were you ?" said Minister Hubbard with a laugh. They dent use 'em here. Look at this. Here is a young Jap taking his first lesson in dentistry. A twelve-year-old Japanese boy sat on the floor, having before him a board, in which were a number of holes into which pegs had been had been tightly driven. He was attempting to extract the pegs with his thumb and forefinger, Mr. Hubbard explained that as the strength of the natural pair if forceps developed by pectic° the pegs would be driven in tighter. After a couple of years at peg pulling the young dentist would graduate, and be able to lift the most refectory molar in the same manner that he now lifted wooden pegs."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>MRS. MAYB RICK'S ILLNESS.</head>
        <p>MRS. MAYB RICK'S ILLNESS.</p>
        <p>ASTOUNDING STATEMENT.</p>
        <p>The" St. James's Gazette" says :—We have autho- rity for stating that the recent alleged illness of Mrs. Maybrick, which led to an attempted agitation for her release, is now definitely ascertained to have been caused by her own acts. The prison authorities decline to give any informalion on the subject ; but the facts, we believe, are that prisoner contrived by some means to secrete some needles, and either by pricking herself with these, or swallowing them, she managed to produce effusion of blood and other apparent symptoms of the most serious disease of the lungs. In consequence, it will be recollected, a clamour was raised that Mrs. May- brick was dying and ought to be set at liberty. The authorities, however, suspected from the first the bona fides of the patient, and by watching her they succeeded in discovering the true exciting cause of the alleged illness, which rapidly disappeared when effective mea- sures were taken to keep the prisoner from injuring herself. The Exchange Telegraph Company states that Mrs. Maybrick's health is improving, although somewhat slowly, as her condition changes from day to day. Baroness de Rogues paid a visit to her daughter in the prison infirmary on Friday, but the visit was restricted to thirty minutes, the same as is allowed in the case of other convicts. The prison officials are most reticent respecting the cause of Mrs. Maybrick's recent alarming illness, but the Exchange Telegraph Company confirms the above statement that it was caused by her own hand, though whether the act was committed owing to a suicidal tendency or not is at present unknown.</p>
        <p>The " St. James's Gazette" has received the following letter:—'' Sir,—As solicitor for the Baroness de Rogues, I am instructed to at once contradict the statement pub- lished in your issue of Saturday last as to the cause of Mrs. Maybrick's illness. Such statement is utterly un- true and unwarranted.—Yours obediently, ST. BERNARD WiLsoN, 29, Bow-st., W.C., January 2." The "St. James's Gazette" remarks upon this letter :—With refer- ence to the above letter we repeat that we believe our information as to Mrs. Maybrick's deliberate self- infliction uf injuries tending to simulate some mortal disease to be perfectly correct. We believe it to be "true," and, if not yet " warranted " by any official statement, it is at any rate uncentradicted at the Home Office. We have had repeated confirmations from the best possible quarters of our account of Mrs. Maybrick's malingering. It has, we understand, been a great cause of anxiety and worry to Mr. Asquith. And until we receive a contradiction from the Home Office, we invite the public to rely on our statement, in spite of the denial of the Baroness de Rogues and her solicitor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>FATAL CAROUSAL AT GOLBORNE. EIGHTEEN GLASSES IN ONE EVENING.</head>
        <p>THE FATAL CAROUSAL AT GOLBORNE. EIGHTEEN GLASSES IN ONE EVENING.</p>
        <p>"ALCOHOLIC POISONING."</p>
        <p>SERIOUS ALLEGATIONS AGAINST A PUBLICAN. Mr. Jones deputy county coroner, held an inquest at tke New Inn. Golborne, on Wednesday, respect- ing the death of James Connolly, a single man, 19 years of age, which took place under shocking cir- cumstances at his lodgings, Queen-st., Gol borne, early on Monday morning. The evidence showed that deceased got up about noon on New Year's Day, and had two glasses of rum in his lodgings. About seven o'clock deceased and some companions visited the Prince of Wales Hotel, where Connolly had two pints of beer and one glass of whiskey, the latter as a New Year's gift from the landlord. They adjourned to the Charles Napier Hotel, where they had a pint of ale each and a glass given by the landlord. At half-past eight they left for the Railway Hotel.—Thomas Loyden, with whom deceased lodged, said he paid for the man a glass of rum, the remainder having beer. Deceased asked the landlord for their New Year's glass, which the barmaid supplied in the landlord's absence. Subsequently the landlord gave him another one, but at first refused owing to the deceased being so " cheeky" as to ask for it. Deceased had more drink which was paid for by himself and the company.—The Coroner : How much did deceased have altogether ?—.Witness : Nine or ten glasses.—The Coroner : What ! In the hotel P—Witness : Yes.—The Coroner : What did they consist of P—Wit- ness : Nine glasses of rum and one of sherry. About twenty minutes to ten deceased fell down on the bar floor, and was helpless. Witness obtained assistance, and carried the man home.—The Coroner: When he fell in the house did the landlord see him P—Witness : Yes. L —The Coroner : What condition was deceased in ; very drunk P—Witness : He wouldn't have fallen if he hadn't been drunk ; nobody touched him. Witness (continu- ing) said when they got deceased home, Dr. Part was • sent for, awl remained with him until twenty minutes ' to two in the morning, when he died. Deceased had I had some drink on Saturday night, but came home • sober, and was not in the habit of going on the spree.— By Inspector Jump : He had ne doubt deceased was drunk when he fell in the Railway Hotel.—Questioned as I to the quantity of drink deceased had in the Railway Hotel, witness said he could not say who paid for it all, but he held to his original statement that deceased had " about ten glasses altogether, and drank five or six • within a few minutes." Deceased never spoke after leaving the house.—By Inspector Jump : The landlord spoke to deceased about ten adnutes before he fell down, and witness considered h• was then very far gone in ' drink. Part of the drink was served by the landlord.— I The Coroner: Did you know that he was a young man just over from Ireland.—Witness : Yes.— The Coroner: I don't know whether you have reckoned it, but deceased seems to have had that night three pints of beer, a glass of beer, a glass of whiskey, a glass of sherry, and nine glasses of rum—altogether eighteen glasses of different kinds of drink. Did you do anything to restrain him from taking so much drink ?—Witness: No; there were plenty of other men in the room that I thought were more experienced than me.—The Coroner: What age are you P—Witness : Thirty-nine next Aurrust. —The Coroner And didn't you say anythino• to 'him about taking so much ?—Witness : No, I dienot, nor nobody else did.—By a Juryman: He was sure they were sober when they entered the Railway Hotel, and that deceased had nine glasses of rum while there; he drank five or six in a very short time. The landlord kept tell- ing him to" sup up and have another one."—John Parr, landlord of the Railway Hotel, who informed the coroner that he wished to make a statement, denied that the deceased had as much drink as was stated by the wit- ness. As far as he could make out, deceased was only served with five glasses of rum, besides one glass he (witness) gave him. He spoke to deceased a few minutes before he was taken home, when he appeared all right.---By Inspector Jump He showed signs of hav- ing drink, but not of being drunk.—By a Juryman: If the man had asked for another glass, he would have supplied him.—After consulting in private, the Foreman announced that the jury were of opinion that deceased met his death by alcoholic poisoning, adding that they exonerated the landlord from all blame.—The Coroner You must leave that matter for inquiry elsewhere. The cause of death is all we have to do witla.—The inquiry then terminated.</p>
