      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>ALLEGED EMBEZZLEMENT BY A ST. HELENS RAILWAY OFFICAL.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED EMBEZZLEMENT BY A ST. HELENS RAILWAY OFFICAL.</p>
        <p>SURRENDER OF MR. JAMES.</p>
        <p>„At three o'clock on Monday afternoon Mr. Fred. Edward James, late chief booking clerk at the St. Helens station of the London and North-Western Railway Company, surrendered to the St. Helens police, a wvraut against him for alleged embezzle- ment on December, Ist, of £3O 10s., the money of the company, having been issued. Mr. James was at busi- ness on Friday night, December 2nd, but his sudden disappearance and other circumstances led the railway company to commence an investigation into his accounts. the following day, with the result stated. Mr. James,. in his flight, only succeeded, it is believed, in getting to. the Isle of Wight. For some days it has been known thet he was desirous of giving himself up to justice, and for that purpose he quickly made his way home. He remained there some little time, and on Monday a cab. conveyed him from Chapel-st., to the offices of Messrs. J. 0. Swift and Co., who have been instructed for the defence, and after consultation he went to the police office, where he is detained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>PRISONER BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>PRISONER BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens Police Court on Tuesday, Edward James was placed in the dock charged with having embezzled £3O 10s., on the Ist December, 1892, the moneys of his employers. There were on the bench Alderman James M'Bryde and Mr. W. J. Thomson. Some delay occurred owing to the late appearance of Mr. Fenna, solicitor for the Railway Company, and upon his appearance in court James was called up the steps into the dock. He looked very pale. He was represented by Mr. Swift, of St. Helens. Mr. Fenna commenced his statement by saying the charge was a most serious one. It was that of embezzl- ing £3O 103., the moneys of the Company. James had been in their service as parcel agent and chief booking clerk. The period of time over which the defalcations extended would probably embrace a period of four or five months,certainly from July to the end of November. Some time would, therefore, be required to go into the accounts. fle (Mr. Fauns.) had not seen the report of the auditor who had examined the account—that had been sent to London—and the matter was being con- sidered by the directors. He was not ins position to go on with the case that day, and he therefore proposed simply to offer sufficient evidence to justify a remand. There was evidence that the whole of the deficiencies amounted to £22 Os. 9d. There were very many items, which would require fully a week to go into. Mr. John Scrymgeour was then called. He said he was an audit inspector in the service of the Company. From instructions he received on the 3rd December he proceeded to investigate the accounts of the prisoner. From the book he produced in court he was able to say that James had received £92 Os. 6i., and his siguature for that sum was in that book. Witness had also the transmit note to the cashier at Liverpool, amounting to £6l 10s. 6d., leaving a sum of £3O 10s. not accounted for to the Company. Mr. Swift : I suppose you know that prisoner ab- sconded ?—Yes. And his when &amp;bouts were not known P—l don't know that. I believe he wrote to Mr. Shaw admitting the full amount of defalcations, and saying that he would sur- render ?—I don't know. Have_ you not seen the letter ?—No.</p>
        <p>Mr. S,.'vift said when prisoner -found himself in diffi- culties he left the neighbourhood, and although search was made for him his whereabouts could not be dis- covered. James, however, wrote to Mr. Shaw saying he would give himself up, and he had kept his promise. He had no intention of going away again. He there- fore asked for 3_ reasonable bail.</p>
        <p>Mr. Fenn&amp; said their Worships must bear in mini the fact that he did go away, and although he had returned he might go away again. He was now in a contrite condition, but he might not be in a week's time, and he thought it rather risky to grant bail. The prisoner was remanded till Wednesday next, and it was decided to grant bail, prisoner in £2OO, and two sureties of £lOO each.</p>
