      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SATUYDAY, JANUARY 7, 1893.</p>
        <p>THE inhabitants of these islands Lessons from are, as a rule, not inclined to admit Abroad. that they have much to learn from foreigners ; and, above all, we have been very Much in the habit of pluming ourselves upon the superiority of our Factory Laws and the effective Municipal administration of our towns and cities. Yet it seems that both in regard to Factory legislation and municipal administration some of the despised foreigners have set us an example Which it would be well worth our while to imitate. On the first day of this year the amended Factory and Workshop Act came into force, and it raised the age at which "half-timers" could be employed M factories to eleven. Even now, we are still behind such countries as Austria, Germany, Benmark, Switzerland, France, and Spain, In which no child is permitted to labour under</p>
        <p>twelve years of age, and, in some cases, the limit is higher still. The new English arrangements 'as to " half-timers" will, it is said, in Lancashire affect 9,85-3 children between the ages of ten and eleven ; in Yorkshire there were last year 5,086 81-ICh children ; and in Cheshire 882. There is still much room for progress, and we are told by a German newspaper that if the latest ordinances of the German Labour Office as to the minimum age for Work were in force in England more than Beventy-six thousand children would be thrown (I'4 of work in the textile industries alone, for this is the number of children under thirteen Years of age who are sent to work here. This newspaper adds, what is, unfortunately, only too true, that "England has in this fact every ground for modifying her self-confident tone in regard to labour questions." And then, with regard to municipal administra- tion it is again from. Ger ,nany that a useful lesson IS to be derived. Mr. JAMES POLLARD, who is</p>
        <p>"chairman of the Public Health Committee of Zdinburgh, and Secretary of the Chamber of Com- Merce in that city, has been making an exhaus• tive invest;gation into the methods of the Berlin Corporation, and he has published the results in a little book entitled " A study in Municipal 'Government." Twenty years ago Berlin was an extrewely insanitary town2and its death rate,both 1:4 children and adults, was increasing at an ex- tremely rapid rate. Its redemption from this state</p>
        <p>'of things was largely due to the enlightened and Public spirited efforts of one man—the world- 'amed pathologist, Pr ofessor VIRCHOW. Although fatuous in science, and also a member of more important bodies such as the Landtag "d the Reichstag, Professor Vißcuow 'lid not think it any derogation from his Clignity to serve as a town councillor in the city in which he resided. He urged the need of sanitary teform, and the Berlin Corporation was wise enough to heed his warnings and to act upon his "vice. The result of this is that Berlin now seems to be almost a model city from a sanitary Point of view, and it enjoyed one of the benefits Which always follow upon an enlightened sanitary • Policy when, last year, it had almost a complete iramunity from cholera, although that scourge was taging at its vet), door. The streets and other Parts of the city are kept clean and sweet by Iteans of an admirable drainage system, and the tefuse of the city is used for fertilising a sountry tract of something like twenty-two square miles. The Corporation of Berlin, we sz'o told, wage "constant and successful 17ar against dirt and material pollution amoag Nople and dwellings in any form in which thtse evils menace the general health of the com- baunity." If people are incorrigibly dirty they are ,11111)13, turned out of their dwellings, but not to -` drift about the city in shelterless misery." What happens is described by Mr. POLLARD as follows Whenever these people are turned out—they are tisk 4ally, of course, among the very poorest—the 'tgeuse is cleansed and purified, and the inmates are Laken to Shelters, situated in different quarters of 'fie city, and under the charge of the Poor Depart- Silent. In these Shelters they and their clothes 4.1111. e scrubbed and made clean. The workers among "eta are allowed to go out to their daily avoca- ti°os, and daily they are made to cleanse them- ;elves. Food is provided for them and their "lilies at moderate cost,which is paid out of their 6arolngB. If they are out of employment they are Dot to some simple work within the Shelter, and 4112 s Paid for the work they perform. Persistent st'reitard of the rules of the Shelter is 'reated as a police offence. After a few weeks' tßsidence the people find themselves actually in l/hc)slo degree out of sympathy with uncleanly O:bits. *They are then allowed once more to 13take themselves to a home of their own, all the tter for the lessons they have learned. This Iteattnent is repeated where needful. But, when beoome incorrigibly dirty, their offence ~,e.e°4les a matter for the interference of the "t:lliTui nal authorities. They are then drafted off to 'sewage fields or some other department of PUblic Work, • where their earnings are used, in the " m.tance, to defray the cost of their own keep,</p>
        <p>I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>_Aii6l.._ _ 1</head>
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="4" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <p>the balance being applied in the next place to the maintenance of their families, who pass under the care of the authorities." Such being the salutory municipal policy practised in Berlin, there is no need to be surprised when we are told that there are no slums there such as disgrace many English towns.</p>
        <p>The proposal for the abolition of the pleasure fairs held in Warrington which the Markets Com- mittee submitted to the Town Council on Tuesday, was carried by a large majority. The fairs were roundly condemned and there was not much said in their defence. But, after all, the proposal was one of which much might be said on both sides. There were some features or incidents of the fair in defence of which nothing could be said. The noises of the fair called for cons iderable modera- tion in the interests of the people who lived within a pretty extensive circuit of the ground on which the booths and caravans we re pitched. Steam-driven " musical " instruments are modern innovations which can well be dispensed with, and the same may be raid of all the loud-toned devics introduced for the sake of attracting attention, by enterprising showmen. That the half-yearly carnival was, however, in reality as well as in name a " pleasure " fair to many people is proved by the fact that it needs the interference of the Town Council to put an end to it. If it had not been supported by townspeople it would have died a natural and unlamented death. Unfortu- nately, there are in Warrington as in other towns, a by no means inconsiderable proportion of people who can only, to a very limited extent, chose their pleasure. The amusements that they can afford to indulge in must be near at hand and must be cheap. That the fair broke the monotony of some lives and gave in some cases a colour to what would otherwise be a very colourless existence there can be little doubt. It would be a sound policy to require those who propose to abolish any source of public pleasure that is not absolutely vicious or harmful, to substitute for it something that is more suitable and not less accessible. •</p>
        <p>THE speech which the Duke of WESTMINSTER de- livered at a meeting of the the Cheshire Chamber of Agriculture on Saturday, should be of interest to students of sociology of a future generation, it is eminently calculated to throw a light upon the sort of "equality " that we are sometimes disposed to boast of as existing in these days. In that speech the Duke of WESTMINSTER congratu- lated the agricultural labourer of Cheshire upon , the fact that he was " comparatively, well off," because his " average wage" is about fifteen shillings a week! The Duke of WESTMINSTER is, by repute, the wealthiest man in England, and certainly he is one of the very wealthiest. Fifteen shillings a week amounts to a total of rather less than forty pound a year. The Duke of WEST- MINSTER has, undoubtedly, a much larger income than that every day in the year. Tot he congratu- lates the agricultural labourer of the county in which his own palatial residence of Eaton Hall is situated, on his earning by a week of hard toil the munificent sum of fifteen shillings. There is no reason to beliovo that the Duke was speaking ironicAly ; in fact, from the general tenour of the speech it might be reasonably inferred that he was, in a mild way, endeavouring to rejoice with the agricultural labourer in his great good fortune. Incongruities of this description are inevitable when people begin to talk of that of which they practically know nothing. The Duke of WEST- MINSTER has no clear conception of what living upon fifteen shillings a week means. Had he during any part of his career attempted for, say, six of the coldest months of the year, to maintain himself and family upon the sum named, and thrown long days of arduous toil into the bargain, we venture to think that ho would have- been liable to m;sconstrue the remarks of any benign millionaire who congratulated him upon the affluence of his circumstances.</p>
        <p>By the way, the, agricultural labourer is, just now, receiving much at at the hands of those who in times past have not over-estimated his importance. In his speech referred to above the Duke of WESTMINSTER moved that the basis of the Cheshire Chamber of Agriculture be ex- tended to comprise agricultural labourers at a</p>
        <p>"nominal subscription." The explanation of this is, of course, that the labourers have now got votes which will be an important factor in moulding future legislation. It will not matter how "nominal "his subscription may be so long as his vote goes in the desired direction. It is, however, satisfactory to note that the labourers seem to be thoroughly alive to the fact that they have grievances of their own to redress, and that those grievances are touch more likely to receive attention through the return to Parliament of such men'as Mr. JOSEPH Alton, than by throwing in their lot with those who think they are well off because they earn an average of fifteen shillings a week.</p>
        <p>IT must be conceded that the MAYOR displayed some dramatic talent by the manner in which he made public the Now Year's gift which has been made to Warrington. By permitting the announcement last week that such a gift had been made, while carefully withholding all the details, the MAYOR whetted, so to speak, the curiosity of the public, and the unusually large attendance of " outsiders " at the Town Council meeting on Tuesday evening was evidence of the interest that had been aroused. It is not always a discreet plan to xcit 3 anticipations. When that is done, realisation of ten falls below expectation ; and if people who have expected to see a mountain only find a molehill, there is apt to be a reaction in their feelings; and an undue amount of disparage- ment follows. On Tuesday evening, however, nothing of this kind happened. No doubt many of those present had been, like Councillor FLEM- ING, exercising their minds over the week end as to the amount and object of the benefaction, and they had, for the most part, probably been equally unsuccessful in their predictions. When it was announced that the, until then, un- known donor was Mr. J. CHARLTON PAaa,and that the donation amounted to the substantial sum of £5,000 for the purpose of providing a Public Hall for the town, it was felt by every one that the gift was one that was well worth acceptance by War- rington, and that Mr. PARR could not by any possibility have selected a more suitable object upon which to expend the money. The thanks which were expressed to Mr. PARR by the Mayor and other members of the Council will be re- echoed by all sections of the townspeople, for the benefaction has not been dictated by any sec- tional or sectarian spirit. As Councillor HENRY ROBERTS pointed out, its value is enhanced by the fact that, apart from the primary purpose to which it is to be devoted, the Corporation are not bound down, in the spending of the money, by any conditions orrestrictions. They will have a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>WIDNES IRISH NATIONAL LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>The members of this league have passed a resolution condemning the recent dynamite outrage in Dublin. If you wantyour watch repaired by skilful workmen, take it to PRITCHARD'S.—Advt.</p>
        <p>MOORE'S RUNCORN ALMANAC AND DIRNCTORY. This volume for the present year has just been pub- lished and contains a fund of useful and valuable infor- mation, besides a directory of the town.</p>
        <p>It you want a lucky wedding ring go to 51, Iltau-aT.,where you will get a present with it.—Advt.</p>
        <p>- - - RUNCORN'S PRIDE LODGE G,U.0.0. The first annual social tea was held in the Union Hall on Wednesday night, when about 100 members and their wives sat down to the repast provided. Dr. McDougall presided over the meeting, and delivered an interesting address. - - • - -•- • -</p>
        <p>Wlien in want of a chaste and elegant wedding or birthday preaent go to PRITCHARD'S, 51, High-St.—Advt. WESLEYAN SALE OF WORK AT WIDNES. • —• • •</p>
        <p>On Monday and Tuesday a sale of work and Christ- mas tree were held in Lacey-street school, in aid of the circuit funds. On the first day the proceedings were opened by Councillor F. Neil and on the second day by Councillor Davies. The sum of £l3O was realised. JOSEPH PRITCHARD, Watchmaker and Jeweller, 51, High- et.. and 4, Bridge st.. Runcorn.— Advt. ALLEGED ILLEGAL PAWNING AT WIDNES. On Thursday, at Widnes Petty Sessions, Catherine Sheridan, lodginghouse keeper, Moss Bank, was sum- moned by a lodger named Win. Budd,for illegally pawn- ing a wedding ring with with £1 is. The detendan- alleged that Rudd gave her a ring.—The Bench consid- ered the evidence inconclusive and dismissed the case. dosErn Pnrrciaann,Watehmaker and Jeweller, E, High- at.. and 4, Bridge-st., Buncorn.—Adrt. CHANGES AT RUNCORN RAILWAY STATION. . .</p>
        <p>.... In the course of a few days Mr. Robert S mith, sta- tion master, Runcorn, will he transferred to Lowton, near Newton-le-Willows, and he will be succeeded by Mr. Rhodes, of Stafford. Mr. Smith has had a lifelong acquaintance with railway work, and during the eight years he has been at Runcorn, he has earned the good will of those with whom he has come in contact. The change is made on his own desire to have a quieter position. JOSIZPII PRITCHARD'S sale shop is 51,Hign-st., nis workshop 4, Bridge-st. —Advt. THE SEAMEN'S' INSTITUTE KUNCORN.. .. .</p>
        <p>The annual tea meeting was held on Tuesday night. About 150 persons were present. A capital entertain- ment was provided. Mr. John Moadowcroft presided, and in responding to a vote of thanks pointed out that there was a larger number of boats registered at Run- corn than in any town in the kingdom, and, referring to the Ship Canal, said he waslookin,g forward to a great future for Runcorn. On Wednesday the children attend- ing the Sunday Sehool had their annual Christmas tree, which had been laden with a vast array of good things by Mr. Walter Collier. Mr. Boxworth gave a magic lantern entertainment. JOSEPH PRITCHARD is prepared to wind. and keep in repair, private clOcks from 10s. per annum.—A4vt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>SUDDEN DEATH AT WESTON POINT.</head>
        <p>SUDDEN DEATH AT WESTON POINT.</p>
        <p>On Monday morning, about to o'clock, James Dillon aged about 65 years, labourer, died very suddenly. For some time he had worked in John McNulty's gang on the Ship Canal works, McNulty having known the deceased for about 20 years. He had always been a healthy man. Just before Christmas he suffered from a cold, which prevented him following his employment. At the hour above stated a fellow lodger, namvd Henry Dawson, N 0.5 hut, heard a shuffling noise in his bedroom and on looking round saw deceased trying to get out of bed. He could not stand. Dawson went to his assis‘- ance, but Dillon died in a few minutes. Mr. Worsley, coroner, deemed an inquest unnecessary. PRITCHARD'S have now stood the test for 50 years, an d quail ty and first-class workmanship are still guaranteed.—Advt. EXTRAORDINARY DEATH OF A PENSIONER AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>An army pensitner named Thos. Winn, who had lodged with Win. Stretch in rettketh-lane, came to an extraordinary end on Thursday morning. At 3.30 a.m. the deceased went to the Sprinch works, in a condition described as " fresh in drink" and asked the watchman, Wm. Deyland, to allow him to warm himself. He said he had been under Delph bridge and was nearly dead. Leyland allowed him to lie down before the fire, and about 7.30 he noticed that the man seemed very ill. John Frodsham, with- whom Winn had worked for some months, then arrived and asked him to get up. Winn replied "Ohl am dying." He was conducted to his lodgings and died at eight o'clock. Time deceased was a player in the Town Band.—Mr. J. E. Worsley held an inquest on the body of the deceased on Thursday, and the jury returned a verdict of" Natural causes." JOSEPH PRITCHARD keeps specttclei to suit all sights from a shilling a pair.—Advt. A BRUTAL HUSBAND AND A. FORGIVING WINN AT RUN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>CORN.</head>
        <p>CORN.</p>
        <p>..... Before Mr. J. Brundrit and Mr. J. Handley, at Run- corn Police Court on Tuesday, George Hignett, labourer, Mill Brow, was charged with committing an aggravated assault on his wile Mary Ann Ilignett.—lnspector Cooper stated that about a quarter to ten on the previous night Mrs. flignett was walking along Church-st., when the prisoner rushed up and struck her a violent blow in the face with his fist, knocking her into the middle of the road. The prisoner then ran at her again, and kicked her several times on the upper part of the legs. The prisoner was wearing clogs at the time. —lnspector Cooper took the man into custody, and he informed the magistrates that he had never seen a more brutal and cowardly assault.—The wife was called, and she gave the prisoner a very good character. He had never treated her in such a manner before and they had been married for 26 years. Mr. Brundrit said there was no doubt that the prisoner had committed a very savage assault, and he must pay a fine of 20s. and costs or go to gaol for a month's hard labour. Mrs. Hignett paid the money. JOSEPH PRITCHARD has the best stock of ladies' silver and gold watches in the town.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>RuNcoRN SONS OW TEMPERANCES.</head>
        <p>RuNcoRN SONS OW TEMPERANCES.</p>
        <p>The half yearly meeting of the above-named societvwas held on Saturday evening, under the presidency of Mr. Thomas Heesom. Mr. W. B. Hamilton, of Chester, the presiding officer of the Cheshire district, gave a very in- teresting address in which he referred to the state of the order in the district and the duties of the members as temperance workers. A vote of thanks was accorded Mr. Hamilton for his address.—The election of officers then took place with the following result.—Bros. Thos. J. Holt, Patriarch; James Johnson, associate; David Smith and Thomas Shaw, conductors ; E. Hobson and George E. Sutton, sentinels ; Thomas Cooke and Wm. Cooke, financial scribes; Edward Key, recording scribe; David Bisbrown, treasurer ; S. M. Timperley, chaplain ; Jamet Johnson, sick visitor; Thos. Heesom, iohn Jones, and L. M. Timperley, trustees; James Bisbrown, and L. M. Timperley, auditors. The following members were elected as representatives to the Grand Divisiom to be held at Crewe this mouth :—Messrs. Thos. Heesom,Thos. J. Holt, James Bisbrown, L. M.Timperley, John Taylor, and James Johnson.</p>
        <p>Go IO PRITCHARD'S for the best live guinea watch a work- ingman can have.—Advt. NORTHWICH PARLIAMENTARY DIVISION RS-ARRANGEMENT</p>
        <p>OF AREAS. On Wednesday the boundaries commission appointed by Cheshire County Council sat at the Magistrates' Courtroom, Northwich, to revise the polling districts of Northwich Parliamentary Division, and also the polling districts for County Council elections. The commis- sioners present were Colonel France Hayhurst, J.P., (in the chair), and County Alderman Christopher Kay. The political agents in attendance were Mr. W. Hanley, for the Liberals ; and Mr. C. J. Hughes, for the Conserva- tives.—The Chairman Raid the agents knew more of the details (,f the districts than perhaps anyone else, and he asked them if they had considered the subject or come to any understanding.—Mr. Handley said he had had a conference with Mr. Hughes, and they had unitedly arrived at the recommendations to be placed before the commission .The recommendations submitted showed the following alterations in Runcorn, namely, that Clifton and Weston should be detached from Runcorn South and form a separate polling district to be called by the name of Weston, in which township the polling place should be. The electorate would be 394. Consequent on this alteration, it was suggested that Runcorn South should c.,nsist of all that portion of Runcorn on the south of the Bridgewater Canal, which would mean the transfer of 24 streets from Runcorn North. The elec- torate of Runcorn South would be 1,353, and Runcorn North would be reduced by 451 to 2,124. Both PRITCHARD'S Shops are under personal supersisien.and customers may rely upon recelving,every attention.—Aldot.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <p>nil E EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 7. 1893.</p>
        <p>THE WRATHE R.</p>
        <p>The frost has continued during the present week with unabated severity, and the consequences to the working classes are becoming very serious. Work is particularly at a standstill on the Ship Canal. Traffic seas completely stopped on the Bridgewater Canal on Thursday up to which time a passage had with oifficulty been kept open by means of ice boats,and nearly all outside workers are idle. It is feared that if there is not quickly more open weather, considerable distress will prevail. To-day a soup kitchen will be opened at the Seamen's Institute. The Navvies Mission in Irwell-lane has been thrown open for reading and recreation, and there is much private , charity being bestowed in deserving cases. No doubt the soup kitchen at the Town Hall, which did such good I service twelve months ago, will be opened, should the adverse circumstances continue. FANCY BALL AT WINWICN.. A ball took place in St. Oswald's Schools, on last evening week, when about 35 couples were present. Dancing commenced at 8 30 p.m., and was kept up with spirit until the small hours of the morning. Mr. Peter Longton made an efficient M.C., and the refreshments were supplied by Messrs. T. Edging- ton and J. McLaren. REMARKABLE EXPLOSION AT ST. HELENS..</p>
        <p>A singular explosion occurred on Tiesday night at 30, Talbot-st., St. Helens, tenanted by Mr. John Holt, glass stainer, and three persons, Sarah Ellen Harrison, and her child,Albert Harrison,aged eight- teen months, and Martha Holt, aged ten years,were scalded. It appears that about three o'clock Mr. 1-bit's daughter put an earthenware foot-warmer, containing about three quarts of water,in the oven to warm, without unscrewing the tap. Two hours later, when Mrs. Harrison, with her child on her knee, and Martha Holt were sitting near the oven, the warmer exploded with a loud report. The oven front was blown out, the door and shelf being smashed, and the water flew out over the persons named. Mrs. Harrison was scalded on the face, neck and hands, Martha Holt was scalded on the neck, and the child who was the worst injured, was scalded on the head and hack. Albert Harrison afterwards.succumbed to his injuries.</p>
        <p>CARPETS and LINOLNUMS made and filted free of oharge DURING SALE.—J. and W. DUTTON, Sankey-at. —Advt.</p>
        <p>MUSICIANS IN CONFERENCE.—LECTURE BY Da. DILES.</p>
        <p>The members of the Incorporated Society of Musicians were again busy on Wednesday in con- nection with their conference which is being held in London. Important lectures were delivered by Dr, A. C. Mackenzie; Dr, Hiles, of Manchester ; and Mr. Dolmetesch. Of these Dr. Hiles' paper was by far the most interesting and valuable to students It was entitled "Form ; some suggestions," and was an earnest plea to young musicians to boldly tackle perhaps the most difficult problem they are called upon to solve in the course of their studies. 4)r. Hiles gave some very apt illustrations of his argu- ments, which were put forth very lucidly and cleverly. In fact, Dr. Hiles' paper is so far the most important contribution to the conference. " Old instruments" were again brought under notice by Mr. Dolmeteseh. Dr. Mackenzie restricted 'himself to some general remarks upon the art of music. The day's programme concluded with a most enjoyable" musical evening."</p>
        <p>proved, subject to the yard space being defined ; that the plaits of Mr. T. Reynolds fur the erect:on of twenty- six houses in the field opposite Greenway Meadows be rejected on the ground that the plans are not complete. and the yard space too small, and to ()noble him to pro- vide for improved aalipits and privies; that the plan of Mr. T. Q. Williams for the erection of two houses in Ivy- rOad be approved, subject to the ashpits, privies, and syphon being made to the Surveyor's satisfaction. The minutes were 'passed on the motion of Mr. Taylor.</p>
        <p>THE CANAL BOATS ACTS. The minutes of the Canal Boats Committee contained the annual report by the Secretary on the execution of the regulations of the Canal Boats Acts in the Runcorn district. The report stated that 20 boats had been re- gistered by the authority during the year, making a total of 942 registered at Runcorn. Seventeen contra- ventions of the Acts and regulations had been reported by the inspector ; 304 inspections of boats had been • made during the year, and the inspector reported that he had found 453 men, 136 women, and 155 children on board. The committee regret to find that the num- ber of women found on board tilt boats inspected had increased, and not decreased as had been the case pre- viously, and that the number of children had again in- crease4,—The report was approved.</p>
        <p>THE FREE LIBRARY COMMITTEE. This Committee recommended that the Library be opened for the issue of books every evening in the week., excepting Friday, from six o'clock to eight o'clock and also on each Wednesday and SAturday afternoon from two o'clock to four o'clock, and that the salary of the Librarian be increased £lO per annum, to cominemie January sth, 1893. The minute was passed on the motion of Mr. Grine, seconded by Mr. Bisbrown. At a later period in the proceedings Mr. Meadowcroft gave notice that at the next meeting of the Board he should move that the Library be open one hdur each day in the week instead of on Wednesday and Saturday only.</p>
        <p>THE ELECTRIC LIGET. Mr. Wynne Humphreys moved that it be an instruc- tion to the Canal Boats and General Purposes Commit- tee to enquire as to the probable cost and practicability of securing an installation of electric light for the public streets of Runcorn, and to report to the Board at the earliest opportunity.—Mr. Humphreys said the advis- ability of obtaining better light at a reasonable cost, must be patent to everybody, as must also the advan- tage of having the lighting of the public streets under their own control.—Mr. Hankinson seconded the motion, which was carried unanimously. THE SHIP CANAL AND RUNCORN SHIPPING TRADE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pritchard said some time ago he called the atten- tion of the Board to the fact that in certain cares vessels coming along the Ship Canal to Weston Point with cargoes for Runcorn, had been charged ship dues and canal tolls, whereas, vessels coming under similar con- ditions to Ellesmere Port, and Weston Point, for the Weaver trustees, had been exempt from tolls. The following letter from Mr. W. H. Collier, manager of the Ship Canal Company, was the outcome of an interview a committee of the Board had had with that gentleman. Mr. Collier in this communication said he had pleasure in confirming the communication which he had verbally made to the Commissioners, at their committee meeting with reference to ship dues, and canal tolls upon steam- ers bringing cargoes for Runcorn via the Ship Canal. during neap tides. The position which the Company's Runcorn agent had at all times taken in the matter was quite in accordance .with both the spirit and text of their act and of their arrangements with the various trading communities using Runcorn docks. It appear- ing, however, that in certain exceptional cases the rigid Interpretation of the conditions placed Runcorn docks at a disadvantage as compared with Weston Point, the Company would be willing that steamers coming by way of the Ship Canal on neap tides would not be required to pay ship dues, or canal tolls provided they were such as would be free if consigned to Weston Point; and pro- vided also that they caine direct by the Weston Marsh Lock and Weston Canal, to the Weston Point Dock. As explained by Itim (Mr. Collier this was as great a conces- sion, as the company felt itself in a position to make, tia in all other cases it would be only right that vessels and steamers should pay for privileges which were not available before the 'completed portion of the Ship Canal was constructed. Mr. Lightburn said another understanding bad been arrived at with the Ship Canal Company,' and that was that the ship's stores dealers an other traders should have every facility for putting their goods on board ship at Weston Point and Saltport free of charge, while Runcorn Docks were dosed. He had heard that charges were now being levied on those traders.—Mr. Smith : Was it a written agreement ?—Mr. Lightburn : It was a verbal arrange- ment.—Mr. Grice remembered the , arrangement, axd was sorryto hear there was any breach of it.—The Chair- man and Mr. Taylor said if the traders had any grievance and would lay it before the Highway Committee it would be investigated and dealt with.—Mr. Smith pointed out that Mr. Collier's letter only referred to steamers.—Mr. Pritchard said it was understood that the free use of the Ship Canal would be granted whether to a sailing vessel or a steamer.—Mr. Meadowcroft (who had only just entered the room) said sailing vessels would be treated quite as fairly as steamers.</p>
        <p>WONKME- N AND BTI-tEET LIGHTING.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lea said there were strong complaints amongst workingmen, of the want of light when they turned out in the early morning. He wished to know what means could be adopted to have the gas lamps left lighted longer in the dark mornings.—The Chairman said good care would be taken that the matter should be brought before the Gerieral Purposes Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>THINGS HEART) AND SEEN AT</head>
        <p>THINGS HEART) AND SEEN AT</p>
        <p>ItIINCOItN.</p>
        <p>BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT..</p>
        <p>The Vicar's account of the School Board cow- promise is a most extraordinary production, and it would be impossible to congratulate him upon his ( Sort as an amateur reporter. He certainly dis- plays some idea of "good copy," hut what a , bplendid opportunity lie has missed in not repro- ducing the scene between Mr. Brundrit and himself at that " upstairs meeting r Certain warlike sounds may have imed from "The Examiner," but the Canon has sent forth not a few calls to arms from the pulpit, only to find himself at the finish in the humiliating position of knocking at the door , of the "other group," and gladly agreeing to accept as his colleague a gentleman whom he has un- doubtedly tried to shelve by all the means in his power. /io person reading the Vicar's description would for a moment imagine such was the case. The whole aim and purpose of his reference to the School Board in his magazine, are intended to make' Dr. McDougall a" cribbed, cahined and confined' representative of Denominationalism, to make him a puppet in the hands of the chief of the parish.</p>
        <p>One would fain believe that the omission of Mr. Fred Norman's distinct announcement that Dr. McDougall reserved his perfect liberty of action on the Board was unintentional, one of those over- sights to which the fallible knights of the pen is liable, but the whole tenour of the article forbids such a conclusion. Time will show whether he has succeeded in converting Dr. McDougall into one of his henchmen, to rise and do his bidding whenever called upon. The phrenologist would make Dr. McDougalfs chart with "individuality very full," and he is not the man to lose himself in the persona- lity of another, however shrewd and calculating. Whatever being a church representative may mean to Canon Wood, Dr.McD,ougall will be found a rate- payer first and a churchman af^erwards when edu- cation is at stake,</p>
        <p>That was a good record of work accomplished that the Chairman of Runcorn School R)ard had to recount to his colleagues on Tuesday night. It is one of which any Board might well he proud, but one is sorry to think that, the Chairman has an idea that School Boards were merely invented for driving children to school, and that in giving the rising youth of the town the good things he en- numerated they had travelled beyond their apeci- fled duties. A little further research will reveal to him that the law gives them power to act as they have done, and provision is made for them under- taking the work. In Acts of Parliament, as in other things, it is the letter that kills, and a bene- factor and a true friend of the people is he who interprets the inner meaning of this dry, stilted, legal phaseology of a Parliamentary enactment, and shews forth the spirit, in elevating.progressive work. It is to be hoped that Mr. Pritchard and his colleagues will keep their hands to the plough, and promote free education, technical education, and continuation schools with still further admirable developments.</p>
        <p>It is surprising to find that Runcorn Board of Guardians continue to pay school fees for children. At the meeting of Runcorn School Board on Tues- day it was reported that the fees of 55 children had been remitted. As the Board Schools are made free these feesmust be paid from the poor rate to the denominational schools. It is altogether against the spirit of the Assisted Education Act that any inquiry should be made into the means of the parent of any child, as shewn by the recent utterances and actions of the President of Education, and here we have the Guardian payircg an inquiry officer a salary of .£5 a year,and also the fees of 55 children. Would it not be an advantage to the town to form a Free Education League, so that application for the right of free education could he made in pro- per form, and poor 'meats would escape the stigma ot pauperism, which is now so unjustly thrust upon them?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF COMMISSIONERS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF COMMISSIONERS.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting was held at the Town Hall on Thursday, Mr. F. Wiswall presided, and there were present Messrs. Fisher, Pritchard, Lightburn, Once, Coward, Abel, Smith, Lea, Bisbrown, Taylor, Hankinson, Humphreys, Meadowcroft, and Dr. Mangan with Messrs. F.A.Lake (law clerk), E. Mar- shall (secretary), and M. Barker (surveyor).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>DEATH RATE:</head>
        <p>THE DEATH RATE:</p>
        <p>_ The Medical Officer of Health reported that the number of deaths registered during the past month amounted to 42. Of these deaths 2 were inquest cases, and of the 40 remaining deaths 4 were due to mesenteric disease, 4 to natural decay, 6 to heart disease, 6 to bronchitis, 3 to phthisis, 3 to debility, 3 to convulsions, and one each to malignant typhus fever, small nox, erysipelas, diptheria, croup, paralysis, cancer, jaundice, pericarditis, kidney disease, and premature birth. The total deaths showed an average death rate for the twelve months of 25.2, and, exclusive of the inquest casas, of 24 per 1,000 of the estimated population.</p>
        <p>HIGHWAY AND BUILDINGS COMMITTEE. This Committee recommended—That the tender of Messrs. Wallis and Steevens, Basingstoke, to supply a new steam road roller for the sum Of £315 be accepted, subject to a three months' trial of the roller, and the payment not being required until six months after delivery in the event of the roller being found to be satisfactory ; the three months' trial to date from October 28th last.—That the steam road roller be hired out on the following terms :-355. per day to include driver, flagman, and oil. The hirer to provide coal and water, and to be responsible for all damage from what- soever cause done or occasioned to or by the steam roller whilst out.—That applications be invited for the situa- tion of driver of the steam road roller at a wage at the rate of 28s. a week; the man, when not driving the roller, to make himself useful in repairing roads and do other work under the direction of the Surveyor. Tnat the plan of Mr. George Marshall for the enlarge- ment of cottage. at Beaconsfield, Weston-road, be ap-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>OUTBREAK OF SMALLPDX AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>THE OUTBREAK OF SMALLPDX AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>THE HEALTH COMMITTEE VINDICATED,</p>
        <p>THE HOSPITAL 32ANAGEMEINT.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the Ittincorn Board of Improvement Commissioners on Thursday, after the ordinary business had been disposed of, .Mr. W. Hankinson asked the Chairman of the Health Com- mittee, whether he considered that with the equip- ment and general condition of the hospital he could have full confidence in recommending the removal there of cases of smallpox, and whether there were provided appliances to carry out the necessary conditions with expedition and efficiency.—Mr. Lightburn said he had great pleasure in giving as far as lie could the information sought, and he could not better do so than by referring to the past. He hoped the Press would give a full report of the facts so that the inhabit- ants of Runcorn and district would t;e ableto thoroughly understand the action which the Authority had taken in the matter. This was especially necessary as unreason- ing and misleading statements had appeared in the public prints. At ten o'clock on the evening of Novem- ber 30th, Mr. Percival informed him that Martha Hughes of Sewell-st., had been notified as suffering from smallpox, that Dr. MacLennan had advised her to ge to the hospital and she was willing to do so. He imme- diately authorised Percival to remove her, and although they had to send to Dutton Workhouse for the ambul- ance, the patient was in the hospital at half-past one on the morning of the first December. The bedding and clothing were destroyed by three o'clock the same morning. On the next day, December 2nd, two more : cases were notified as suffering from smallpox, in the j same house, and one from 47 in Sewell-A. These were removed to the hospital the same day, the beading and , clothing destroyed, and the houses 58 and 47 disinfected. A letter was read from Dr. MacLennan on December sth, in which he complained of the nursing arrange- ments of the hospital, and stated that he would not be responsible for the recovery of his patient without hav- ing a trained nurse. One out of the four patients was under hiscare, and three were under the care of Dr. Brunton, Dr. MacDougall's assistant. On the 19th December, notification was forwarded to the Board rom Dr. MacLennan, stating that Win. Hughes was suffer- ing from smallpox. On the morning of the 20th he (Mr. Lightburn) sent Percival to Dr. MacLennan with an offer to remove this man to the hospital, and to assure him that every care would be given to it. He also instructed Percival to warn Dr. MacLennan, in view of his letter of the fifth that his answer to the offer would be entered in his diary so that Dr. MacLennan could not be taken by surprise. As they would see, Dr. MacLennan refused on the ground stated in his letter of the sth December. The Committee feeling the importance of the matter, and after making inquiries wrote the letter dated December Bth, which would explain itself. On the 10th a further letter was sent to Dr. MacLennan. And so the matter ended. Wm. Hughes had since di,d at his own house. They would observe that Dr. MacLennan objected and refused to take the responsibility of the treatment of his patient at the hospital because there were no trained nurses there ; but he accepted 15 days later the responsibility of deal- ing with a patient in a small room of a small house without a trained nurse and without the beneficial sur- roundings and apparatus which could be found at the Hospital. He asked where did the consistency, come in.. Dr. MacLennan considered the nursing unsatisfactory, , Dr. Brunton, who was an exceptionally well qualified man, was perfectly satisfied with the nursing ; so that they have professional opinion opposed to professional opinion, and not merely professional opinion opposed to their own lay opinion. He should like that thoroughly understood. He did not say that the man Wne. Hughes would have recovered had he been removed to the Hos- pital but the fact remained that four patients who entered the hospital were well, and that the one who did not pass into the hospital was dead. By implication the Health Committee were charged with having little heart,and that the food supplied at the hospital was either Insufficient in quantity or deficient in quality. Now diet was an essential part of medical treatment, and it would be very improper for the Health Committee to interfere with the diet of a patient in the hospital. He made the broad statement that all the food ordered by the medical gentlemen attending the hospital had been promptly obtained, and that ot the best quality. (Hear hear.) There was one exception. Fresh haddock or turbot was ordered for one or more patients, fresh haddock could not be obtained, turbot was is. 7d. a lb., and they drew the line at turbot is. 7d., alb. and sub- stituted fresh codfish. That was the evidence of heart- lessness. The effort of the Committee by efficient and • prompt action to save Runcorn from the foul disease of</p>
        <p>smallpox had had its effect. The disease had ravaged the surrounding townships and the work had been one of unceasing anxiety to the Committee and if the Committee could not obtain help and sympathy it could at least demand fair play from those who professed to have the health and there- fore the happiness of Runcorn at heart. He asked the Secretary to read the communications as he had indi- cated them.—The Secretary then read the following cor- respondence :—" 107, Church-place, Runcorn, sth Dec., , 1892. To the Chairman of the Runcorn Health Commit- tee. Dear Sir,—As you are probably aware' there are at present four patients suffering from smallpox in the Isolation Hospital. Mrs. Rowles, the nurse, is laid up with a severe illness, and, in consequence, I find that two women, a mother and her daughter, are at present acting as nurses. Now, patients suffering from small-- pox require very special nursing and dieting, as well as continual attention in regard to external application, bathing, and other treatment. I understand that the ' present nurses have had no practical experience, and have previously never even seen a case of smallpox. In my opinion it was very unwise to permit these worsen to assume the duties of nurses when they were both ignorant of the work, and themselves unprotected by re-vaccination. In a threatened epidemic, such as we have at present to deal with, we cannot be too cautious. A couple of trained nurses could easily be obtained from the Nurses' Institution, Hope-st., Liverpool, at very short notice and without much extra expense. Mrs. Houlbrook and her daughter, who are very respectable persons,and whom I very highly recommend, could be placed in the caretaker's house to cook the nurses food and make them- selves generally useful. After the patients are well again the services of the trained nurses could, if necessary, then be dispensed with. I can only say, in conclusion, that unless satisfactory nursing be obtained I cannot any longer hold myself responsible for the treatment of the patients at present under my care in this hospital,as I am not assured that my instructions are properly car- ried out under the present system of administration .. I am, yours truly, A. MACLENNAN."—Town Ffall,Water- loo-road, Runcorn, Bth December, 1892. Dr. MacLennan. Dear Sir,—Your letter on the sth inst., relating to the administration of the hospital, was considei ed at a meeting of my Health Committee, held last evening. They have made inquiries with regard to the subject matter of your letter,and they are satisfied from reports submitted to them by their officers, that the arrange- ments made. as to nursing, diet, and attention are ade- quate, and they consider that as the patients are pro- gressino, favourably, there is no necessity to engage a trained 'nurse. The committee desired me to say that if you have any specific complaint to make, either as to diet or obedience of instructions, they will be prepared to consider the same.—Yours faithfully, E. MARSHALL. -</p>
        <p>_ At 6.30 p.m. December 20th. the Inspector of Nuis- ances asked Dr. McLennan " are you satisfied that the smallpox cases are sufficiently isolated."Dr.MacLennan's reply was taken down in writing as follows :—" I cannot see my way to advise the patient Hughes suffer- ing from smallpox to permit himself to be removed to the Runcorn Fever Hospital. It would certainly be safer and better in the public interest to have the patient isolated there if it were a suitable hospital according to the description given in section 124 of the Public Health Act, but in my opinion it is not a suitable place for the reception of smallpox patients, owing to the fact that there are no nurses there tc. ed and qualified to attend to that class of cases. me nursing arrangements be made more satisfactory, I shall use every endeavour to persuade the patient and his friends in the public inter- est to allow his removal. Under the present circum- stances he is now comfortable and gets nursed at least as well at home by his wife, who is willing to look after him."—The Secretary, on the following day, December 215 t forwarded the following communication to Dr. MacLennan :—" Yesterday you notified to our medical officer that a man named Hughes of 49, Sewell-street, was suffering from smallpox. Our Sanitary Inspector thereupon called on you and verbally offered to receive the man into our hospital. lam directed to repeat that offer in writing. This authority is prepared to receive the man into our Isolation Hospital and to see that the case receives every attention. Our Sanitary Inspector took down in writing at your request the conversation which took place at the interview referred to, and he has handed his notes to me. You therein admit that it would be safer and better that the case should be iso- lated at the hospital rather than in his own house. With regard to the nursing arrangements at the hospital. I may say that no other medical practitioner in Runcorn except you have objected thereto and we have reason to believe that they (the medical practitioners) are quite satisfied with them. You are undertaking a very grave responsibility in declining to advise the patient in ques- Con to go into the hospital. It is much to be feared that the means of isolation which are possible to be adopted If this case is treated out of hospital will prove to be in- effectual, and I am desired to say that if in consequence of his non-removal this dire disease should spread in the locality, you will be held wholly responsible.''—Mr. Hankinson said it was a very satisfactory explanation. —Mr. Smith pointed out that the Health Committee con- slating of two medical men, including the medical officer;two chemists, and six or seven business men of sound practical common sense. In this matter they had spared neither time nor money, and the patients of the hospital were well. He thought that the ratepayers when they saw both sides of the question,would rely upon the I work of their representatives before the mere statement of any " outside puffer."—Mr. Meadowcroft drew atten- tion to a statement made in a contemporary with regard to the sanitary condition of the town, which, he said, was well calculated to create a scare in the town, and snake people feel very uneasy. Asa matter of fact,they had only had five cases of smallpox in five months•— Mr. Smith said while they were dealing with this matter it was well that it should be thrashed Out. He was told, and he believed it was correct, that Canon Wood had bien making Some Verbal o..ompleinte to the officials of the Board with reference to the management of the hospital. When he was asked by the Chairman of the Health Committee to put his complaints in writing, he declined to do so. That he (Mr. Smith) considered a very unfair thing to do. In his report on the typhoid fever outbreak, the Medical Officer drew attention to the nuisance caused by an immense open ashpit and privy at one of the schools. He (Mr. Smith) ventured to suggest to the rev, gentleman to devote what spare time he had to bringing the privies of his schools into a more sanitary condition, and thus help the Health Committee to try and prevent this grave disease. It would be more in keeping with common sense than hamperieg the com- mittee with petty details of so-called mismanagement. —Mr. Lighburn said the matter had not been brought before the public, and the committee had intended to ignore a verbal statement of that kind. He should in- sist on having from Canon Wood or anyone else specific charges, and then they would have an opportunity of dealing with them in a practical manner. (Hear, hear.) The Health Committee was quite able to take care of itself, and they would not be dictated to by Canon Wood or Dr. MacLennan, or any other person, but they would give a respectful consideration to any suggestion they might make, and try to put right anything that might be wrong. (Hear, hear.)—Mr. Bisbrown said the medfbal officer Made a complaint respecting the national schools, and of course communication was made with the authorities there and the Board had had a reply that the suggested alterations would be carried out. He thought in justice to Canon Wood that that should be understood—Mr. Humphreys said in connection with , the question of smallpox and other diseases reflection had been made upon their existing byelaws. He wished it to be understood that the Committee which had the by-claws in hand were giving them serious consideration, but the revision of byelaws could not be accomplished ins day or a month. The Committee were making steady progress, and as chairman of the Committee he announced that he hoped before many weeks were passed to recommend the Board how to deal with the subject.—The discussion then ceased.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>CANON WOOD AND RUNCORN SCHOOL</head>
        <p>CANON WOOD AND RUNCORN SCHOOL</p>
        <p>BOAlt D ELECTION.</p>
        <p>In this month's " Parish Magazine," the Rev. Canon Maitland Wood, vicar of _Runcorn, thus describes in is letter to the parishioners the recent proceedings in connection with the triennial elec- tion of Runcorn School Board :—Most happily a School Board contest has been averted by a com- promise arrived at at the last moment It must be i said to the credit of the party which usually is in opposition to the Church candidates, that they very sensibly, notwithstanding certain warlike sounds which issued from the " Runcorn Examiner," de- cided to leave the old Board to go on with its work, so far as they were concerned. The difficulty arose amongst ourselves, and in this way. It will be recollected that in the election of 1889 Dr. McDougall fought as a Church candidate by the side of Mr. Salkeld and myself after baying been chosen as their champion by parish- ioners of Trinity Church. But when in anticipation of a conflict this year, I called to ask him whether he would stand again he replied that he would sit if there were no i contest, but that he would not promise to stand f there were a contest. I made a second call accompanied y Mr. Imison. but obtained no other answer. Dr. Mc Dougall, however, added that he should have himself nominated, and that we had better find others to be nominated, so as in any case to be sure of candidates. In accordance with this understanding other gentlemen were nominated. When all the nominations were known I called a meeting of Churchmen inviting those who had promised, if necessary, to work for the church candidates. The result of this meeting was a resolution arrived at almost unanimously that Mr. Imison and my- self should be the Parish Church candidates, and that if the Trinity people would meet the following day and send word of their decision as to their own caniddate, to me, at the Town Hall meeting on Thursday, the 22nd, all would be pledged to support that candidate. When ' I reached the Town Hall the following evening a note was put into my hand to the effect that the Trinity people could arrive at no decision. This placed me in the awkward position of having nothing to say at the Town Hall meeting. A small gathering of those belong- ing to Trinity was held under the chairmanship of Mr. Meadowcroft, with the result only that Mr. Charles Timmins remained a candidate, evidently with the good wishes of many, but without any formal acceptance by the Trinity Congregation—whilst Dr. McDougall held</p>
        <p>aloof from them altogether. After this the Town Hall meeting brake up, and two groups were formed, each to make ready for a conflict. It was then at the sugges- tion of Mr. Fred Norman, that I proposed to those with me, that if Dr. McDougall, who with Mr. John Brundrit, his nominator, had gone into consultation with the other group, would as frankly declare himself a church candidate before both groups together, as he had done the night before at the meeting of Churchmen, so that no possible misunderstanding as to his position would remain on either side—that in that case we should ask Mr. Charles Timmins to withdraw. This Mr. Timmins most generously agreed to, and nego- tiations were resumed on this understanding. Ulti- mately, not to weary you, after a good deal of consulta- tion, it was declared by Mr. Fred. Norman, who was chairman of the upstairs meeting, that a compromise could be effected on the understanding that Dr. McDougall should take his seat distirrttly as a member of the Church Party, if Mr. Charles Timmins were with- drawn. Mr. Timmins was then asked by me, before all present, whether, Dr. McDougall going in as a Church candidate, he was willing to withdraw his own name. Mr. Timmins bowed assent, and the matter ended peacefully. At this last private gathering there were present—Mr. Fred. Norman, Mr. Taylor, Mr. Brundrit, Mr. Humphreys, Mr. T. Handley, Mr. Joseph Pritchard, Mr.E. Imison,Mr.Pritchard, Dr.McDougall,Mr. Meadow- croft and many others. I should not have taken up your time with this tire- some explanation, if the Liverpool papers had not been furnished by somebody with misleading information which was apparently intended to make it seem that the Church party had consented to the loss of a seat— to prevent which very thing I so pertinaciously in- sisted on the public declarations which Dr. McDougall was good enough to make. On the whole nothing has been lost, if nothing has been gained.</p>
        <p>THE VICAR'S DESCRIPTION CONTRADICTED.</p>
        <p>IMPORTANT ANNOUNCEMENT SUPPRESSED.</p>
        <p>The account of the late " bloodless " School Board Election which is extracted from the Parish Magazine and published in this journal to-day will come upon you those who took an active part in that affair with consid- erable surprise. It is not the plain unvarnished tale of a reporter of news, but the cutely worded despatch of a diplomatist, intended to give those who may chance to peruse the " Parish Magazine," a certain impression. Our representative has gone to the trouble to gain some facts in regard to the extraordinary compromise, and they show the position of the church party in a far different light to that sought to be circulated tie, the chief representative of Denominationalism. Some time before the day of election Canon Wood called upon Dr. McDougall with reference to his future position. The doctor replied that if there was no con- test he reserved to himself the right of acting as he thought fit in the matter, and could make no promises whatever, of course, he should be nominated. Some days later the Canon, accompanied by Mr. unison called again, and remarked that they did not want to leave the doctor in the lurch. After expressing his obligations for their kindness, Dr. McDougall said he had nething to add to his former statement. Canon Wood then said that it would be better if the church party thought proper to nominate someone else, no, with the view of provoking a contest from their side, and the conference : ceased, Dr. McDougall had no further conversation with 1 the vicar upon the matter. On the morning of Wednes- day preceding the 's Compromise Meeting "at the Town Hall, Dr. McDougall received notice of a meeting of the church people in the Parish Church schools that night, Canon Wood was speaking when the doctor entered the room. He was glad, he said, that there were no reporters present, so that they could speak with perfect freedem, and he then went on to state that he was not wholly satisfied with Dr. McDougall's conduct on the Board. There had never lie said, been a consultation between the church representatives since their election as to their policy on the Board, and he bad never been able to see Dr. McDougall nor had Dr. MoDougall ever sought him. Dr. McDougall informed the meeting that they were absolutely free to do what they thought fit—to adopt him or repel him—but he also announced that his nominator,Mr. John Brundrit,would not permit him to retire, and he had no intention of retiring, but should test his claims at the poll. Later on replying to the Vicar's charge, Dr. MeDeogall pointed out that the School Board had been unanitnoua in their policy, and that therefore there could have been few if any divided opinions amongst the Church representa- tives. Some person asked him if he received a Church nomination would he consider himself a Church candi- date. The doctor replied certainly ; one was a corollary of the other, and left the room. Much doubt has been thrown upon the accuracy of the vote recorded, but it is a certain fact that the doctor was one of the selected candidates,though the audience appear to have got"mud- dled "with the manner in which the substantive motion was put. After the meeting in the Town Hall on the Thursday night where no compromise could be arrived at owing to the split amongst the Church party,the rate- payers' candidates, who have been styled independents, together with Dr. McDougall, Mr. John Brundrit, Mr. John Meadowcroft, and a number of nominators, proceeded to an upper room. This meeting of the " other party" as the Vicar styles them,quickly resolved to support the four retiring members, viz., Messrs. Pritchard, Handley, Taylor, and Dr. McDougall, and no conditions were imposed upon them. When they were discussing their method of cativo ign,Canon Wood accom- panied byseveral of his party arrived,and he announced that he was authorised to state that Mr. Timmins was prepared to retire on condition that Dr. McDougall became a Church candidate. Dr. McDougall replied Ithat the situation had entirely changed, and that he could not discuss the question until he had consulted those with wham be was now a‘.'societed. Ca non Wood and his friends retired, and Um meeting debated the proposal. Mr. Meadovecroft who had been the inter- mediary between the Trinity people and Dr. McDougall three years ago, stated that no conditions had been imposed upon him by them, and Dr. McDougall men- tioned that no grievance had ever reached him from the Trinity pecple. Ultimately the meeting agreen that Dr. McDougall should become a Church candidate without conditions. Canon Wood was called into the room and Mr. Fred Norman informed him that the meeting was perfectly willing that Dr. McDougall should continue to . be a Church representative, but he reserved to himself perfect liberty of action on the Board. This most preg- nant announcement is left out of the Vicar's letter to his parishioners.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>HOW SMALLPDX IS SPREAD.</head>
        <p>HOW SMALLPDX IS SPREAD.</p>
        <p>STRANGE CONDUCT OF A WIDNES DOCTOR_ A tramp presented himself on Wednesday even- ing for admission to the Hope Hopital, Warrington, haying had the smallpox rash out for a week. He gives the following history of his movements : About ten days ago he left a lodging house in Marybons, Liverpool, walking to Widnes. He stayed there at a house known as " White Paddy's" and afterwards he went to Runcorn, living at a lodging-house near the Market place. He broke out there with smallpox on the 29th December, but stayed until the morning of the 4th January. He then wended his way back to" White Paddy's "at Widnes, but was refused admission. He then called lat a doctor's surgery and the doctor told him to make the best of his way to Warrington.</p>
        <p>NEW YEAR CELEBRATIONS AT ST. HELENS. FREE BREAKFAST TO POOR CHILDREN. The annual free breakfast to 1,200 poor child- ren of St. Helens, including the newsboys, took place on Monday morning at the Ragged Schools,in Waterloo-street. Among the many willing pat, one and helpers Were the Mayor (Mr. A. Sinclair), and the Mayoress (Mrs. Sinclair), Mrs. R. Pilkington Colonel W. W. Pilkington, the Rev. J. W. M.A. (vicar of St. Helens), the Rev. J. C. Pigot, M.A. (vicar of St. Thomas's), Councillor .T. B. Leach (founder of the movement), Councillor T. IPLachlan, Rcv. G. White, Rev. J. Richardson, Mr. Wm. Gamble, Mr. Pringle, and Mr. Harrison (town missionaries), Mr. 'l'. Massey (police court missionary), and a large number of ladies and gentlemen, newspaper representatives, tze. The hungry little children were seated comfortably in front of a bag containing a substantial breakfast, and hot tea and coffee were plentifully served, after which Mr. Jones, knovvn as Professer hP Laughter, save a con- juring and ventriloquial entertainment. Miss Emily Leach played several popular ditties, such as" Ta-ra-ra- boom-de-ay," the words to which were sung with great gusto. The children were loud in their expression of thanks to the donors of the breakfast, which was re- newed when, on leaving, an orange and bun were pre- sented to each child. SKATING MATCH AT ST. HELENS. On Monday afternoon a skating match took place on Carr Mill Dam, St. Helens, between John Attila and John Highcock, two well-known St. Helens skaters. Ihe distance selected was 200 yards, and the sketches were Z 5 a side. About a thousand persons witnessed the match. Ilighcock was generally regarded as the favourite, though Arnitt had a large number of supporters. Highcock got away at the start with a two yards lead, but his opponent gradually gained on him, and won a good race by about five yards. Great excitement prevailed among the spectators.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>LOWE HOUSE SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>LOWE HOUSE SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>On Monday night, a dramatic entertainment, en- titled "The Seven Clerks," was given in Lowe House Catholic Schools, St. Helens, in aid of the Lowe House Recreation Club, an institution which embraces a gymn- asium, billiard-rooms, cyclists' club, and other attrac- tions for young men of the congregation and schools. The performance was followed by the comic operetta " The Blind Beggars." There was a large audience.</p>
        <p>TORTOISE STOVR.S.—GARLiND. il'oll2lollger, IT OITIRatOII and Earleeto vin.-4 do.</p>
      </div>
