      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <p>.44,:(1P 1 .1" 'Ol 'y ! ' _</p>
        <p>labourer, no address, was charged with being found on the premises of Messrs. Pearson, Knowles, and Co., Bewsey Forge, on Sunday morning with intent to com- mit a felony, also with using threatening language to the watchman,Lloyd,on his finding him on the premises,and after he had been given by him into custody the prisoner told Lloyd he would not mind doing a term of imprison- ment for him, and would lie in wait for hint.—He was discharged with a caution. ' DRUNK AND DISORDHRLY.—Thomas Hennessy, labourer in Cropper's Brow, on Christmas Day, was fined ss. and costs.—Wm. Davies, in the Old-road Latchford, on the 27th of December, who had told the officer, who appre- hended him, that if he locked him up or summoned him, he would blow his brains out with a gun he kad with him, was fined ss. CHIIINNTS ols FIRIL—Ellen Balshaw, wife of Robert Balshaw, 69 Dallam-lane, was charged with setting her chimney on fire and fined is., and Jane Quinn, widow, Smith-st., for allowing her chimney to be on fire was fined the like amount. . .</p>
        <p>TIIESDAY.—Before Mr. R. Garnett (in the chair), and Dr. Starkey Smith. OBSCIRDiI Laxaulaa.—Annie Daly, Dial Court, was sent to jail for seven days for using obscene language. DRIMICEMNPISSI.—James Pigot, a labourer, Town Hill, was fined Sc. and costs for drunken and disorderly con- duct in Buttermarket-street, the previous night. COULD WELL DO WITHOUT Tuam.—Ellen Connor and Annie Smith, two well-known characters, were charged with frequenting an unoccupied house in Naylor-street.' the previous night. A constable stated that he noticed one of the panes in the window was broken, he peered through and saw the two prisoners lying on the floor. He took them into custody. In the course of the case it appeared that Connor had been before the Bench at least 52 times.—The Chief Constable said if it was not for about 12 such characters the town would be somewhat Peaceful.—The Chairman said he did not think imprison- ment had much effect upon such characters, but as it was the only punishment he would send them to prison br seven days. WEDNasnar.—Before Mr. J„ .Geddes (in the chair), and . . .</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Wright. A PaHDATORY TorTa.—A youth named Lionel John Smith was charged with stealing a whip value 63. the property of Mr. Wilkinson, sadler, Bridge Foot. The evidence showed that the defendant, will was twelve years of age, was the son of very respectable people, who were connected with the °gird Barracks. One of the officers at the Military Depot had ordered a whip from Mr. Wilkinson, and the defendant on his own behalf went and procured it on the 29th Sepnember, saying he had been sent by the officer. The theft was only discovered on the 2nd inst., when Mr. Wilkinson sent in the bill for the whip.—The boy was discharged with a conviction recorded against him. WALLOWID ON TIIN Ftoon.—Annie Quinn was charged with being drunk and disorderly the previous night. She said she came from Wigan that day to look for work at. the factory. But having partaken ef pota- tions with friends during the day she lay on the floor that night when the constable found her. But she wasn't drunk. No T. She said it was all owing to having " taken the beer on an empty stomach." The constable said she wanted to break the windows at the Boars Bead and when they prevented her she lay down and wal- lowed on the floor. It took two constables to bring her down. —Fined Sc. aria costs. FBlDAY.—Before Alderman Wright (in the chair), Dr. Gomel!, Messrs. W. Pierpoint, H. Roberts, and J. W.</p>
        <p>Hayward. SMASHING WlNDOWS.—Michael Hogan,Ship-yard, was charged with breaking windows to the value of 205., in a house formerly belonging to the late Mr. J. Porter, the charge being brought by the executors Messrs. T. and J. 1.. Tunstall and Co , Bewsey Chambers. Defendant pleaded guilty, and said be committed the offence on the 28th December. The case was adjourned. RBPOSING TO MAINTAIN HIS MOTHSS.—Wm. flallOWeS, I 55, Bewsey-street, was summoned by the Warrington Board of Guardians for getting into arrears to the extent of £5 on a weekly order of 2s. per week,f or the mainten- ance of his mother. An order for one month's commit- went was made. A Tate Ow A NEM YBAlit'S TOHYET.—Elizabeth Howard, Hall•street, was summoned for assaulting Martha Stirrup, Irlam-street. A cross summons was also taken out against the prosecutrix by the defendant. —Mr. P. Davies appeared for Mrs. Stirrup, and Mrs. Howard was represented by Mr. A. Browne. Mr. Davies said that Mrs. Howard went into Mrs. Stirrup's shop on Saturday night and ordered a turkey, which she said she would pay for later on. She took the bird, and some time afterwards she returned to the shop in- ebriated, and demanded the turkey. She had had the turkey and probably lost it. She demanded another one, and on being refused created a disturbance, and upon being ejected struck Mrs. Stirrup in the mouth, the latter calling in P.S. Rowbotham in for protection. Sergeant Rowbotham said late on Saturday night he heard a noise opposite Stirrup's shop. There had been a row, an Mrs. Howard was going away—people were pushing her, and she seemed to be under the influence of drink.—Mr. Browne said the case ought not to have come into court because the two persons c'ere I of a most excitable temper. There was a certain am ou.-.t of spleen existing on the part of Mrs. Stirrup against his client. Pile evidence for his defence was to the effect thgt Mrs. Howard ordered a turkey and said she would send her daughter for it. Her daughter went with the money to Mrs. Stirrup's about eleven o'clock, but, upon being told that her mother had taken the pur- chase away with her the girl returned home. Her mother came in then, but she certainly was not under the in- fluence o! drink. They both returned to Mrs. Stirrup's shop. A quarrel ensued, but her mother did not strike prosecutrix, she merely shoved her in retaliation.—The prosecutrix : You took the turkey.—Sergeant Chantler gave evidence to the effect that he saw the defendant returning home, as though coming from Mrs. Stirrup's, but she had no turkey with her, and was certainly not under the influence of drink. That was about half-past nine.—The case lasted about two hours, and culminated _ _</p>
        <p>in the Bench inflicting a fine of ss. and costs upon the defendant.</p>
        <p>COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WBEINISDAY. —Before Mr. J. Rylands (in the chair) Major Pickmere, and Mr. W. Mortimer.</p>
        <p>..... DEUTNEINNOSS.—Aaron Dixon and Joseph Sudlow, of Sankey, were fined 2s. ed. each for drunkenness at Penketh. CYCLISTS IN TRAYUBLIL—Henry Deakin, Win. Bent, of Manchester, and Charles Claude }3enry Verity, of Weast, were each fined is for riding bicycles on the footpath at Rixton,on the 17th.</p>
        <p>WIDNES POLICE COURT. MONDAT.—Before Mr. H. Wade Deacon. DRUNLHNNESS.—MichaeI Timmins, Wellington-st., and Joseph McGuire, 17, Farrant-st., were charged with being drunk, and were fined 2s. 6d. and costs eath.— Patrick Quinn Walmsley-st., and Rachael Connolly were charged with similar offences, but on promising not to get drunk again were discharged.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. Moxper.—Betore Mr, J. Brundrit and Major Hazlehurst,</p>
        <p>- MISCELLANBOOS.—Joseph Hartles,Mersev-road, Widnes, was summoned for being drunk in Waterloo-road, Run- corn, on December 22nd. Fined ss.—John Palmer, Cholmondeley-place, was summoned for committing a breach of the peace on the 24th December. The charge was dismissed.—John Donavan, J. W. McDonald, and E. Melia were summoned for obstructing the footpath in Brunswick-at., on the 25th ult. Michael Sheeney and ' Thomas Feeney were summoned for a like offence,on the same date. The Chairman said it was quite time the police took notice of that matter, which was a growing , ' evil. The language used was unfit for anyone to hear. He was pleased some action was being taken. Ordered to pay the costs. Other cases, said the Chairman,would ' be dealt with in a severe manner. TMIiFT OF TRIPH AT RIINCORN.—AIIan Ackerlev, New- towo,Altrincham,was charged with stealing five pounds of tripe, value Es. 6cf., the property of James Allen, Church-st.,Runcorn, on Saturday afternoon. In answer to the charge the prisoner said, "Well, I had no money." lie now pleaded guilty, remarking that he was the worse for drink at the time. Committed to gaol for seven days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>.3"-Affiv,7'?,;1,5141-1 _ ,</head>
        <p>.3"-Affiv,7'?,;1,5141-1 _ ,</p>
        <p>PROCLAMATION.</p>
        <p>£2O Reward.—Whereas it has come to our know- ledge that certain unscrupulous person or persons have lately beea offering for sale and passing off Quinine Bitters not fit our manufacture, and have endeavoured when GWILYM EVANS' QGININTH BITTERS has been ssked for, to substitute and pass off other preparations.</p>
        <p>The above Reward will be paid to anyone who will give such information as will enable us to ensure sonviction in any case of passing off or substitut- ing.</p>
        <p>WARNING!</p>
        <p>We also desire to warn the Public to be on their guard,and see the name " GWILYA EveNs' BITTHILS " on each bottle and label.</p>
        <p>For further PROTECTION each bottle has the Government Stamp, wit the name " GWILYM EVANS, F.C.S., M.P.S., LLANELLY," engraved thereon,—to imitate which is FORGERY.</p>
        <p>Signed, QUININE BITTERS MANUFACTURING</p>
        <p>CO., LD., LLANELLY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>HE TRAVELLED TO WIGAN.</p>
        <p>"Ingenious youth where will thy brilliant course end," Shakespere asked, and the queition never seems to have been answered until Wednesday when John Wand exclaimed " Wigan." His course had ended there. The Bench were told that last January the youth before them delivered goods for Mr. William Fairhurst, pork butcher. Buttermarket-street. He also collected the money for these goods, and once when he g2t 225. he said" I'll travel." Be did ; he went on foot to Wigan. And he forgot all about Mr. Fairhurst's 225., so Mr. Fairhurst told Monoghan to remind him. When the latter met John in Warrington on Tuesday, puffing luxuriously at a cigarette, he said " Come with me"; and he took him to the mansion and left him there until the next morning when John said his course of travel didn't extend beyond Wigan ; and the bench said it might have extended to Stra.ngeways, but they would let him off with a conviction recorded against him.</p>
        <p>ASSAULTED BY • GIRL.</p>
        <p>Eve's descendants seem to be loosing all claim to the title of "weaker sex." Man wants it now; at least so it would appear from the action taken by John Garrity of the Old-road. Before the magistrates on Friday. he summoned Catherine Salmon, a young girl 6f Bake- house Yard, for assaulting hire. He Bard she met him at Bridge Foot, on Thursday, and charged down on him with a broom. Perhaps it was a new one because they always sweep clean and this swept clean past him. So he ran away, and by and by when he was coming past a shop he said she ran out and knocked him down with a can, and then her brother came up and sat on his chest. Then he felt something colliding with his head with pendulum like regularity. He judged it was his brain leaping at irst, but upon turning round, perceived that defend- ant's little sister had a brick tied up in a handkerchief, and she was flattening his head with it. Presently her mother came with a poker in her hand and looked at him, and then some little boys began to put mud in his mouth. The girl denied this, and said she merely asked him for an explanation about some "nasty things he had said about her." Both parties brought about an equal amount of rebutting evidence, and the Bench dis- missed the case, and when the girl—a good-looking girl —made sheeps' eyes at John,he shrunk away,and said he was afraid. . _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>Jorm Rooligt's lATTLE Joan.</head>
        <p>Jorm Rooligt's lATTLE Joan.</p>
        <p>"Rooney "was the simple word which the magist- rates' clerk uttered on Tuesday morning ; and yet it had a strange power upon the court ; people looked to- wards the box with keen interest plainly in their eyes, as though expecting to see the heroine of that little bollard "Little Annie Rooney is my sweetheart." But she didn't come. It was Thomas of that ilk whom Sergeant Chantler assisted gently into the old spot with the observation" Go 'long Thomas—clutter." Be did not clutter. He simply sighed the word "Bah," when told he was charged with being on the premises of John Hatton, Mersey-st., the previous night. He did more, he winked softly at a rude pair of base coloured pants ; so rude that a purple blush sprung forth from beneath his tobacco coloured hair, and stealing gently down thel hills and valleys of his face, roosted like a resplendent sunset on a pimple on his nose. Then Mr. John Hatton spoke up. It seemed that Rooney often joked. He used to go along to peoples shops and hide ; and when they turned their backs he would pick up a money till or any ether useless little thing and run away,and laugh. Once a humorous man in blue cloth saw him do these jokes ; and he took him to amuse the Government, and they kept him picking up oakum for a long time. On Tues- day night Mr. Hatton saw him climb into his yard, and John, who is a bit of a locksmith, went to a hen coop. When Mr. Hatton got to John he was testing the lock and whistling gently " Thy sweet patron is awaiting," or something. Well, they didn't agree about something and Mr. Hatton handed John over to the police. Maybe it was because he wakened the hens but anyhow the Bench said it was naughty, and John had to pay either 14s. or" do 14 days.' He went for the days.</p>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>Skruaney.-13;fore Messrs. T. J. Gillespie (in the chair), W. F. Goozh, W. Mitchell, and T. D. Grimke Dray-</p>
        <p>ton. Dunsammase.—The following individuals were dealt with for drunkenness :—Harold Grundy, Patrick Clarke, John Larker, James Fawshaw, Thomas Locker, Michael denglish, and Thomas Toby. " A DISORDERLY FATHER.—Thomas Pennington of Golborne, was charged with doing wilful damage to the windows in the house of his son, Alfred Pennington, on the 20th December.—The complainant dercribed how his father came to his (complainant's) house where he liyed on the 20th of December, and used a considerable .okount of offensive language, created a general row and bMke the windows.—Defendant was ordered to pay the cost of the damage and was also fined 2s. 6d.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT. SATTISDAY.—Before Messrs. Burgess (in the chair), and</p>
        <p>, L. Voisey. RE LOVED NOT HIS WlFE.—Richard Jackson was charged with having committed an assault on his wife, Agnes, on the 10th of December.—Agnes Jackson stated that on Saturday, the 10th December, the defendant came home and she asked him for his wages. He thereupon rushed at her, and thumped her in the face a number of times. He finished by kicking her about the body several times. Her husband's wages were about 30s. a week. Sometimes he gave her 15s. a week,some- times 205., and at .ther times nothing at all. She had two children to keep. The defendant said that the rea- son he did not give his wife the wages was that she spent the money in drink. They had been married eight years, and during that time the home had been broken up seven times. After working hard for a week he went towards his home on the 10th of December, but found that his wife had broken it up. It was " enough .to make a man do anything."-- Ile Bench committed the defendant to gaol for 14 days. Noy A" BED or Rosms."—John McCue was charged with having been found sleeping in Dall im Forge dur- ing Friday morning. Sergeant Lawrence, a police officer, employed specially by Messrs. Pearson and KnOwles's forges at Dallam and Bewsev, stated that on Friday morning he found the prisoner sleeping in the works. He was lying in a most dangerous place, and was practically under one of the large hot-water boilers. If the outside tap of the boiler had been accidently turned on, the defendant would have, without adoubt, been scalded to death. The defendant was a man wilo never worked regularly, hut when working he was a v,ry steady and industrious man. It was not the firsl time he had been caught sleeping in the works. The defendant stated that he commenced work on the Tues- day night, and continued at it for the two next nights. He had no money to pay for lodgings, so lie was forced to sleep in the works or else roam about the streets in ' the cold. He had asked his employer for a " sub," but was told he could not have one. Sergeant Lawrence, re-called, stated that this was not true, as any work- man who applied for a "sub " could have it. Defend- ant was sent to prison for seven days with hard labour. DRUNEENNESS.—Thomas Stirrup was fined for this offence.</p>
        <p>MONDAY—Before Mr. F. Monks (chairman), Alderman Francomb, and Messrs. W. nerpoint, T. Birchall, and J. W. Hayward. ON PREMISES UNLawentax.—Thomas Farrington,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>SONG PER TR' NEW YEAR.</head>
        <p>SONG PER TR' NEW YEAR.</p>
        <p>"King Christmas comes but once a year," Is beaut a deaubt quite true ; But it's ez weel we don't ferget, That New Year's Day does too. Woes sauce fer th' gonder owt to be Sum sauce fer th' goose, aw say ; Then keep yore sperrits up, an' don't Get deawn 'ith' meauth to-day. Its fine to hey yore kith an' kin Areaund yet festive board ; Wi' ev'ry care an' trubhle gone, An sweet content restor'd. But, ei! heaw grand to hear the bells Go crashin' eaut so gay; An' tellin' ev'ry livin' sowl, "'o moan't be deawn to-day." Wen friend meets friend wi' shake o' fist, An' healths are drunk NVP glee ; Wen we con mak' another start Fer tale prosperity. Wen o'er the jovial pipe an' glass, We give eaur sperrits play; An' summat whispers in eaur ear, "Yo moan't be deawn to-day ! " Wen chums i' aw the nooks on earth Are wishin' us God speed ; An' blessin's upo' ev'ry yd Is ev'rybody's creed. Then, here's success to th' Good New Year, An' let's be free an' gay— lie's his own enemy, my lads, That's deawn i'th' meauth to-day ! TUM 0' PEGS.</p>
        <p>Rome Bonk, January 4, 1893.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 7, 1893. • THE BLACKBURN MURDER. EXECUTION AT WALTON GAOL.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 7, 1893. • THE BLACKBURN MURDER. EXECUTION AT WALTON GAOL.</p>
        <p>A PENKETH DOMESTIC FATALITY.</p>
        <p>SAD DISCLOSURES OF INHUMANITY.</p>
        <p>On Monday afternoon, Mr. S. Brighouse, coroner, held an inquest at the Infirmary Dispensary, con- cerning the death on the 29th ult., of Elizabeth Townley, aged 61. wife of John Bond Townley, whitestnith, Doe Green, Penketh, it having been alleged that she had died from the . effects of injuries caused through being kicked on the 3rd of September by, her son,John Townley, who was now present in custody having been apprehended the previous night. Mr. J. D. Buckton defended the ' prisoner. Police InSpector Kelly was also present. John Bond Townley deposed : [ recollect a Saturday in last year, but not the month or day of the month. On that Saturday I went to bed about half-past eight. I left my wife downstairs reading. My son, John end his wife also lived in tne house. He was not in. I got up between ten and eleven.—The Coroner: What made you get up 7—Witness: Well I don't like to answer that— The Coroner : But you will be obliged to. You know I've got before me your statement made to the police.— By the Coroner : John hit me a time or two, while I was in bed. I did not see my son do anything to my wife between 11 and 12 o'clock. As I was coming down stairs my wife was at the bottom of the stairs, and I had to stride over her. I don't know how she had got into that position. I didn't see it, and I can't swear whether she was knocked down or fell down. (left John upstairs. I asked her if she was hurt, and she said "My leg's broke." I went to nty son Peter's. I called first at Phillip Forshaw's. My wife was removed on the Monday following to this Infirmary. I went to see her on the Sunday morning. I stopped out of the house all Sunday night, because my son was rather bad tempered and that—The Coroner: Better put it shorter and say you were frightened of him.—Witness: I don't think he would harm either of us.—The Coroner: Although became upstairs and hit you; you think, he meant you no harm 11—Witness: Well, he was always very fond of 11;s mother and that.—The Coroner: A very peculiar way of shciwing his affection.-=lnspector Kelly : Did you not tell me yesterday evening that your son came upstairs when you were in bed and commenced to beat you, and that while he was beating you, your wife came upstairs and he knocked her downstairs ? The Coroner repeated the question.—Witness: I could not tell you that, because I was in the room and could not see what happened.—The Coroner: Did you or did you not tell him that 7—Witness : He must have misunderstood, because it was an impossibility for me to see out of the room into a lot of stairs.—The Coroner : Did you or did you not tell him ? —Witness : Well, if I did tell-him, it was a mistake. 1 could not tell him I saw it because I could not.—lnspec- tor Kelly : Did you tell me that your son knocked your wife downstairs 7—Witness: I could not tell you that because I could not see it done. I only tcici you what my Mrs. told me.—lnspecter Kelly: Did not you tell me that she said that John bad broken her leg 7—Witness: Perhaps I did. If I said so, she might have said so.— Inspector Kelly : I think you said your son was very violent, and you had to run out with your clothes under your arm 7—Witness: I had to go out till he got his temper down.—The Coroner : What clothes had you on? —Witness : My trousers and shirt.—By Mr. Buckton : I had been to Warrington market that evening. I had one glass of beer at the Crown and Coshion, Sankey, and I had some drink in Warrington. My wife was with me and had some drink too. I was under the influence of drink. My son is like me, he gets a drop too much, sometimes. I think he had that night. There is no hand rail up the stairs. She might have tumbled down them. Supposing she had had a knock or a shove she would have been more likely to fall if she was not perfectly, sober. She might have fallen con- sidering the condition I left her in at half-past eight—By. Mr. Buckton : I am not sure whether she said " He has broke my leg," or" John has broke my leg," when I came downstairs. At that time, John was coming down. He might have heard what she said, but he made no remark.—By the jury : I did not assist her up when I saw her lying. My son picked her up, I was told.—A. juryman: Do you think that was humane conduct ? The Coroner said it was pretty clear that the witness went-out of the house for fear of his son.</p>
        <p>Peter Edgar, assistant clerk to Mr. H. Greenell, clerk to the Warrington county justices, read the depositions of the deceased, taken before Mr. John White, J.P., at the Infirmary, on the 12th of December, in the presence of the prisoner and his solicitor, Mr. Jenkins. These stated that on the night of September 3rd, soon after 11 o'clock, she was at home with her son's wife when her son, in whose name the house was taken, came in, witness's husband being upstairs in bed. His wife "began on him " about having ale : he was not sober. She ran away from him into the ash-house. He went upstairs to his father's room, and she heard a noise there. She started to go upstairs, but he met her and pushed her downstairs, and broke her leg. he fell to the bottom of the stairs, and lay there all night. Her son's wife came and spoke to her and went to bed. Next , morning, she did not know what time, her son came, and carried her on his back, and put her on a bed, and he sat with her all day. She was quite sober on the Saturday night. Her son was not very sober or very drunk. On the Monday he and his wife brought her to the Infirmary. Her husband did not get drunk on the night of the 3rd of September, that she knew. Since her son had been in India, where he had been 5 years, in the 40th regiment, he had not been very right, and she was sure he did not know sometimes what he was doing. He had been very kind to her. He might.have kicked her hip by accident. When he had had drink 'he did not know what he was doing. Elizabeth Forshaw, wife of Peter Forshaw, living next door to the Townleys said she recollected a cer- tain Saturday night, but not the month. The deceased and her husband came from Warrington, about half- past seven to eight o'clok, both drunk, the deceased the most so. Witness went into their house to cook for them. Her daughter brought a pint of ale for the old man and he went to bed. Witness went home, leaving Mrs. Townley downstairs, about a quarter to nine, meeting the daughter-in-law coming home from the nine train down the village. After 11 o'clock,when witness was at borne, her daughter opened the back door and Mr. Townley sen. came running in, with only his shirt and trousers on, and his son ran after him. The old man said "Please' Mr. Philip Forshaw, save me." The son struck his father twice violently between the eyes. The deceased came in, and , said "John, don't strike your father," and got hold of him by the shoulders, begging him not to strike. They were in the lobby, and he took hold of her and dashed her out of the door down on the stones, telling her, with an oath, to go out. She called out "Mrs. Forshaw, he has broke my hip." John went back home, and witness and her daughter took her home</p>
        <p>and put her on tt chair in the kitchen. Her daughter- in-law, Louisa, was in the parlour. Witness left her daughter with the deceased. She could not have walked as straight as she did if she had broken her hip. Wit- ness did not think she had sustained any injuries. Wit- ness heard no noise like falling downstairs. The chair she was in in the kitchen was near the stairs and she might have fallen off it. She kept moaning, and crying "Louisa" all night. Annie Forshaw, daughter of the last witness, gave corroborative evidense, and said during the night she heard a noise as of somehedy falling. The deceased and her husband were not sober. She could not walk straight when she came in. Dr. E. E. Bowden, house surgeon at the Infirmary, said the deceased was admitted there on September sth, suffering from a fractured thigh and bronchitis, and died on the 211th of December. He had made a post mortem examination. In his opinion she died from bronchitis and inflammation of the lungs. The fact of the fracture necessitating her being kept in bed made the bronchitis worse. But for having to be kept in bed she would have had a better chance of recovery from the bronchitis. The Coroner Men summed up. He pointed out that if the prisoner had caused injuries to the deceased which accelerated her death from other causes by one hour he was as guilty in law as if he had caused the death of a previously healthy person. He thought that the chance of her recovery had she not had to be confined to bed on account of the fracture was to remote an issue on which to commit the prisoner for trial, and no jury would convict upon it. He advised a verdict of death from natural causes. The jury, after some minutes' private consultation, re- turned a verdict in accordance with the coroner's direction a rider at first being added that death was accelerated by injuries caused by her son, but on the Coroner pointing out that this would necessitate his being sent for trial, this was withdrawn. The jury also recommended that the deceased's husband, son, father, and daughter-in- law he censured for the cruel manner in which they had left deceased to herself. The Coroner had them called before him, and in- formed the son that he had only been saved by the medi- cal evidence from committal for trial. There could be no doubt that he came home drunk, and beat his father while in bed .and afterwards pushed his mother down on the flags. He hoped the narrow escape he had had would be a lesson to him, and he would advise him never to drink again. He and his father and the daugh- ter-in-law could not haven grain of compassion in them to allow the poor old woman to lie groaning and moan- ing as they did. rhe son said nothing would have happened but for his father,and he did not do it intentionally, but because he had had teo much drink. He did not know till next morning that his mother was lying at the foot of the stairs. The widower also said that he was unaware of this at the time ; and the daughter-in-law denied that this had occurred, and said she put the deceased on to the sofa in the parlour. The Coroner said the daughter-in-law was as bad as the others, because she appeared to have been the only one sober, and asked her why she did not go for a doctor or a neighbour.—She replied that it was too late.—The Coroner : Oh, no, the workhouse doctor was bound to come.—A Juror : Dr. Murray is close to.—The Coroner again addressed some remarks of censure to the three persons concerned, and the proceedings concluded.</p>
        <p>ley, and thereby causing her death. Mr. Jenkins, solici- tor (Messrs. Jenkins and Unsworth), appeared on behalf of the prisoner, and explained the circumstances of the case. An inquest had been held on the deceased, with the result that the jury had returned a verdict that she had died from natural causes, with a wish that the Coroner should reprimand the prisoner for his conduct. With this, however, his Worship had nothing to do, and as he (Mr. Jenkins understood that the police offered no evidence against the prisoner, he applied for his dis- charge. Inspector Kelly said that in face of the verdict of the Coroner's jury he was instructed to offer no evidence, and the prisoner was thereupon discharged.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO A RUNCORN WORKS MANAGER. Mr. W. M. Sowerby, who has for nearly two years held the position of manager of works for the United Alkali Co., at the Runcorn Alkali Works, was on Saturday presented by the general staff with a very handsome afternoon tea service, in silver, by Elkington. The occasion which brought about the presentation was his promotion to the man- agement of Mathieson's works at Widnes, one of the largest manufacturies belonging to the Union. There was a large attendance at the ceremony, which took place in a large room at the works.—Mr. John Poole eulogised Mr. Sowerby's energetic personality, great business ability, and sterling character. They regretted , his removal, but they remembered that their loss was his gain, and they felt some little elation that their re- vered manager had been chosen for the position at Widnes out of very many able men. The changes did not affect their loyalty- to their employers, and they should be prepared to do their best for Mr. Callender as they had done for their much respected departing man- ager.—Mr. Brookfield added his testi monyto Mr.Soweity's good qualities, and said he was sure those with whom he would in future be associated had only to know him to esteem him alike for his ability as for his personal charac- ter. Mr. Gleeson, general foreman, and others,also made speeches of a laudatory character, and then Mr. Thomas Riley made the presentation. In doing so he said it was with feelings of regret that they heard of his removal, and they felt that they must not allow this opportunity to pass without in some way proving their sorrow and showing the esteem in which they held him. As their mknager he had shown unvarying kindness and consid- eration, and they should feel his removal keenly,but they did not forget that the position of greater responsibility which he would take up would be his gain,and thus they felt consoled. On behalf of the subscribers he expressed the hope that Mr. and Mrs. Sowerby might long be spared to enjoy health and prosperity, and that Mr. Sowerby's future labours might be crowned with success.</p>
        <p>Mr. Sowerby, who was received with applause, said : . _ . . . .</p>
        <p>I am sure that I feel extremely obliged for the handsome present you have made me to-day. I do not know that I have any way deserved such a substantial token of your regard ; but whilst saving that Ido not wish to hide trim you the fact that I have at all times endeav- oured to deal honestly and fairly with each and every member of the staff. Ido believe, and your gift is a proof of it, that I go away with the good feeling of every one of you, from the youngest to the oldest, and I can assure you that my own feeling with regard to your- selves is one of very great respect. I need not prolong my thanks : it will be sufficient to say they are sincere, and in bidding you good-bye I again, on behalf of Mrs. ISowerby and myself, thank you very heartily for your present and good feelings.—The proceedings then ter- minated, with three times three for Mr. and Mrs. So werby.</p>
        <p>AGED FOLK'S TREAT AT THE DRILL HALL.</p>
        <p>The annual treat to the aged Poor of Warrington came off in the Drill Hall, on Tuesday. This year in conseqUence of a decision induced by the epidemic, the treat assumed the form not of the customary dining together after the old-fashioned style, but the distribution of tickets constituting orders upon tradesmen for a certain quantity of pro- visions,tor which they were to be subsequently paid by the Committee. The Drill Hall had been placed at the disposal of the Committee by Colonel Greenall, who had assented to the request for its use, in a most genial letter of appreciation of the object in view. All the poor of the town in really necessitous circumstances and over the age of 80, weretinvited by placard 'to assemble in the Hall, where the men were supplied with their tickets from ten to twelve o'clock, and the women from two to four In the afternoon. The invitation was accepted by at least a thousand persons. Each candidate was in turn subjected to exhaustive examination in order to insure against imposition ; and it really came very fre- quently to a contest of wits between the Committee and many of the applicants, in the discussion of their claims te the dole so eagerly coveted. Some of the answers to the questions put were characterised by much Hibernian comicality, for instance, one applicant declared her age wipe "30 years odd and more, and this was a fair example of some of the problems that the committee had to solve. Many, of the most various ages below that prescribed as the limit, presented themselves, and some of these told such pitiable tales, and were mani- festly in such misery that a relaxation of the rule was made in their favour. In other cases collections were made among the committee in aid of such applicants. The oldest male claimant to the relief was a man aged 90, but no woman appears to have presented herself who was out of the eighties. The committee, it may be men- tioned, experienced somewhat more difficulty in keep- ing the female applicants under proper discipline than they had with the men, though a great amount of good humour and even hilarity was observable among the women, who apparently were determined to make as pleasant as possible the tedium of waiting their turns. The men received from the committee a ticket value is. 6d each, and nalf an ounce of tobacco, and the women tickets value is. each, and a mince pie, the women 'also receiving a gift from Mr. Fletcher, gas stove manufacturer, consisting of half a lb. of tea, and a lb. of sugar. His Worship the Mayor was present during the afternoon, and rendered some assistance to the committee in the preservation of order, in which service also Sergeant-Major Byrne, of the .Drill Hall proved a useful auxiliary, while Mr. W. H. Reed also was an exceedingly serviceable ally. The committee consisted of Messrs. T. H. Sutton (president), P. Chrimes, J. Smethurst, C. Skinner, S. Jones, D. Jones, D. Fanshaw, George Pilling, P. Caldwell, J. E. Wright (hon. secretaty), and I'. Morris (hon. treasurer). We may mention that during the interval between the morning and afternoon distributions, the committee partook of an excellent hotpot dinner, at the Lion Hotel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>SCENE ON TNE SCAFFOLD.</head>
        <p>SCENE ON TNE SCAFFOLD.</p>
        <p>THE CONVICT PROTESTS HIS INNOCENCE.</p>
        <p>At eight o'clock on Tuesday morning, Cross Duck- worth, aged 32, a brass dresser, of Witton, near Black- burn. was executed at Walton Gaol, Liverpool, for the murder of Alice Barnes, aged nine, daughter of a Witton fanner, on the Bth November. The High Sheriff (Mr. John Knowles, of Pendlebury) decided not to admit re- presentatives of the Press to the execution, which was accordingly only witnesied by the prison officials, anaongst those present in front of the scaffold being the Under-Sheriff (Mr. W. P. Fullagar, solicitor, Bolton), Drs. Hammond and Beamish, prison surgeons; Mr. Haverfield, the governor; the Rev. David Morris, Pro- testant chaplain; and two of the principle warders. Billington of Parnworth, near Bolton. who had slept in the prison overnight, was the executioner. The execution was carried out with the utmost speed, the whole process of pinioning arms and legs, adjusting the fatal noose,the drawing on of the black cap, and the handling of the levers not occupying five minutes. The scene on the scaffold was quiet and eventful, and outside the prison gates there was little indication of any wide- spread interest in the legal tragedy passing within. Billington did his work swiftly and skillfully. The noose was adjusted dexterously, to the left side, the bolt ' was withdrawn, and the man was shot into eternity. Duckworth did not make any communication as to his guilt. He protested his innocence to the last. Death appeared to be instantaneous, the rope hardly quivered at all. "If there be such a thing las instan- taneous death by hanging," said the Governor after- wards, "it was in this instance. The poor fellow," he added, " bore up as well as he could, but he was very much distressed. He did not need assistance to get to the scaffold. Ido not know the length of drop the executioner gave hi.i ; but everything was done to the utmost satisfaction."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>DUCKWORTH UTTERLY DAZED. EXECUTED IN A TRANCE.</head>
        <p>DUCKWORTH UTTERLY DAZED. EXECUTED IN A TRANCE.</p>
        <p>It has now transpired that Duckworth, when he roached the scaffold, and for some time before, was in an utterly dazed condition. Ho was like a paralytic in a dream, and appeared to be totally unconscious of his surroundings. His progress to the scaffold was accom- plished in a mechanical automatic way, and he passed into the pit without realising that he was being hanged. Duckworth entered into this mesmeric trance on Friday morning immediately after the governor communicated to him the decision of the Homo Secretary, and he re- mained in it to the last, with a brief luminous interval when his wife and children visited him. It is said that he was injudiciously buoyed up by the positive assur- ance that he would be reprieved ,and the statement that in no instance had a recommendation to mercy, such as. the jury had made in his regard, been without effect. This undesirable bolstering up of a false,theory, which Duckworth clutched at with great tenacity, and under the spell of which he became restful and easy, had a terrible denou6inent. When Mr. Havertield entered the doomed cell on Friday Duckworth was quite persuaded that his life had been saved, and his surprise was in- tense when the contents of the fatal missive was divulged. He did not utter a word, but under the stunning stroke he passed in tha presence of the gover- nor into a state of bewildered stupor, from which he never was really roused. He cried hysterically at times, and then relapsed into a state of utter mental vacuity. Previously he had employed a good deal of his time in reading, but after the governor's message he did not look at a book again. On Sunday he remained in the same stupified condition, and on Tuesday morning it was more acute than before. He was, therefore, really executed in a trance. A pathetic incident has come to light with respect to the interview Duckworth had with hif wife, children and relatives, on Monday afternoon. Ode of the children became rather tired of the dulness of the proceedings, and in a feverish half-complaining Way, said," Dada, aren't you coming home to-night?" The clear reasonant pitch of the youngster's treble sounded strangely through the grated chamber, where the interview was taking place, and produced a very painful sensation on all present.</p>
        <p>On Monday afternoon, by permission of the Prier:in Commissioners and an orders duly signed by the local magistrates, Mr. Haverfield, governor of her Majesty's Prison, Walton,admitted a sorrowing party of the Duck- worths to visit the condemned man on the eve of his execution. The party was the largest that had ever visited Walton Gaol to take a final farewell of a man or woman about to die. It consisted of abont twenty per- sons, notably among whom was Mrs. Duckworth—the condemned man's wife and children. The others con- sisted of the relatives of Duckworth, uncles, aunts, and cousins in varying degrees, They were all well-to-do in appearance, and seemed deeply grieved at the insinua- tions that they were so poor that they could not afford to visit Walton. on Monday they seemed to be middle- class people, comfortably circumstanced. They arrived at Walton before one o'clock, and after a call at a neigh- bouring hostelry, proceeded ois mane to the prison., Once inside they were divided into two parties. The first consisted of men more or less distantly related to the condemned man. One or two of them were middle- ' aged,and appeared thoroughly convinced of Duckworth's innocence. The others were younger members of the Duckworth root, and were not visibly distressed. The interview granted to those first admitted was short, but acutely distressing. Duckworth was ushered in by two warders to the place appointed for such meetings. On emerging from the prison gates, a member of the male party said, "Duckworth has undergone a great ohange since I saw him last. He is careworn and very much reduced. He hardly spoke to us when we were present. At last he found his tongue and said something." "What did he say ?" " He said he was innocent, and his words were the same as those used at the trial, he knew nothing about it.'" "Did he say anything particular about the murder beyond this?" "No ; how could he? He said he knows, and I know he knows, neawt abeawt it. How can a fellow tell you anything about a thing he does not know, and yet they are going to bang him. It is horrible ! " The speaker looked to the flag-pole on the tower and shuddered. Then he went away and joined a group of his friends. Duckworth, it was further ascertained, had slept fairly well during the night, and had eaten his meals with some relish. He was in good health, in fact just as he has been all along. He was seen by the chaplain, the Rev. D. Morris, to whom he attentively listened. He has made no confession of guilt, " and if he had," said the chaplain, "I would treat it as sacred." Duckworth was in a state of intense agitation as the day—his last on earth—wore on, for he anticipated a visit from his wife and children, though he was not aware that such a large number of the family would put in an appearance. In the early portion of the day he was deeply distressed, and suffered acutely from melan- choly. He brightened up for a moment as his male relatives came hate the leavetaking apartment, but he afterwards sank into a state of deep gloom and silence, out of which he could not be roused. The visitors per- ceiving that they were doing no good, and were only distressing the unhappy man, rose and took a tearful farewell of him long before the time allowed for the in- terview had expired. The second batch of friends, consisting of wife, child- ren, aunts, and cousins, was then admitted. The interview was extremely painful. Duckworth utterly broke down, emitting loud groans when he saw his children. Mrs. Duckworth cried bitterly, and the , chamber was indeed full of lamentations and of wee. ' Duckworth cried like a child. The interview lasted a longtime, and at its conclusion Duckworth had regained more of his nerve. He protested again his absolute I ignorance of the whole tragedy, rather than his inno- cence. On hurrying from the prison gates, Mrs. Duck- worth and the woman who accompanied her wept copiously, but the children manifested no distress, being apparently altogether unconscious of their where- abouts, or the terrible fate hanging over their father. The Duckworths were very reserved about their visit to their unhappy relative, and anyone of them being asked for some detail replied, " Theaw's gotten enough, and theaw'll get no more."</p>
        <p>At Zwartsluis, in Holland, on Saturday night, there was some rioting by the unemployed, and in the melte, a man was shot dead by a police officer.</p>
