      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>ALLEGED MANSLAUGHTER Al WAR RINGTO N.</head>
        <p>THE ALLEGED MANSLAUGHTER Al WAR RINGTO N.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Police Court, on Saturday morning, before Mr. Burgess and Mr. L. Voisey, Michael Connelly was charged on suspicion ; with having caused the death of James Hostey by kicking or striking him in Mersey-st., on Tuesday night, December 27th. John Hostey, brother of the deceased, stated that his brother died on Thursday afternoon at four o'clock. On Tuesday he and his brother were in a public-house in I Mersey-st.,when two men named Collins and Cain came in and began quarrelling and making a disturbance. ' They were ejected by the landlord, and witness and the I deceased went out shortly afterwards and walked to- wards Warrington Bridge. When near the end of the street they met Collins and Cain. Collins went up to wit- ness's brother,knocked him down,and then almost "kick- ed him to death before his (witness's) eyes." Be (Collins) might have finished his work in the street, as the de- ceased was never able to stand up until his death. Stephen Collins stated that he had a quarrel in a public-house in Mersey-street with Cain, and as a conse- quence they were turned out into the street. When near to"Warrington Bridge, the deceased and his brother came up,and the prisoner Connelly without a word went up to the deceased and gave him a violent blow in the mouth, which felled him to the ground. The case was here adjourned, bail not being granted.</p>
