      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <p>WEI&gt;II EISTEDDF.OD AT ST. 4ELIINS.</p>
        <p>The • Welsh residents Of St. Helens held their -second annual eisteddfod in the St. Helene Town Hall, op Monday. The proceedings commenced at • half-past two o'clock and continued, with a short interval for tea, until ten o clock at night, the s'• ' Programme containing no fewer than twenty-two t 1 items. The. chief choral competition was "Worthy • • IS the Lamb" (Handel), for which a prize of £lO Was offered, and there was a brass band contest. 4 "Pride of Wales" (Round), for Which .£lO was - , also offered. The other competitions were as • ' follows:—Cornet solo, "All through the night" ;., • ~ (Round) ; choral competition for children, "The ' Trumpet shall sound (Dr. Parry) ; pianoforte ;•'^ '• Solo competition for children under twelve years -*at age; alto solo competition, "Entreat me not to '-• , leave thee" (Gounod) ; violin competition " Seren- atle " ((louno(1) ; tenor solo, " Oh, that summer •'•7:,,, , trailed for aye" (W. Davies) ; duet competition, 'Howell and Blodmen " (Dr. Parry) ; bass solo,' 'The Tempest" (R. S. Hughes) ; Alaw Cymreig ,•,','' . (Welsh air) " Serch Iludol " with piano accom- ,paniment ; soprano competition, "Angels ever • 1 , bright and fair in F (Handel). There were also ..:• ' • Prizes for poetry, for an essay on the history of St. :4: Helens, and for handwork. In the afiernoen -there was a fair attendance, When .- the Rev. W. Wynn Davies, of Bolton (conductor), ap- .7i rued on the platform, accompanied by Mr. H. Beton- ' - Karr, M.P. for Bt. Helens, Councillor J. B. Leach, Major •. Vl.Te,,r, and other gentlemen. A piano solo by Miss Pamela Barfoot, of St. Helens,.having been rendered, Councillor Leach stated that a friend of his claimed , that the Welsh language was the first language spoken, , . .. but he (Mr. Leach) had to confess if that were so, his 'education had been very much neglected. (Laughter.) ; lie would have liked to address a few words in Welsh</p>
        <p>to those present, but he was not able to do so. He had 14 „. -Much pleasure in introducing the• member for the , :•.' borough to that meeting. (Cheers.) '-' ' • Mr. Seton-Karr said he understood that gathering was to encourage music and literature and scientific pur- suit&amp; He had to plead the same ignorance as Mr. Leach, and was sorry he could neither sing a Welsh song nor make a Welsh speech. At the same time it was a pleasure to assist in promoting anything which tended to advance literature, music arid art. (Cheers.) He thought he might fairly congratulate the Welshmen of St. Helens on promoting that gathering. The profits of the gathering would go to the Welsh Chapel fund, which they all felt was a very good object, and not only was it good for that purpose, but there were other coneidera- tions. It showed that the people of St. Helens could think of other things besides trade and commerce. A friend of his thought St. Helens people did nothing more than build tall chimneys and make nasty smells. (Laugh- ter.) That friend was under the impression that the People of St. Helens spent most of their time in produc- m. commodities like glass and chemicals and medicines, and taking rools from the bowels ef the earth. All iliostapursuits were very well in their way, and they "lust work if they must have bread, but he must point sut that this was an age in which they also gave time to higher and more intelligent pursuits. That meeting *owed the people of St. Helens could, and did, think W ether things besides hard work. (Cheers.) It waa atit the names of millionaires, such as Jay Gould that lived in history, but the names of men of literature, such as Shakespeare, of composers, of painters,of artists, anel of singers. He could therefore say with all . eincerity that it gave him great pleasure to be present. Re spoke as a Scotchman when he said it was only right to recognise that in Wales they bad a nation Much had always been pre-eminent for its musical ability and for its muds and poets. He willingly ren- dared his tribute to the fact that the Welsh nation were leaders in this respect. (Cheers.) The programme was then proceeded with. ... _ _The awards were as follows :—Brass band contest, Milord Band, conductor Mr. T. German; the Gossage r)ap Works Band, the Nutgrove Band, and the Earles- • c)"tia Viaduct Band were the other competitors for this Prize. Cornet solo, Lewis Wilson, Manchester. Choral _ "ampetition for children, Welsh Chapel, Hardshaw-at., et. Helens. Welsh essay (I), the Rev. W. R. Parry, St. Helens; (2), Mr. Peter l'homas, Ashton. Children's ' raneforte solo, Miss Lucy Clarke a pupil of Miss Bar- foot, St. Helens. Alto solo, Miss Kate Jones, Manchester. The Mayor of St. Helens (Mr. Arthur Sinclair), who , !resided in the evening, said his first connection with l'iht meetine was in hemg asked to adjudicate in one of milpetitions relating to an essay on the" History of , r rielens," and as he was one of those who Ind lived ;Vest in the town he thought it a reasonable request, , the oe. which he undertook with great pleasure. Aid taltne, be Mayor of the town, he was very willing to part in a meeting • mainly composed of people of Welsh birth or descent. They knew that Liverpool was 'ed the capital of Wales in England, and he believed after Liverpool there was no town in England in which there was such a large proportion of the population of Welsh birth or descent as in St.Heleas. He was not sur- Prised that the Welslincen of St. Helens were anxious to keep up among themselves the memories of their glorious literature. Though they as Englishmen could not read it, they know how highly it was appreciated among them- Selves. (Cheers.) The programme then proceeded, 'With the following results :—Chief choral contest, "Worthy is the Lamb," Mr. J. T. Elliott's choir, St. Helens. This was the chief competition of the meeting, and occupied an hour and a half. The folic wing choirs tittered, and took part: Parr (St. Helens) Primitive Methodist augmented, Cambrian Choir Peasley Har- ironic Society, Liverpool Harmonic Society, Tyldesley Choral Union. and Mr. J. T. Elliott's Choir. Violin, ktster Willie Molyneux, St. Helens; antimacassar, rim divided between Mrs. Shoemaker (" Dore* " 9 ,„.Iverpool, and Miss Richard (" Mary .of Anglesey " , :yarrington ; four o'clock tea-cloth, Miss Pritchard, vyarrington ; tener solo Mr. J. T. Elliott, St. Helens ; wle on the late Rev. C.'''. Spurgeon. H. Isgaer Lewis, (3." Collwyn "), Port Diriorwic, Carnarvon ; duet, Miss anet Jones and Mr. Richard William Jones, Manches- , tee •. gentisman's hand-uiade stockings, Mr. Roberts, r.arlestewn ; hand-made white shirt, Mrs. Hughes, ilangor ; bass solo, Mr. Price Davies, Liverpool ; .°l3Tailo, Miss Janet Jones (" Claudia "), Manchester; ' History of St. Helens," Mr. James Brockbank. le• Helens. The adjudicators were Mr. J. T. Rees, lgus. 1 t:ea Ahersytwith ; Mr. J. 'O. Shepherd, Liverpool; ; g° Rev. W. W 5 one Davies, Belton ; and the Mayor of ' t b • Helens ; (34r. A., Sinclair). The prizes were distri- it,ed in the afternoon by Miss Leach, Of 'St. aLene, and in the evening by Miss Morris, of --orti&amp; i .</p>
        <p>41C INQUIRY THAT WENT TOO FAR.</p>
        <p>It isn't well to be too inquisitive, as the Queen of the ' 'aillndwicli Islands has just found out. Her deceased nshand—King Hermann I.—was a Dalmatian, and it ZrPears she has been always anxious to learn something the relations of her deceased spouse, and commis- every captain of an Austrian ship who visited 44"er to enquire after them and send her word. At last, „Ye a Vienna correspondent, the officers of the Fasana, ftorted to the Government on the subject, have „len successful. Hut among the relations of the de- thy have found a wile, or rather widow, who, as ,720 as she heard how matters stood, declared she would idoaed Queen to obtain the private property IT her runaway husband.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>GROWTH OF CO-OPERATION.</head>
        <p>THE GROWTH OF CO-OPERATION.</p>
        <p>The fact that the Fales of the English Co-operative Wholesale Society smount in the last quarter to £2,570,000, or £122,000 over the amount in the corres- ponding quarter Of laOt year, is a remarkable illustration of the vigour of the organisation. The Society, how- ever, does not show sadecided a growth as the Scottish Co-operative Wholesale Society. As a fact during the last twelve months the Scottish co-operators have shown considerable octivitv. The English Society now err.- ploys altogether 5433 persons, or 484 more than were employed last year, but the necessities of the Scottish trade have required the addition of no fewer than 622 persons to the staff. Altogether the Scottish Society now employs 2,828 persons. In a recent twelve months that Soddy Daid away nearly £3,400 as bonus on labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>LIFE-304T SERVICES IN 1892.</head>
        <p>LIFE-304T SERVICES IN 1892.</p>
