        <p>The life-boattOf the Royal National ilie.-Wavtin-ititu- tion, of which ti,re are now 305, have been unusually busy during thE past year, which has heen a stormy and unsettled on. The boats, manned by their gallant coxswains and eews,were called out out service as many as 335 times, rfulting in the rescue of 834 persons from the peril ofirowninz, and, humanly speaking, in the majority of eyes from death itself. This grand list of services of thute-boats includes the saving of much valuable proper; thirty-one vessels having been res- cued from total t partial destruction ; a great deal of assistance was ao given to fishing boats, which, had it not been for thrld of the life-boats, would have been unable to make a harbours and would in all prtbabilitV have been wrecke, Besides the launches resulting In the saving of life t property, the life-boats were sent out as many as 18times in response to signals of dis- tress, or what wersupposed to be such, only to find that the signals lv been improperly made or that the vessels had succee(d in getting out of danger. Rewards were also granted I the institution during the year for the saving of 220 lime by means of shore-boats, fishing boats, &amp;c., bringinup the total number of lives for the saving of whichhe society granted rewards in 1892 ito 1,058, and to 37,8 since its establishment in 1824. The cost of maintalng the institution's fleet of life- boats is very heavy,nd the ,annual subscriptions and assured income are kogether inadequate for the put, pose. More help is ,orefore much needed.</p>
