      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Zettero to the ebttor.</head>
        <p>Zettero to the ebttor.</p>
        <p>- - - SMALLPDX HOSPITAL NURSES. Sir,—May I suggest that a substantial testimonial be given to all who have been employed in this distressing service. I shall be pleased to subscribe five guineas for this purpose, and trust that the Mayor will take the matter up at once and take charge of and dist:ibute the amount received.—Yours, Sm., THOMAS FLE'rCHER. Grappenhall.</p>
        <p>THE HOPE HOSPITAL.-A PATIENT'S TESTIMONY.</p>
        <p>Sir,—! shall thank you very much if you will allow me a little space in your valuable and interesting paper as regards the treatment I have received during the period of six weeks that I have been in the Hope Hos- pital. I was surprised on coming out of the hospital, a few days ago, to learn as I did, that rumours had been afloat giving the Hope Hospital and all connected with it a very bad name therefore,as a thoroughly cured per- son, I feel it to be my duty to contradict these false and unjust rumours, which I have found out are set about by people who have had the good fortune not to be afflicted with this disease. I am sure during the time I have been in the "Hope" I have had no cause to complain, nor have I heard any complaints from my fellow patients ; and I am sure I don't know how to return my heartiest thanks to Dr. Gornall, jun., and the nurses, and, in fact, all connected with the hospital. The cheers which were given by the patients for the nurses and Drs. Goruall, jun. and seri., on Christmas Day.were plain proofs that they were held in high esteem by the patients. I think a word of praise ought to be given to the Rev. Mr. Willis and Father Femey for paying the poor sick patients visits occasionally. In conclusion, I (-rice more wish to return my sincere thanks to the staff at the "Hope," and wishing all the patients a speedy recovery, I remain, yours very truly, 37, Warburton-St.. Warrington.</p>
        <p>w. MAKIN.</p>
        <p>A LEGACY FROM BARBARISM. "They jest at scars who never felt the wound." Sir,—The gravity and importance of the subject again emboldens me to trespass upon your space, in order to Vead the cause of our "poor relations" the animals. To my mind, this cause commends itself more forcibly as being that of the weak and defenceless; the plaint of the victim against the persecutor; the cry of the innocent unhappy for protection from the atrocious treatment of</p>
        <p>. _ their "unnatural enemies." It is reserved to man, who has imagined a hell for such of his kind as differ from him in certain opinions, to have also the unenviable distinction of creating a real hell upon earth for certain of his fellow-beings who happen to be at once innocent of offence and defence. Does it not seem a cruel anomaly,that in this last decade of the 19th century—in the midst of our boasted ad- vanced civilisation—atrocities should be perpetrated by legal license, which would have been reveltin,g to the humanity of ruder ages? We have come to see crimes most inhuman perpetrated upon animals, in the name, and ostensibly, for the benefit of humanity. Surely it is high time the vivisector should be cited before the bar ef public opinion, to show cause why his malpractices be not interdicted ? A practice which can show no moral right for its existence,and against which so much of moral wrong is imputed, should have no mercy from those who have the least tinge of righteousness and a fair sense of moral responsibility. I have asked, "Whence does the vivisector get his " right " ? The only answer, as yet, has been, "Its utility." I have shown where the utility plea would lead, if admitted ; in the murder of infected people for the benefit of th.?. community ! The only " right" a vivisector has for his malpractices is the old, old, right, now so deservedly in disrepute, the right of might. He has the "power" to do it, and having lost, or never hav- ing had, the sense of the moral iniquity he is perpetrat- ing,uses that power,which you and I, reader,have given him, remorselessly to embitter the latter end of the living " materials of investigation." Surely, the more advanced and reasoned morality of our age will Rot recognise this legacy from barbarism as a right? Tbe more I 'I fleet upon this most painful subject the more the conviction is borne in upon me that we, the electors of England, are they upon whose shoulders the responsibility rests for the continuance of these atroci- ties. As to the vivisectors themselves, they are so " interred " in their study as to have lost sight of its moral iniquity. The man who is digging the well sees only the inside of his subject, and is perhaps a good authority on matters pertaining to the interior of the well but outsiders are perhaps better qualified to form a judgment on toe general bearing of the work with other objects. I have come to regard the practisers of the living dissection of animals as the victims of a mild form of monomania,who have lost their sense of moral respon- sibility;in this matter. Just as Calvin, in the matter of Servetus, and the auto-da-fi-ists generally, while being men actuated by what we may consider the highest motives, were yet, when weighed against our higher standard of morality, the perpetrators and instigators of Most horrible crimes. We do not now think a difference of opinion ought to subject a man to death in its most terrible form. But the morally less advanced opinion of the 16th:century condoned the evil of the means for the sake of the assumed good of the end. Now we, clearer sighted, do not think the end justified the means ; and should undoubtedly use more than moral suasion to pre- vent a repetition of Calvin's crime. As we now regard the Calvin-Servetus episode, so will posterity regard vivisection and our attitude towards it ; it will undoubtedly hold us responsible if we do we not put the restraints of civilization upon a practice that disgraces that civilization. It is charitable to suppose Calvin and the Inquisitors to have been monomaniacal on their sub- jects ; equally so with the vivisectors. They are the victims of their convictions; and we know that the home of the strongest—if most unfounded—convictions is in asylums, for the reception and retention of the victims of over-strong convictions. How can anyone aver that " His tender mercies are over all His works " while such iniquities occur ?—Yours, &amp;c., JOHN PEERS. January 1, 1/393.</p>
        <p>HOW THE POOR LINE IN WIDNES.</p>
        <p>DISGRACE TO WIDNES CORPORATION. Yesterday afternoon at Widnes Accident Hospi- tal, Mr. F. A. Jones, deputy coroner, held an inquiry into the death of Catherine Burke, aged three years, daughter of Michael Burke, chemical labourer, 71 Lugsdale-road, who died in that in- stitution on Wednesday from the effects of burns sustained on the previous day, while play- ing with a piece of lighted paper. The evi- dence revealed the existence of a shocking state of affairs at the girl's home. It appeared that the house was a tenement consisting of two small rooms on the ground floor ; and a family lived in each room. The front room was occupied by a family named Mulloy, consisting of the father and mother and five children, aged respectively 23. 13, 12, 9, and 6 years. There was no bedding nor bed clothing, and the inmates had to sleep as best they could. The eldest son, it was stated, was allowed to sleep on the table beneath the window "because he was delicate and it was warmer there." Sometimes a Sheet was thrown over him but frequently he had no covering whatever. In the back room lived Burke, his wife, and four children, and their habitation was as wretched as the other part of the house. B, th rooms were stated to be in an exceedingly filthy coadition. The Deputy Coroner said this was a shocking state of affairs and a disgrace to Widnes Cor- poration. He had never heard a more shocking storp. —Councillor Smith who was a member of the jury, said he had no ides that there existed such a terrible state of affairs in the town, and he assured the jury that he would bring the matter before the Health Committee at the first meetin,.—The jury returned a verdict of accidental death.</p>
        <p>emigran s to New York, Boaton Philadelphia, San Francisco, Cana, China, Japan, Aus- tralia, or New aland, should take ti ..kets (lowest; rateo) at. the •• Examiner" Office, by Guion. American. Australian, Oriental, ni other lines.—John Walkt,r, ;gent ,"Examia,r ' °Mee. Warrington.—Advt.</p>
