      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <p>MUNIFICENT GIFT TO WARRINGTON. £5,000 FOE A NEW PUBLIC HALL. Before proceeding to the ordinary business of the Warrington Town Council, on Tuesday evening, the Mayor took the opportunity of wishing all present a happy and prosperous new year, and he expressed a hope that the town would be more fortunate in the coming year than it had been in the one just passed. He was pleased to hear from the Medical Officer that the disease was now almost completely mastered, and that there were prospects and hopes for a. bright new year. He then said: Gentlemen, I have a communication to read to you which, I am sure, will give you, as well as the public of War- rington, much gratification. The letter is dated December 24th, and reads as follows Dear Mr. Mayor,—lt has long been my wish to offer to the town of Warrington, with which I have been so intimately as- sociated all my life, some recognition of the kindly feel- ing and mutual consideration which I am happy to be- ; lieve have marked our long and successful business con- nection, a connection which I am proud to feel, has linked the name of Parr with the commercial prosperity and development of the town. I think the opportunity now presents itself in the need which seems to ecist, for a Publio Hall, and it will give me much pleasure if the town will accept the sum of £5,000, which, lam told, I will suffice for the accomplishment of this object. It ; gives me much additional satisfaction to be able to take advantage of this opportunity ; under your Mayoralty.—Believe nie, yours very faithfully, .1. CHARLTON PARE."—(Loud applause.)— Well, gentlemen, I move that Mr. Charlton Parr's ex- ceedingly kind offer shall be referred to the General Purposes Committee, and also that the warmest thanks ; of this meeting on behalf of the town of Warrington, be accorded Mr. Parr for his generous present. (Applause.) ' I have answered Mr. Parr's letter in an unofficial man- ner, and I hope I have only done what was right uuder the circumstances. (Hear, hear.) Alderman HOLMES had the greatest pleasure in second- ing the Mayor's proposition. He considered the present made them by Mr. Parr was the most acceptable one that could have been conferred upon the public, for they were now almost entirely at a standstill for a Public Hall. Alderman SUTTON in supporting the resolution said the gift was a most valuable and handsome one, and was one which they would not have the slightest hesit- ation in accepting. He thought that if the Council had been asked to suggest the form of the present they could not have done so in a more suitable way than Mr. Parr had done it, or in a more serviceable way than he had done. The town was solely in need of a place for public meetings and concerts, &amp;c., and lie felt sure that the generous donor would be earning public gratitude, by the present he had made to the town. Councillor H. ROBERTS said that before the resolution was put to the Council, he desired to express personally his deep obligation to Mr. Parr for his handsome gift. If they had been asked to make the selection he thought they would unanimously have selnted as the most suit- able and serviceable present, a Public Hall. (Applause.) It was frequently the case when wealthy men made pre- sents to a town to attach to their gift restrictions and conditions which often marred much of the real value of their donations. This particular gift, however, was unaccompanied by any such restrictions, and the menev was to be spent simply for the erection ot a new Public Hall, which was to be for the public use and benefit of the citizens of Warrington; and of a certainty they would find it serviceable. They had undoubtedly been in con- siderable difficulties respecting the substitution of a public place for meeting el the old Public Hall ; and there had been a deputation to that Council asking for some endeavours to be made to meet the demand. But they had been unable to cope with it, and the question had been left as an embarrassingly open one. The General Purposes Committee had I shrunk from making any suggestion, and just as the matter was likely to fall into neglect, Mr. Parr made the generous and welcome offer. (Ap- plause.) He was sure that the sincere thanks of the I whole town were due to the kind donor. Councillor FLe3rEso,who also supported the resolution, said he had, like many of the public of Warrington, heard that a present was going to be made. But he had been unaware of the form that present was to take. First he thought "Maybe it will be a church." (A voice : Ora chapel) yes, or a chapel. Then he thought, " No, that would not be right ;" and he thought again, but nothing he thought of was half so suitable as this magnificent offer which Mr. Parr had made. (Ap- plause.) It had come at the right moment, and deserved their warmest thanks. (Applause.) The resolution was put to the meeting and carried with acclamation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE</head>
        <p>THE GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE</p>
        <p>AND THE GIFT.</p>
        <p>The General Purposes Committee will meet on Monday, with the Mayor presiding, to c,,nsider the obligation of Mr. Parr's donations of .E5,1:00 for a new Public Hall. It is understood that the Cor- poration intend to provide a site somewhere. A suggestion has been made to the Committee that ths erection should be made abutting the Town Hall. As there is a movement now afoot to centralize all the municipal buildings, including the Police Court, this suggestion will probably meet with proper consideration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>WA RRINGTON UNION RURAL SANITARY AUTHORITY.</head>
        <p>WA RRINGTON UNION RURAL SANITARY AUTHORITY.</p>
        <p>--- The monthly meeting of this Authority was held in the Mayor's Parlour at tke Town Hail, on Thurs- day morning. There were present Messrs. R. Heaton (chairman), R. Owen, W. Houghton, T. Smith, J. Houghton, J. Kilshaw, J. Dignum, S. Sutton (clerk), J. C. Sutton, W. Owen (surveyor) ; Dr. Seph ton (medical officer) ; and Kerfoot (nuisance inspector). THE SMALLPDX EPIDEMIC. The Clerk read a letter from the Local Government I Board stating that they had received intimation of the outbreak of smallpox in Liverpool Road near the !boundary of the borough, and forwarding a copy of the official memorandum as to the steps requisite to be taken in places where smallpox was prevalent. The Board also requested the Authority to instruct their medical officer to report upon the recent prevalence of the disease in their district with regard to non-fatal as well as fatal cases, and the mea- sures to be taken for checking the spread of the disease. The Board would be glad to receive a copy of the report. —The Medical Officer said there had only been eight cases since the outbreak, all convalescent except two, and all of them had come from the borough. He would prepare a report.—The Medical Officer's report stated that during the month there had been three smallpox cases (one at Sankey Bridges, one at Grappenhall, and one at Hollins Green). The case at Grappenhall was removed to the Hope Hospital, Warrington. He tried both at Warrington and Monsall to get the Hollins Green case removed to hospital, but could not get her ad- mitted. She was now quite well. URBAN POWERS FOR THE AI7THORITY. A letter was read from the Local Government Board in reply to a letter of the 20th, with reference to the Author- ity's contemplated application by the authority for an order investing them with certain urban powers for the whole of their district. The Board would only be pre- pared to entertain the application as regarded such existing streets, or parts of such streets as had already been sewered, and in respect of which proceedings were to be taken forthwith. The authority would not be I invested by the Board's orders with power to require owners and occupiers to sewer such streets. The Clerk said it was not quite certain at the last meet- ing under which Act of Parliament the Authority should proceed as to street-making, Mr. Owen and he had dis- cussed the matter since, and obtained from Mr. Whittle a copy of the Private Street Works Act, 1892, and of his report upon the Act.—The CLERK read the report,which showed the advantages of proceeding under the Act.— The CLERK advised the authority to pass a resolu- tion asking the Looal Government Board to give the Authority the urban powers,and at the same time adopt the Act of 1892.—A resolution was passed accordingly. —The CLERK read a letter from the Local Government Board, forwarding two copies of an order investing the Rural Sanitary Authority with certain powers under which districts would have to pay for their own , 1 ASKPIT CT EANING AT PENKETH.—EXTRAORDINARY COINCID- ENCE 01' TENDERS. The Clerk read tenders from Mr. Joseph Eccles and Mr. James Fisher, of Penketh, for cleaning the ashpits and closets in that township, both undertaking to do the work for £BO per annum.—lt was considered that this sum was too much and the Authority decided to re-advertise for tenders, and request Mr. Grimshaw to do the work in the meantime.—Subsequently amidst some laughter, a tender was read from Mr. Grimshavv, [which was also £BO.</p>
        <p>THE CEMETERY QUESTION. The Clerk read a reply from the Local Government Board sanctioningthe borrowing of £2OO for the erection of a mortuary chapel for Rixton-with-Glazebroolc ceme- tery. The Clerk said as understood at last meeting, the Authority did not intend to make use of this permission, under present circumstances, nntil they were forced.— The Clerk read a letter he had received from Mr. James Davies, Hollinfare, a member of the Rixton Parochial Committee. He stated that there were yet four signa- tures required in the case of the proposed burial ground, instead of merely one as stated at last meeting of the Authority. There appeared to be no small amount of dissatisfaction in the place with respect to their action in the matter, and he suggested that the Authority call a meeting of the Parochial Committee, or, preferably, of the ratepayers on the subject. In this way he thought they might get out of the present deadlock.—The Clerk read his reply to Mr. Davies, pointing out that the Auth- ority could not call a meeting as desired, and that arrangements for purchase of the land for the cemetery were about completed, and if the parish had its rati</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 7, 1893.. EFFECTS OF THE FROST AT W ARRINGTON. CROSSING TIIE RIVER ON ICE FLOES.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 7, 1893.. EFFECTS OF THE FROST AT W ARRINGTON. CROSSING TIIE RIVER ON ICE FLOES.</p>
        <p>increased for this purpose without advantage from the expenditure, it would solely be caused by the religious quarrels within the parish. The Clerk read a letter from Mr. Davies in reply to this, expressing the opinion that Mr. Roberts as Chairman of the Authority could call a meeting of ratepayers.—Mr. J. C. Surrou stated that the Authority was taking the proper legal course, and must not be hindered in that course by difficulties caused by religious dissensions.—Ths Medical Officer made a statement as to the dissatisfaction he had on a recent visit found to prevail in the parish on the ceme- tery question, and mentioned a suggestion made to him that the unapplied £3OO left by Lord Winmarleigh should be devoted to burial ground purposes.—The Clerk read a letter from Mr. Heyes, giving his reasons for not assenting to the Cemetery scheme.—The Clerk advised the Autkority to borrow the money for the pur- chase of the laud and proceed.—The Medical Officer stated that in the existing churchyard bodies were be- ing buried close to the walls, though according to law they ought not to be buried within three feet of the wall.—The Clerk submitted tenders for the loan of the purchase money and that of Messrs. Charles Dagnall and Co. for the loan at 3i per cent, was accepted.</p>
        <p>and bearing skaters. The great place for the past few weeks has been the unopened river diversion at Arpley. People about town who havn't been there, didn't go either because they couldn't skate or had no skates. Then they stopped at home and complained of the distressing, cruel cold in their 11 heads. It has been as entertaining as going to a I th</p>
        <p>ieatre, watching the skaters there any night dur- ng the week. The scene was almost fairy-like. The old moon shone his benignant smile around, and made things float in a sea of quicksilver, as it were. It wasn't too light or too dark. You could see there on the lonely spot on the far bank the tall youth sailing majestically along ; then a little way ahead is a maid. She isn't an adept pro- bably, or she is troubled with fainting tits, else why should she reel like that? But no matter, the kind youth has seen her danger, he lends her his arm to lean on, and she makes it a permanent sup- port for the evening. In another spot can be seen the aspirants, plunging here and there, ramming their poor heads against the ice,and point- ing their legs out to heaven, all so that they may get the hang of" how to skate." Then right in the centre of the deviation you generally find a ring of some two hundred people watching the airy per- formance of some crack skater, J. Lockett, or some one else.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCILLORS AND THEIR LONDON TRIPS. Councillor FLEMING at the monthly meeting of the Warrington Corporation on Tuesday night, pro- posed, pursuant to notice,that the following resolu- tion be rescinded :—" That members of the Council be allowed .£2 per day and first class railway fares when engaged, in London on Corporation business, and first class fares and out-of-pocket expenses when so engaged elsewhere outside the borough." He said he opposed strongly the resolution that had been adopted on this subject because he did not think that any member of the Corporation should be allowed to make a charge except expenses out of pocket. He might just travel first class or third if he liked ,but for his part,in order to keep up the dignity of the Council he should like to see th -m travel first. (Hear, hear.) He begged to move that instead of this .£2 a day and first class railway expenses, the members of the Council be allowed to charge.— (Cries of" No.") Alderman SUTTON : You must stick to the motion. Councillor SHAW GREEN : Tell us what is sufficient. Councillor FLEMING said he considered it sufficient to allow all out of pocket expenses. Councillor SHAW GREEN : Go to a guinea a day. Councillor FLEMING said he would not say that, but out of pocket expenses would not come to £2 a day. Councillor ROBERTS seconded. He said he had per- sistently and consistently opposed in the General Pur- poses Committee the motion to which he now took ex- ception, and he should only be consistent in taking up the same position that night. He objected to the principle of any member of the Council being in a position to make a profit of a single penny—(loud applause)—when he was sent out on business, repre- senting the Corporation. It was perfectly well under- stood that gentlemen when they were elected to seats there promised faithfully to serve the electors to the utmost of their capacity for love of the work. Therefore when a rule was laid down that any member of that Council engaged on Corporation ' business in London, should be allowed a sum out of which, if he liked, he could make a very handsome profit, a temptation was held out which was derogatory to the best interests of the members of the Council, un- fair to the ratepayers, and likely soon or late to bring trouble in its train. (Applause.) No one, of course, would like the Mayor to go to London and be a penny out of pocket, and it was probable that in some cases, in the Mayor's for instance, £2 a day would not be suffi- cient to enable him to carry on the business in a manner befitting the-importance of that town, so that it would operate disadvantageously to restrict him to that amount. On the other hand they could not lay down a principle for allowing a certain sum for members of the Corporation generally. he result of carrying out such a principle might be—he did not say this was the case at present—that members of the Corporation might some day absolutely race for appointments on deputti- tions—(A voice : " They do now," and laughter)—for the purpose of having a cheap trip at the expense of the ratepayers. It was only about two years ago that the sum expended upon deputations from that Corporation come to over £4OO in a single year. That was most extravagent and uncalled for. (Hear, hear.) The business of the town never required so large an expenditure. What they were entitled to was the same treatment they would expect from private employers. He, as a teetotaller, could go to London as cheap as or cheaper than any member of the Council could do. (" Question.") At the same time he did not say that members of the Council who were not teetotallers should be held strictly to the teetotal pledge all the time they were away. He did not wish to force his principles down the throat of everybody else, but members of the Corporation should conduct themselves in London exactly as they would do in their own town. They should not expect to feed and guzzle at the expense of the rate- payers of Warrington, which they would not indulge in their own town. The resolution should be rescinded,and each man sohuld be put on his honour when he went away to represent the Corporation to do what was right and what was consistent with his duty to the town. Alderman FRANCMIII3 reminded the Council that the chief part of the £4OO referred to by Councillor Roberts was spent on witnesses in promoting a Bill in the House of Commons. (Hear, hear.) Councillor Roberts had said they did not expect members of the Corporation to guzzle when they went to London in deputations, but the happy expression on his face showed this was only a joke. Some people outside might think that mem- bers of the Council went to London to have sprees. He had never seen anything wrong on the part of deputa- tions when with them in London. Councillor ROBERTS assured the Council that he used the expression in joke. Councillor SHAW GREEN considered £2 a day too much and that a limit should be fixed, as in the cases of the Corporation officials. Councillor BOLTON: I should certainly think this resolution has been put on by somebody who really wanted to go to London and has never been. (Laugh- ter.) Councillor FLEMING : That is out of order. I have been going to London for 25 years, and I know more about it than any gentleman sitting round this table. (Laughter.) Igo often enough. Councillor BOLTON said one member of the Parliamen- tary' Committee said he could make a trifle of profit at a guinea a day and he thought a member of the Council ought to do his duty without profit. Councillor BROWNE supported Councillor Fleming's motion. As to the Mayor he had to keep up the dignity of the town, and no sum should be fixed. But if other members of the Council received the £2 per day and it could be proved that they had not spent that they would be liable to a penalty. First class railway fares, and what was legitimately spent out of pocket should be allowed. Councillor BOLTON : What do you call legitimate ex- penses? The Alhambra? Councillor PLIMPTON said when they went to the ship Clio the expenses were £1 a day, including railway fare I and boats. If a member were allowed first-class tare he i ought to travel first, not third-class, and put the diffe- rence in his pocket. He considered the present allow- ance extravagant. A deputation recently went to Lop- don on a Friday, and it was pretty near evening when I they got there, and charged expenses for Friday, Satur- day, aud Monday, though all the work could have been I done in one day. It would come very expensive to the ratepayers if all deputations acted like that. The cost should not exceed a guinea a day. Councillor SMETHURST Supported Councillor Fleming's resolution. He endorsed the remarks of previous speakers, and referring to an eighteenpenny excursion be had had with several members to Liverpool and Birkenhead, said if all deputations of that kind were allowed to charge £2 a day and first-class expenses, it would be a godsend to all of them. (Laughter.) Councillor Straw supported Councillor Fleming as the representative of St. John's Ward. (A Voice : Hallam- lane). , Alderman SUTTON thought an amount of jealousy had been imported into the question by members who never had the opportunity of going to London. (Much laughter.) Councillor Fleming indignantly, and amidst loud laughter, rose to order. Alderman SUTTON : As one if the cleverest and best members or the Council—(laughter)—l may say that I have not been deputed to go to London more than once since I was Mayor. Councillor SMETHURST : They won't have you there. Alderman SUTTON considered his services on that occasion were worth ten times the amount mentioned in the motion. He was always out of pocket when he went to London, and, although the resolution was in force, did not take advantage of it to the fullest extent. When he went to London on business he went to enjoy himself. He went to keep up the position of an Alderman of the borough, and did not drink a bottle of pop. (Loud laughter.) He preferred a bottle of wine. (Laughter.) In conclusion, Alderman Sutton said that when members acquired greater experience they would no doubt be sent on deputations to London to represent the borough(laugh- ter) and then they would be allowed to charge what was reasonable. The resolution on being put was carried unanimously. RESCUED FROM STARVATION.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Lord, a middle-aged woman, living in Blackburn- road, Haslingden, has been rescued from a terrible death. Her mind being unhinged, she fancied people wished to rob, harm, or murder her, and barricaded herself in her house. Under the advice of a doctor the house was broken into after a lapse of several days, and the woman was found in her bedroom in such a condition that she must have died of sheer starvation in a few hours. It is Delieved she will recover.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>THRILLING ADVENTURE OF A YOUTH.</head>
        <p>THRILLING ADVENTURE OF A YOUTH.</p>
        <p>THE SKATERS AT ARPLEY.</p>
        <p>Winter has been clasping us to her bosom with unwonted energy this week ; and as a consequence we have been left very cold and icy. It is con- sidered to be quite four years since we were blessed with such a keen, biting, visitation of frost. And probably it is much more than four years since King Hoar threw his cloak about us in such a pictures- que or pretty manner. There have been scenes in the neighbourhood during the past few days that have been almost Arctic in their semblance ; scenes to enchant the eye, and send the mind scurrying back for years to, the times of the good old winters.</p>
        <p>Taking it from a scenic point of view, a line or I two of description of the surroundings seen from Walton Arches may be interesting. Standing on this structure on Tuesday, and gazing towards Hill Cliff, a sight seldom to be seen presented itself. The sky was of a tender, clear blue, with no cloud to mar its vast grandeur; and the sun's rays covered everything with a shimmer, tinging the tops of the tall trees on Hill Cliff with Tdby tints, making them glisten with their coats of hoar, like dazzling pinnacles of brilliants ; the side of the hill looked like a pure white robe, and the windows of the few straggling houses shone with a liquid purple reflec- tion of the sun which set the robe off with royal splendour. Narroving the scene, the eye rested upon the length of the Ship Canal reaching to Tom Paine's Bridge. But this seemed like a dark mark on the surface of the frost ; like "a gash in the white bosom of mother earth."</p>
        <p>Then a little nearer came Morley Common, a desolate jagged patch: its boggy blackness, its stunted trees and willows ill-suiting the sun- bedazzled hoar cloak, and the whole making but a mean contrast with the majestic undulation which extended te the hill. It did look mean. More than that, in its contrast with that hill, it looked like a seedy second-hand coat alongside a Lord Mayor's robe. From that place the eye wandered across the river to Arpley meadows; a group of bare, cold, fields, partitioned by small white hedges, and dotted with a few starved horses, which stood soli- tary and lonely, with their heads between their forelegs, apparently gazing at their limp, hanging tails. One streak of animation enlivened this picture. It was the river deviation, which from end to end, was dotted witi* swift moving figures, swimming in the blissful sea of exhileration, common to skat- ing.</p>
        <p>We leave the bridge and get to the river bank going in the direction of Bank Quay. Here a scene, which has certainly not been observed before for years presented itself. The river from this spot down to Fiddler's Ferry, was blocked from side to side with a solid wedge of ice floes. It looked mag- nificent. There it was, as far as your eye could see, a great length of wobbling lumps of ice, the sun shining upon its glittering animation and making it look like some great serpentine monster. That was from a distance, but closer inspection made it look different. The floes —there were thousands and thousands of them—were struggling with each other on the tide like so many clumsy porpoises. Each one was large enough to hold a man ; the rush- ing water collided them together with sounds like pistol shots, and then they jambed against each other with a crunch; they swerved and collided with a report and a crunch again, and wobbled beneath the surface of the water and rose again. Here and there a mass of floes was crushed together near the hank, where their sharp jagged ends pro- truded from the water.</p>
        <p>This condition of things considerably interfered with the river traffic,andonTuesdaya striking picture of this interference was on view. Opposite the Spel- ter Works was one of Messrs. Fairclough's steamers ice bound, and unable to reach its destination at Latchford. It was firmly hemmed in on every side with huge pieces of ice and frozen snow. Away round the bend of the river near Monks, Hall, and Co.'s, works was another steamer in similar straits. The boats had come down the river on the pre- vious night's tide, and after brave attempts to force a passage, they had succumbed to the overpower- ing pressure of the floes on each side and laid" up for the remainder of the night. This was done, not so much because of the actual danger that existed of getting the ribs of the vessels" staved in" as in consequence of the overpowering solidity with which the floes were wedged together.</p>
        <p>In the afternoon of Tuesday a large crowd of persons gathered opposite the vessel near the Spelter works. They could be seen craning their heads forward in the direction of the boat, and anon a loud peal of laugher emanated from them. Closer inspection showed the cause of this. A man —possibly one of the crew—was proceeding to the vessel in a verynovel manner. He had procured two light planks. He placed one across two ice floes, and with the other in his arms walked cautiously along to the end of the oie on the ice. Then he would balance himself gingerly on one leg while he placed the other plank across. two more blocks of ice. Then he walked gently on to it turned round, after many demonstrative performancesin sustain- ing his balance, and pulled the other plank up, walked along to the end, placed the other plank down, and did the same thing again until he reached the steamer amidst the applause of the onlookers.</p>
        <p>The same afternoon a very exciting scene occurred about 300 yards from this spot. A boat known as "Old Jem " had been moored in an inlet for pro- tection against the blocks of ice ; around its stern and extending some feet into the river was a strong, solid heap of frozen snow and ice. A number of boys who had been observed playing about the boat during the afternoon began to devote their atten- tions to what is known in their particular parlance as" venturing." That is to say one endeavoured to distinguish himself by daring on to more dangerous parts of the ice than his companions. In the midst, of this one of the youths stepped on to a block of ice; the force of his jump was too heavy; the block became detached from the bank and glided into an open space of water.</p>
        <p>In a moment the youth realised his position. His face blanched with terror as he perceived himself drifting from the bank, and he gave vent to a series of piercing shrieks. There were several people about at the time. The boy's cries for help were heard,and two men came running down to the spot. When they were within a distance of a few yards, and as the boy's piercing screams were loudest, the detached block collided with a group of floes. There IFS a report and a crunch; then a splash and a mothered scream, and the next moment the boy was seen to disappear beneath the water. A few bubbles and eddies rose to the surface ; then the boy's head appeared, his eyes starting wildly as he beat the water in his panic, and screamed in a dis- tressing way. Then he sunk again ; more eddies and bubbles rose; and the two men waited anxi- ously until the head appeared again. It came, one of the men stepped deliberately on to a block of ice, stretched out the boot-hook and struck it into the drowning boy's coat. With a considerable amount of difficulty the youth was hauled ashore, terribly frightened—and wet—by his immersion. It was providential luck that the the two men arrived in time for otherwise there is not much doubt that the youth would Irive drifted beneath the floes and been drowned. His name was James Carruthers, staying in Church-st., with some relatives named Johnson. lie was taken home in a cab from Bank Quqy station. The name of the man who rescued him we did not hear, but he certainly ought to be looked up and rewarded in some way.</p>
        <p>Wednesday's big tide had 901310 effect upon the ice bound river. As it came rushing in at mid-day it absolutely split the great white compact. The blocks were sundered apart ; their colliding created reports like the fillets of a hundred rifles ; and through the dark opaque fog the heavy square blocks could be seen slowly moving, swirling, sink- ing, and colliding. Then the steamers—of which there were now five—began to get a chance. Their bondage of ice had been broken; they bad merely to steam through the loose floes. Hundreds of people were watching them, and a brave eight it was too. Through the fog the crew could be seen rushing with a tramp ! tramp ! about the deck ; then the cling-er-cling of the bell came, and a gruff voice would shout " Leer go." The engine could be heard at work; the churning of the paddles followed, and the vessel moved slowly away, the crew doing their best to protect her from the Roes by pushing them away with poles.</p>
        <p>Another instance of the effect of the frost is that the Ship Canal work is at a standstill owing to it; and that every piece of slow water is frozen</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>NOTES ON -GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON -GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>(3-RO. G. HUSSEY &amp; CO. are leading Makers of Poot Balls, Hockey, Golf, La Crosse, and requisites for Sports, Games and Gymnasia. Catalogue may be had on appli cation to the Sports Manufactory, Peckham Rye, S.R. Trade f-a. 13 CE Mark.</p>
        <p>Rugby football was at a discount in Widnes and Run- corn on Saturday; in consequence of the frost,the whole of the matches arranged had to be cancelled.</p>
        <p>The match between Swinton and Newport, which has been eagerly anticipated for some weeks, had been ar- ranged for decision at Swinton, but on inspecting the ground the Newport team declared it to be unfit for play, and the match was abandoned. This decision caused great disappointment, especially as it was con- sidered by many persons that the ground was in better condition than on Saturday when the match in the Lan- cashire Cup championship between Swinton and Wigan I was played.</p>
        <p>The position gained by Newport is remarkable. The I club has not been defeated for 48 consecutive matches, ' in which they have scored 845 points to 37 points, or 113 goals and 132 tries, against 3 goals and 9 tries. This season they have played 15 matches (14 wins and one draw), scoring 305 points ag,ainst 12 points. Swinton on the other hand have played 18 matches, and have won 15, drawn two and lost one. Their record is 133 points to 14 against. It should be explained, that a try counts three points in Wales and only two according to Eng- lish Rugby rules. The reputation of the clubs therefore warranted the anticipation that there would be a hard struggle for supremacy had the match taken place.</p>
        <p>Again it is necessary to repeat that football is "on." We have not even had the pleasure of a junior team match in town during the week end. This was dis- appointing. And as though just to vent the spleen of the town the first team went and disappointed the people at Wigan on Monday. It was a kind of one-sided " tit-for-tat.'</p>
        <p>The match was under the auspices of the Lancashire Competition, and the Wiganers anticipating a good game, had kept their ground in splendid condition. In the afternoon a large audience gathered upon it. And it waited. They had been occupied thus some consider- able time when they were told that only nine Warring- ton people had arrived to conduct the contest. A veil should be drawn across the remainder of the ptoceedings , on the field.</p>
        <p>I have a kind of prophetic presentment that Swinton are going to have that cup. Saturday's game at Wigan ended like all its predecessors, and the " lions " have now fifteen points to their credit. They also possess the highest aggregate of points scored, and are proudly conscious of the fact that only two goals and two tries have been registered against them in the whole of the eight matches. Broughton are hopelessly in the rear, and Oldham, having played ten matches and only won two, are not enjoying anything like the success we im- agined they deserve, and which the calibre of the team would warrant us believing they are capable of accom- plishing.</p>
        <p>The standing of the remainder of the clubs interested in this competition may be seen from the following table :</p>
        <p>RESULTS UP TO SATURDAY, DECEMBER 31. For. Agst.</p>
        <p>Pld. Won. Lost. Drn. G.T. G.T. Pts. Swinton 8 . 7 . 0 . 1 .. 8 13... 1 2 ...15 St. Helens Recreation 7 ... 5 ... 2 ... 0 ... 8 Q ... 2 6 ...10 Warrington Tyldesley Salford Oldham Rochdale Hornets Wi,zan Broughton Rangers Broughton lO . 1 . 7 . 2.. 3 2 ... 816 ... 4</p>
        <p>It seems that there has been another "kick up " between the St. Helens Recs. and the town club. From personal knowledge I kaow that the two combinations have assumed, for years, such warlike attitudes towards each other, as to make one suppose that a kind of football vendette had been bequeathed to one of the parties. They go about confronting each other with surly looks when nothing else offers. But there are times when the savage passion which corrodes their athletic bosoms bursts its bonds.</p>
        <p>These occasions are when one party wants to"make up" by suggesting a hot pot supper or asking for the loan of the others field. An instance of this happened on Saturday. The town club had a home fixture with the Manchester Rangers, but their field was too hard to play on. The Recs. were not playing so the town club asked for the loan of their field. The request was refused and the match had to be abandoned. All I can say is that this kind of" petty spite" is very unbecoming to both teams.</p>
        <p>Why,bless me, both clubs are becoming quite promin- ent, and ought to be far above such small jealousies. The " Athletic News" says of them both clubs have done splendidly this season, St. Helens in particular having a better record than can be remembered by the oldest member of the club, whilst the "Recs." have al- ready justified their claims to admission in the first group of Lanchirg clubs. Few towns can boast of such a record on the part of two leading clubs, which is here- with given : Played. Won. Drawn. Lost. St. Helens Recreation l6 ... 11 ... 1 ... 4 St. Helens</p>
        <p>The scoring power of the teams is also of a somewhat remarkable character, as will be seen from the following statistics derived from a reliable saurce : G. T. agst G. T. Points. St. Helena St, Helena Recreation</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>AIM.</head>
        <p>AIM.</p>
        <p>TRII NEW YBAR.—The new year was ushered in in the orthodox fashion. The Volunteers, in accordance with their usual custom,played at the Cross at midnight. The Church bells rang a merry peal, and watchnight services were held at all the places of worship. A WEEK OF PRAYER.—A special week of prayer, pro- moted by the Evangelical Union, was commenced at Lymm on Tuesday evening, at Zion Chapel, when the proceedings were conducted by Mr. Edwin Brown. TRAP ACCIDENT.—Lieut. Groves met with an adven- ture on Saturday night which might have been attended with serious results. He was driving through Lymrn in a trap, to which was attached a very spirited animal, and when near the canal bridge he was observed dashing along at a great speed. On descending the incline, in endeavouring to turn to one side to avoid an obstacle that was in the roadway, the trap overturned, and Lieut. Groves was thrown out. It was stated by an eyewitness that he turned a complete somersault and alighted on his feeet. This must account for the fortu- nate fact that he sustained no injury beyond a slight shock. The trap was slightly damaged, but assistance was ready at hand, and in a little while he was able to proceed on his journey. LOCAL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Board was held on Tues- day night, when there were present—Mr. J. Gregson (chairman), Messrs. Brazendale, Perrin, Whitelegg, Swallow, Appleton, Harrison, Holt, Mclntyre, Ridgway (law clerk), Mullard (clerk), and Mr. Mort (surveyor and inspector).</p>
        <p>THB COST OF INFECTIOUS DISEASES PATIENTS. The Clerk informed the Board that the Altrincham Union were complaining as to the price charged by the Board for infectious diseases cases from Lymm. They thought that 28s. a week per head was too much to pay, and stated that for patients from Knutsford they paid £1 is. to Monsall and 10s. 6d. to Altrincham Local Board.—Mr. Brazendale was of opision that if 10s. 6d. was a fair price for Altrincham it shculd be the same for Lymm.—The Law Clerk : It seems a small sum.—lt was eventually decided that the Clerk should write to the Altrincham Union suggesting 17s. 6d. a week instead of 28s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>HOSPITAL FOR SMALLPDX CASES.</head>
        <p>A HOSPITAL FOR SMALLPDX CASES.</p>
        <p>_ A letter was read from the Local Government Board oiving sanction to the Board to use the huts purchased From the Ship Canal Co., for the purpose of a caretakers house and stares, and stating that the land would have to be bought before they could build a permanent hos- pital. It was resolved that the consideration of this question be left to a special meetine of the Board.</p>
        <p>BEAZENDALE AND THE HEATLEY SEYTERAPE SCHEME. The CLERK submitted the agreement between the Board and the engineer for the construction of the pro- posed sewerage works at Heatley, which are estimated to cost £3,300. Mr. Brazendale said he would not be a party to the expenditure of such a large sum of money, as he believed it could be done for less, there being sufficient fall there, in his opinion, to admit of the gra- vitation scheme being adopted. The agreement was adopted and the seal of the Board _ffixed to it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>TUE INTERNATIONAL RUGBY FOOTBALL MATCH. _ KEEPING THE GROUND SOFT.</head>
        <p>TUE INTERNATIONAL RUGBY FOOTBALL MATCH. _ KEEPING THE GROUND SOFT.</p>
        <p>In consequence of the continued frost great tracer- tainty exists as to whether the condition of the turf at the Cardiff Arms Park ground will not neccessitate a postponement of the first International match of the season which is arranged for to-day. The ground has been covered with straw for some time, but the protection has not been sufficient to withstand the hard and prolonged frost. On Thursday evening the turf was inspected by the committee of the Welsh Union, and found it to be too hard for football, and ordinarily a postponement would have had to take place. During the day, however, experiments had been made with a view to softening the ground by the use of what are known in the country as " devils," straw stacks so put together that when ignited they they throw heat downwards, and the results were considered so satisfactory that on Thursday eveniog the Match Committee came to the conclusion that the ground coUld be rendered fit for play. It is Intended to place 300 of these " devils" over the ground and to keep them burning continuously until the hour for the kick- off.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>FIRST WA RRINCTON STARR-BO WKE L'T BUILDIING SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>FIRST WA RRINCTON STARR-BO WKE L'T BUILDIING SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the First Warrington 826th Starr-Bowkett Building Society was held in the Leigh-street Schools, on Thursday evening. Mr.G.J.Brown presided.—TheChairiMiti,in opening the proceedings, said during the year time appro- priations had been held, and the amount of £2,800 had been appropriated, of whicO £1,310 had been taken up. He urged upon the members the hecessity of tak- ing up their shares, or if they could nol take them up, , disposing cf them. The Starr-Bowkett Society was not an investing society, and their success depended very largely upon the members making ose of the facilities the society offered by either using or disposing of their shares. The Chairman mentioned that £752 had been paid out to withdrawing members (limn the year, and in view of the membership having d!creased the ex- penditure had been decreased to the extent of aboist £l5 per annum. He mentioned alai the completion fee for members had been reduced froo 10s. to ss. REPOIZT AND FINANCIAL STATISIdENT.,—,ARG) /kraut/NT OF WITHDRAWALS. The Secretary (Mr. J. Titchett) red the sixth annual report and financial statement. Thereport showed that the receipts for the year had been •s follows :—Mem- bers' subscriptions, £1,399 14s. 9d. Repayment of ap- propriations, £833 9s. 11d., which with minor sums, made a total of £2,292 4s. 9d The report also stated that Mr. H. Bond ha been appointed to fill the vacancy caused in tie Directorate by the death of Mr. John Walker, nd that the retiring Directors Messrs. J. Owen, R.Suttoi T. Barlow, and H. Bond, and the auditors Messrs. Peisou and Edwards, who also retired, were eligible ir re-election. The balance sheet showed on the incne and expenditure account a sum of £215 us. 9d. o the credit of the society at the end of the year.--fr. James Sutton re- ferred to the amount of withdraals, as exceedingly heavy, and the Chairman explain' that that was be- cause no member was entitled t, withdraw until five years had expired, and this bel; the sixth year the whole £752 had been withdrawn luring the last twelve rnonths.—Mr. James Sutton aske if the members gave any reason for withdrawing. I also referred disap- provingly to the reduction of ti Directors' salaries.— The Chairman said the Society Id simply acted on the Same principle as private busing men in cutting down the expenses of the Society wit income was not com- mensurate with expenditure. The present reduction did not meet the requirements wki next year's balance sheet came to be published, ere would be further reductions.—Mr. Titchett saith had asked many mem- bers the reason of their withdiving, and they had all assured him that it was not 87 want of confidence in the society.—Mr. J. Sutton: I don't think so.—Mr. Titchett : But because the wanted the money for other purposes.—Mr. Hickm, said there had scarcely been any notices of witirawal lately, so that this trouble was not likely tcecur. He explained that the Secretary had consentede a 'reduction of £lO per per annum on his salary andie Directors to a reduction of £5 per annum each in Ors. With strict economy the Society would in two ~ohree years begin to make large profits. One tv by which this would be done was ls. The Board bad never yet exercised their power t purchase members' appro- priations, but they intenchto do so. The society now stood with a profit of £9so that it was in a sound financial position.—Mr. ilutton expressed himself as perfectly satisfied with ttexplanations, and moved the adoption of the report it financial statesnent.—Mr. Holford seconded.—The ition was carried.</p>
        <p>THE DEATH OMR. JOHN WALKER. The next business beinhe election of four directors, the Chairman said it woi be very opportune to make a remark or two with rtrence to their late director, Mr. John Walker. Thmard certainly felt that they had lost a very valualotnember in the death of Mr. Walker. (Hear, hear.)He was a most ardent sup- porter of every instituti that had for its object the 1 propagation of thrift, g the benefit of his fellow-men, and in particular he s an ardent supporter of the Starr-Bowkett Buildiniociety. So long as his failing health would allow hihe was always at their meet- ings, and they alwaysmd him very wise in his judg- ments, and very disinested in everything that he said or did in connection vi their institution, and consider- ing that he was in sc failing health and took part in so many public matti he got through a tremendous amount of work; in t he (the Chairman) thought it might be truly said aim, as was said very recently of another public man Ao died, that he lived much in a Very short time. (Lr, hear.) He was quite sure that he expressed the fetgs of his fellow Directors and of the members gently, when he said that they deeply regretted tilt had been the will of Provid- ence to call lidtWalker away, and that they felt they had stained a very severe loss ; and the very next tlg they could do was to elect in his place a man who ild as far as possible follow in Mr. Walker's footstepeThe Board had in the meantime elected Mr. Bond,fill his position, he being about the most suitable matey could find, and he had been in the habit pccasioty of attending on Saturday evenings in Mr. Walkers lence, for of course he could not always be present' those nights.—The four retiring directors alreadymed, were re-appointed. THE AUDITORS. Mr. Critchley posed that the salaries of the audi- tors be reduced In £4 4s. to £3 3s. each per annum,. Mr. John Griffitl seconded.—Mr. James Sutton pro- posed an amendat that there be no reduction.—Mr. Shakeshaft seco d.—The original motion was carried. —Messrs. Pearl and Edwards were re-elected auditors. -</p>
        <p>ROPRIATION DRAWING.</p>
        <p>A subsequenbeting under the same presidency, was held for the puke of making the 45th appropriation, Messrs. John ffiths and Beddard being appointed scrutineers.—lresult was that Mr. S. J. Shakeshaft, of Grappenhaseame entitled by the drawing of No. 460 to an appriation of £2OO.</p>
        <p>FURTHER OtaFAIC OF SMALLPDX.—Two more cases of smallpox In' occurred at Haydock this week, the victims being lan and his wife living in Jackson's Cottages. Drayward ordered their removal to the temporary licgal on Newton Common, whither they have been cooed. This makes six cases which have occurred at pock. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-07_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <p>11</p>
        <p>DISTRESS AT NV ARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>RELIEF FOR THE POOR.-SOUP KITCHEN AND COAL DISTRIBUTION.</p>
        <p>The Mayor is taking steps to provide relief for the people who are suffering from the inclemency of the weather. The appeal which appears in our advertising columns asks for subscriptions to as- sist the Mayoress and a committee in organizing a soup kitchen for children, which it is intended to open at the Drill Hall, oil Monday next, at 12: o'clock. Mr. George Riley, school attendance officer, will distribute tickets, on application, at his office in the Park.</p>
        <p>The Mayor has also been able to provide Corpor- ation employment for about 50 men who have been thrown outof work by the severe weather,preference having been given to cases where men have families depending upon them. It has been ascertained that there are many cases in the town where people are absolutely without coal and food, and it is therefot°. considered advisable to distribute tickets for cosi relief to poor people. During the past fewweeks a great number of out door workers have had to cease work and this bad. intensified the distress. The clergy and ministera of the various denominations are all alert with regard tc this serious consequence of the severs frost, aad are doing their best to ameliorate the distress.</p>
      </div>
