        <p>THE ALTHORP CASE. THE MURDERER'S INTERVIEW WITH HIS WIFE. Mrs Macrae, wife of the prisoner, visited him in Northampton Gaol on Saturday afternoon. She found him tolerably cheerful, but evidently ill. She says he is considerably worse in health than he was during the trial. The interview, which took place in the presence of two warders, was very affecting. Macrae told his wife she knew he was innocent, and he said, apparently with much sincerity, that when it was too late his innocence would be proved. It is understood that the expressions Macrae has let drop since his conviction have led to communications being sent to the Northamp- tonshire police by the prison authorities, and the police are still pursuing investigations in regard to the murder. It is said that inquiries are yet being made as to the place of concealment of Annie Pritchard's body prior to its removal to Althorp. -- ANOTHER LETTER FROM MACRAE. The following letter was received on Saturday by Edward Macrae, brother of the condemned man : Her Majesty's Prison, Northampton, Dec. 30th, 1892. Dear Brother,—l was indeed most pleased to receive your kind and welcome letter this morning. Glad you also are much better. lam sure that no one who knows me would believe for a moment I could be guilty of such a crime,for which lam condemned,basely condemned,be- cause lam innocent. I can seeyou on Monday. I know you will do all you can for me. It has been a cruel injustice all through. I only wish tor justice, mostly for the sake of those I love, and to whom I ought to have been restored long ago. I send my love to all—with best wishes for a brighter new sear than we think it will be. I cannot leut;think God In His goodness and mercy will help me yet, if it be his will. I shall die guiltless at least of this crime, and then be sure I shall meet you in heaven.— Your faithful and loving brother, Andrew. When you come, Ted, do let me beg of you not to upset yourself or me. NEWS OF GUY ANDERSON. Mr. Edward Macrae writes that he has discovered that Guy Anderson left Birmingham on August this year to go abroad. DATE OF EXECUTION. Macrae has been informed that he will be hanged on January 10th. He received the news in sullen silence. AN ALLEGED ACCOMPLICE. ' The Northamptonshire police, telegraphs our correse pondent, are now in possession of information which convinces those who had the conduct of the Althorp murder case that Andrew Macrae had an accomplice in the disposal of the victim's body. They are confident that this second person, and not the prisoner, secreted the body in the ditch near Althorn railway station, and they state that they are able to put their finger on him at any moment. They are weighing the evidence in their possession as to the wisdom of making the arrest. A STRONG PROTEST OF INNOCENCE. Mrs. Macrae yesterday received the following letter from her husband "Dearest wife,—Knowing as I do how truly innocent I am of the crime for which I have been condemned, I cannot but feel how awful it would be if such a mis- carriage of justice should take place as my having to suffer the extreme penalty of the law for an offence which I have never committ2d. The greatest "reason I should hot do so is because I am sure sooner or later the mystery will be cleared up by the supposed murdered person returning or friends hearing from her. For my own part, for aught I know, she must be alive and well. I have no doubt of this as far as I am concerned. The jury went into the box prejudiced against me, with their minds made up as to the verdict. They took not the slightest notice of the judge, who was decidedly in my favour, and would, if left to him, have acquitted. The judge pointed out many doubts, as a most just and merciful judge, and I hope he will do so again. The evidence of many of the witnesses was very untrue. The evidence of the witness Elliott as to the clothes worn presumably by Annie Pritchard en the night when last seen is entirely false. Instead of having a cloak and serge skirt on, she had on a jacket, black skirt, and serge bodice. She never went to or ever saw the warehouse. She left that same evening for London to meet Anderson from Castle Station, Nothampton, at 83. She took with her all she thought necessary. I did not see her again, but on the day following I received a few lines from her telling me not to send the things left, but to sell them to help to pay the several little things which she had left. Thus I did so without fear and openly. She was in communication with this man, anchon the 19th July told me she had received a leiter from London, part of which she read. He said that he should come for her on Thursday. Thus her determination to go at once, so that he should not come over and find out. She begged my protection, believing she could join him on his return, and I was weak enough after a time to give way, more out of kindness than anything else. Thus far only have I sinned. I hope you have forgiven me this long ago. Both you and I knew of Guy Anderson. He was in Birmingham at the end of November last year. Pritchard knew all about him, and of their sister going to his home, This they kept back. She had no claim upon me whatever, so that I had no motive for such a crime, which I could not possibly commit. What I did was out of kindness to an old and esteemed friend, and for this I have to suffer if I die. I shall die innocent, and that being so I beg for justice. Fondest love. • ANDEBR MACRAII." THE RECENT OUTRAGE' IN DUBLIN. MR. CONYBEARE'S INFORMATION. Mr. Conybeare, M.P., addressing the Belfast Nationa- lists on Wednesday night, said he had bees informed, when in Dublin, that the late outrage there was caused by a dismissed Castle official, and that it was the same individual who carried out the previous explosion at the Castle, and was the author of the outrage at the " Na- ,l tional Press "office. '- Information has reached Wolverhampton that a man named Went has died in America leaving about 40•000dols. to be distributed amongst his relatives. A sister of the deceased, a Mrs. Holland, the wifetof Mr. Charles Holland, dyer, Great Brickkiln-st., Wolverhamp- ton, will be a sharer of the good fortune.</p>
